      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <p>2 XLWAYS USE McDOUGALL'S Patetit SELF—RAISING FLOUR IT IS THE BEST. The Examiner, Saturday, September 10, 1904.</p>
        <p>enough to present his report to a committee if it was desired, and he was quite sure that if in this case any desire whatsoever to see it had been expressed Dr. Gornall would have been only too glad to have shown it before it was printed. The committee might then have made their comments and criticisms, and the report might have been altered before it went forth to the public. No doubt there was a great deal of justifi- cation for the remarks that had been made, but such a position ought to be avoided in the future. Councillor Tisstos said it was impossible for the Icommittee to know that there was cause for Dr. Gornall or anyone else to make complaint, and they had not the least expectation that he would make a report adverse to the work the committee was trying to do. He had had some conversation with the doctor. He could not say that the doctor exactly saw the error of his ways, and while he was not prepared to sacrifice his position as the one responsible for the report, Dr. Gornallsaid he would take full responsibility for the statements he had made in it. He (the medical officer), however, saw the advantage that would arise from co- operation in matters in which he was interested, and he (the speaker) hoped in future that the Medical Officer of Health would work both with the Health Committee and with the Paving and Sewerage Committee, for the advantage of the borough. • The minutes were then adopted. MESSRS. CROSFIELD AND THEIR WORK-BOYS. At a meeting of the Town Hall, Parks and-Baths Committee held on August 4th, a letter was read from Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd., stating that they proposed to send a certain number of boys in their employ to the baths twice a week during working hours in charge of one of their own men,and asking that books of tickets of 100-300 to the second class bath be issued at a reduced price. It was resolved that a reduction of 25 per cent. be made for youths using the second class bath under the above conditions. The MAYOR moved the report of the .committee without comment. Councillor WILKINSON seconded, and the minutes wero confirmed.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND THE SHIP CANAL BILL. At a meeting of the Rivers Committee on the 29th August, the Town Clerk reported that the Manchester Ship Canal Act, 194 had received the Royal assent. The MAYOR, in moving the adoption of the minutes, stated that they would see %hat the Ship Canal Bill had nowfreceived Royal sanction, and he thought that War- rington wasin a very fair position as far as they were concerned in it. Councillor JOLIN ROBERTS seconded, and the minutes were adopted.</p>
        <p>THE DISMISSAL OF THE ASSISTANT STOKER AT WINWICK.</p>
        <p>Councillor STOTT asked the Chairman of the Water Committee :—(1) On what date was Mr. John Roe dis- missed from the position of assistant stoker at the Winwick Pumping Station? (2) What is the reason for his dismissal? (3) Why was he refused any information as to the reason for his dismissal by the Water Engineer? (4) in refusing to give any information, was the Engineer acting under instructions from the Water Committee? (5) What are the wages and hours of labour of the man who is now engaged on the work previously done by Mr. Roe? (6) Will the Water Com- mittee consider the reinstatement of Mr. Roe? Alderman FRANCOM 11 replying to the above questions stated :—(1) That Mr. Roe left on August sth and notice was given on July 28th. He was warned shortly after the resolution was passed that he had better look out for another job, but he was kept on for four weeks longer after the resolution was passed in the hope that he would get other work. (2) The committee felt that they could do without the position of assistant stoker by bringing a man back who had been in the position previousb ,as they expected the water to be brought from Houghton Green, and so do without his services. (3.) When he (the speaker) knew that a request for information had been made, he asked Mr. Dees to give what information was wanted. (4.) He did not remember any instructions being given to Mr. Dem. The resolution simply meant that the man would finish his work shortly. (5.) The hours were the same and the wages were the same as were paid to Mr.'Roe,except that the present man was a casual labourer and allowed" walking time" similar to other casual labourers. (6.) It was utterly impossible for the committee to reconsider the matter. The manager would have no power whatever over a man so reinstated. THE EXTENDED TRAMWAY SERVICE&lt;TO</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH. THE POLICE AND THE TRAM CARS.</p>
        <p>The Electricity and Tramways Committee recommen- ded the acceptance of the following tenders :—The St' Helens Cable Co.. Ltd., for the supply of cables, &amp;c., required in connection with Light Railway No. 1, at £876 13s. 2d.; Sir W. G. Armstrong, Whitworth and Company, Limited, for locking gear, &amp;c., at £220, and for labour at the rates quoted by them, and Messrs. Gilbert Thompson and Company, of Birmingham, for delivery and erection complete of lattice girders at £99 10s., and cast iron curbs at £72 10s. Alderman SMETHURST, in moving the report, pointed out that an error had occurred in the contract for lock- ing gear at the Swing Bridge, Stockton Heath. The £220 was for such gear at one end only, but the Ship Canal Company required that it should be at both ends. The amount of the contract would consequently be increased from £220 to £440. Councillor WILKINSON seconded. Councillor LORD moved an amendment that a recom- mendation that the police ride free on the tramcars be not confirmed. Councillor BARTON deconded. Alderman SMETHURST urged that it was most impor- tant that the police and firemen should have free use of the cars when on duty. Some two months ago a report had been presented by a member of the Council on the use of the cars by Corporation officials. The matter had been fully investigated, ana the committee had gone so far as to write to the heads of the various departments giving a wa ruing that if Corporation servants were found using checks except on Corporation service the checks would be withdrawn, and they would have to pay fares like anyone else. Councillor PENIBERTON said the object of Councillor Lord's amendment was not to prevent the police from riding on the cars. It was simply to defer the matter for further consideration. The MAYOR urged that the police should be welcome to ride whenever necessary when on duty. Councillor ROGERS said the recommendation of the committee applied to the police and to no other depart- ment of the Corporation. Councillor TINNION called atention to the proposed locking gear at each end of the Stockton Heath Swing Bridge. He thoupht locking gear at one end would be sufficient. The MAYOR said it was a matter for the engineers and the Council was in their hands with respect to the pro- posed arrangement. The amendment was lost. THE TRADING INQUIRY CLOSED.</p>
        <p>The minutes relating to the further progress of the Trading Inquiry were submitted by the General Pur- poses Committee. The MAYOR, in moving their adoption stated that the inquiry was now closed, and a condensed statement of the evidence and the findings would be submitted to them for their inspection. The DEPUTY-MAYOR seconded, and the report was adopted. SAFEGUARDING TRADE UNIONISTS. NEW SERIES OF FAIR WAGE CLAUSES. INTERESTING DEBATE.</p>
        <p>The following mo ions stood in the name of Councillor STorr :—(I) Contractors tendering for or executing wlrk under this Council must be paying to the vhole of their workpeople the trades union rate of wages for the district, and must also be observing the hours of labour as well as the aforesaid rates of wages recognized by the trades unions in the various trades where the work is being executed. (2) Should there be no trades union rate of wages or hours of labour in the district "there the work is being executed the trades union rate and conditions of labour in the nearest dis- trict to where the work if being done shall be observed by the bontractor. (3) The contractor shall not assign or under-let the contract or arty part of it, or sub-con- tract, except with the consent of the Council, and upon such conditions as they may think fit; but if the tenderer at the time of tendering states his desire to sub-let any portions of the work not usually done by him, the Council will consent, provided that the sub- contractor is a person approved by it. The principal contractor shall be respo nsi ble,ho wever,f or all work done by such sub-contractor and for its being carried out under the same conditions as if executed by himself. (4) Contractors executing work under this Council must not prohibit their workpeople from joining trade societies or from continuing members of such societies. (5) Should the• Council have, in its opinion, reasonable grounds for believing that the above conditions are not being complied with,the contractor shall be required to produce proof to the satisfaction of the Council of his compliance with the said conditions. (G) Any contrac- tor having been proved by the Council to have broken the conditions of a contract, the Council shall not con- sider any tender submitted by him for the carrying,out of any work fora period of five years from the date of the contract having been broken. (7) Clauses embody- ing various points herein named shall be inserted in all contracts for work, and contractors shall be required to signify their assent to them in writing. Failure to comply with any of the conditions set forth in such clauses shall leave it within the power of the Council to cancel the contract. Councillor Srorr said his duty was made much easier by the fact that some years ago the Corporation adopted the principle of the fair wages clause in contracts. They all knew—trade unionists in particular—what difficul- ties had been experienced in the carrying out of the clause. It had not been satisfactory to the Council, the contractors, or the workingmen of the town. His object in moving the resolution on behalf of the Trades and Labour Council was to make the clause more definite He asked permission to move each clause of the resolu- tion separately. The DEPUTYSfAYOR thought it would be a waste of</p>
        <p>time. Councillor LORD: Suppose theyare taken separately and one is thrown out. How will that affect the motion? The TOWN CLERK said the clauses carried would become standing orders. If they chose the Council could take them seriatim.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR TINN lON AND THE MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Trade Union Contract Clauses.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Town Council was held on Tuesday evening when there were present: The Mayor (Alderman Bolton),the Deputy-Mayor (Alder- man Fairclough), Aldermen Sutton, Hutchinson, Smet- hurst, Evans and Francomb,Councillors Rogers, Ellison, Duncan, Craik, Stott, Tinnion, John Roberts' W. J. Forshaw, Thornton, Wilkinson, P. Peacock, Davies, Ashcroft. Barton, Burton, Dodds, Pemberton, W. J. Wilson, John Maginn,Lord, Jacob Sankey, Brough, with Messrs. J. L. Whittle (town clerk), John Fairhurst (borough treasurer), Stanley Wilson (deputy town clerk), J. Moore Murray (director of education), J. Deas (water engineer), J. Haddock (gas engineer), Dr. J. Guest Gornall (medical officer of health),andT.Longdin (borough surveyor.) PROPOSED STREET FROM KNITTSFORD-ROAD. A meeting of the Street Improvement and Lighting Committee was held on August 4th, when Mr. Wallhead again attended with regard to the plan submitted by 'Messrs. Mainwaring and Pemberton, showing among others a proposed street 40ft. wide from Knutsford-road to the roadway under the new line of railway from Warrington to Latchford, and which it was stated would be carried through to Wilderspool-causeway. It was resolved that the plan of the proposed new street on the north side of the said railway arch be approved, and that the committee will offer no objection to the con- Urination of such new street to the Wilderspool-cause- way, provided such street complies with the bylaws; and that the sum of £1,300 be paid to Mr. Thomas Hill for the purchase of his premises in Silver street and Silver-square. The minutes were passed, on the motion of Alderman SUTTON, seconded by Councillor BARTON.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR TINNION AND THE REPORT OF THE MEDICAL OFFICER. THE PAVING OF PRIVATE STREETS AND PASSAGES. DISEASE IN THE BOROUGH. "COMNIITTEE NOT RESPONSIBLE."</p>
        <p>Councillor Tmros, in moving, the confirmation of the minutes, of the Paving and Sewerage Committee alluded to the recent annual report of the Medical Officer of Health. What he was about to say was not a personal attack on Dr. Gornall except in so far as it defended the action of the committee. Now for the first objection to Dr. Gornall's report, and that was its exaggeration of language and its mis-statement of facts. They knew that the doctor was an enthusiast. He did not quarrel with him for. that, but still he (Coun- cillor Tinnion) was sorry, and the doctor must be corrected when his enthusiasm seemed to get the better of his judgment as it had done on this occasion. He believed that if the doctor had to re-write his report he would not word it quita so strongly or fall into so many errors. What the committee most strongly ob- jected to wasthat the report went to the Local Govern- , ment Board. It was the report of an official of the Corporation, who could act almost independently of any of the committees. It was the doctor's report, and no one else's. He did not send it to the committee. If he had done so, and the committee had accepted it, well and good. He rather fancied that it was an error the doctor had fallen into on this occasion. The doctor had spoken "of the daily trudging to school through slush and pools of water." Ho did not even qualify that state- ment, and say that what he had stated occurred once in a year or twice. He led the Local Government Board to think that the whole of the school children daily walked through pools of slush and water, which was an exaggerated account of the condition of affairs, the worst condition at the worst possible times. What the doctor had stated was not a fair representation of the streets in their normal condition. Then he went on, evidently thinking that he would like to put the respon- sibility upon somebody, but did not know exactly who, "Little or no improvement can be observed in the rate of the paving of streets and still less of passages," and further added that there was apparently no movement towards putting things right where the situation and character required it, namely, in the Doorer parts of the town. That, he thought was not a 'air statement of the facts,and altogether the report was very misleading. With reference to THE DOCTOIVS FACTS he mentioned altogether 343 streets and passages.l He (Councillor Tinnion) had gone through and visited many of the passages, and the committee would,he hoped, pay a personal inspection to the whole of them and see exactly how things were. However, they had a general idea, but it did not coincide with the view of the Medical Officer of Health. They found that 16 of the passages referred to had been done eight years ago; 27 of them were mentioned twice over; 31 were built only on one side; and eight could not be found at all. That accounted for 82 of the 343, which showed that the doctor was about 25 per cent. wrong in the facts. Also he contended that the doctor was further wrong. He had personally visited some of the passages which had been in their present position for a considerable number of years, because they were at the time paved with boulder stones, which fulfilled the conditions of theauthorities at that period. There- fore he did not see how they !could call upon the owners to make them, seeing that they had once been made to thesatisfaction of the authorities. He would now like to show the Council WHIT THE COMMITTEE HAD BEEN DOING. For the ten years 1875 to 1884, £16,500 was spent in the ming of streets and passagps ; 1885 to 1894, £27,090; 1895 to 1904, something like £62,000; and they hoped to get another batch ready shortly requiring £6.000 or £B,OOO, which would make about £70,000 spent on private street works during the past ten years. Even twang the present year, they had already spent £4,453, and had got the sanction of the Local 'Govern- ment Board for another £5,847. That made 210,300,and with £6.000 they hoped to get the plans and specifica- tions ready for and the inquiry held would make some- thing like £16,000 or £lB,OOO for the year. In 1903 they did fall somewhat behind, but it was not the fault of the committee. At any rate in that year they only spent about £9OB on private streets. For one thing materials were exceedingly high, setts being up in price from 25 to 50 per cent higher. Therefore the committee did not think it would be desirable to do much with things in that condition. At the same time he would like to say again that he thought the Council made an unfortu- nate blunder in extending the time for paving,from three to seven years. Speaking of the parts of the borough recently incorporated, he pointed out that these were not on the ordnance plan, and the result was that they had to make a survey and,plan and a sectional plan. He contended that in the face of the greater difficulties that had existed since 1903 in dealing with these matters the committee had done their duty in a way which instead of having the condemnation of a good many people, should have their approbation. There yet remained to be paved some of a mile of 40ft. and 361 t. streets, of a mile of 30ft. streets ; 11 miles of 20ft. passages; 21 miles of 15ft. passages ; mile of 12ft. passages; 1 mile of 10ft. passages ; Mile of Gft, pas- sages, and 4 mile of 41t. passages. He wished also to deal with the question as to who was really responsible for the disease that existed in the borough. The Paving and Sewerage Committee were NOT GOING TO TAKE THE RESPONSIBILITY for that. They were in no way responsible for any of the epidemics, neither in originating or in causing the continuation of any of the epidemics of measles, fevers, or anything else. He was informed that the Medical Officer of Health had made a report to the Health Committee. He had not seen it, but it was deal- ing with the number of cases of infectious disease that had occurred in the paved and the unpaved streets of the town. He had asked for the report, but unfortun- ately, Mr. Floodrthe inspector' who was away on his holidays, had it locked up in his desk. However he was given to understand that as a matter of fact where the cases were investigated, the unpaved streets—it was very funny and he did not see why it should be so— showed up more favourably than those which had been paved both back and front. He mentioned this to show that it was not altogether the fault of the unpaved streets. He did not admit that it was the fault of any of the unpaved streets, but it seemed to him strange that they should be charged with A want of care for the health of the inhabitants and that when the matter came to be investigated it should be found that the unpaved streets and passages came out more favourably than those which were properly paged. They did not object to any criticism that was fair and honest, and they were always pleased to have any faults pointed out, not only in the Press, but by others as well, but he wished at that juncture to say that the progress of the work now going on was not caused by the very great amount of criticism that had been put upon them, but simply because the committee felt that they had a great responsibility in providing, as far as they could, the best possible conditions which, not only the poor but every class of ratepayers, could have round their dwellings. It was simply the desire and determination of the committee to provide the best conditions and the progress was not on account of the adverse, unfriendly, and in a goad many cases unfair criticism to which the committee had been subjected. Councillor Joirtc ROBERTS seconded. He was very sorry that Dr. Gornall made the report without well considaring what he was going to put before that Council. Alderman SUTTON said as the Medical Officer had not the opportunity of replying to the rather severe attack made upon him by Councillor Tinnion he woull like to say that although Councillor Tinnion might have had just cause to make complaint, still he thought that it was a mistake altogether that the committee did not take the opportunity of reading Dr. Gornall's report before it was printed. Councillor TINNION : \‘'e never had the opportunity. Alderman SurTos said he thought that the doctor as a rule presented his report to the committee before it was printed. Councillor TINNION : Not this time. Alderman Srrros said he had no doubt the committee could have had it, for Dr. Gornall was always courteous</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <p>, - - - -</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT</p>
        <p>COUNCIL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>Pollution of Sankey Brook.</head>
        <p>The Pollution of Sankey Brook.</p>
        <p>SUGGESTED SCHEME OF SEWAGE DISPOSAL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the above Council was held at Bewsey Chambers on Wednesday,under the presidency of Mr. T. Halton. There) were also present:-Dr. Murray, Messrs. Brooks, Mather, Houghton, Longton, Backhouse, Thompson, Ball, J. C. Sutton (clerk), S. Sutton (assistant clerk), Dr. Sephton (medical officer of health), F. Kerfoot (inspector of nuisances), B. S. Newcombe (surveyor), and H. H. Burnage (assistant surveyor).</p>
        <p>THE ESTIMATES. The CLERK submitted estimates of general and special expenses for the half year ending March, 1903. It had been customary to call for sixpence in the $ for the corresponding half year to meet the general outlay and 6d. was now advised. The present total of assessable value was £87,086, compared with £83,498 in 1903,giving an increase from £2,088 to £2.178 of income. The pre- cepts submitted for the Council's approval totalled as follows: Burtonwood £417, Cuerdley £145, Houghton £77, Penketh £219, Poulton £l6l, Rixton £lB2, Great Sankey £230, Southworth £B7. Winwick £536, Woolston £94, Total £2,178. They were made payable in two portions, on 2nd November 1904, and 4th January 1905. In regard to special expenses, these were charges upon particular townships in respect of exceptional outlay including sewerage, sewage works, water supply, lighting,cemetery charges, loans and interest, &amp;c. At the close of the last half-year the favourable balances were applied to a reduction of the calls. Exhaustion of balances, and increases of outlay under different heads made necessary increased calls,and precepts were submitted forapproval as follows: Burtonwood £l2O, Penketh £llO, Poulton £6O, Rixton £55, Great Sankey £45. Woolston £5. Mr. Sutton added that he felt sure that the effect of putting 6d. on now would be to take off a penny if not 2d. when they received the county rate call for the next half year. The SURVEYOR pointed out that as they were still undertaking improvements for the County a certain amount would be outstanding.</p>
        <p>NEW BYE-LAWS.</p>
        <p>The Council passeWa-re.so-lution confirming the new bye-laws regulating offensive trades and dealing with ,new buildings, prior to submitting them to the Local Government Board.</p>
        <p>EURTONWOOD WATER SUPPLY. The CLERK said that as he hoped and expected this question had ended in a compromise which would Involve a new agreement. The Town Clerk wrote : " As a result of negotiations with the Parish Council of Burtonwood, the Corporation are prepared to lay addi- tional mains in the above parish as may be required subject to a guarantee of 6 per cent. on the capital out- lay, and to a limit of £l,OOO as the aggregate amount to be spent under the agreement." Under the old agree- ment the guarantee was 5 percent., and the recent letter from Mr. Deas required 8 per cent. The new conditions appeared to be reasonable, but Burtonwood would have to be careful, because it was evident the Corporation would not go beyond £l,OOO at 6 per cent. When they had spent above £l,OOO in the parish they would require the full 8 per cent. to be paid. Mr. THOMPSON moved that the Corporation's offer be accepted, and the resolution was agreed to. BURTONWOOD RIGHT OF WAY QUESTION. The CLERK said he had received a letter from the Clerk to the Burtonwood Parish Council, from which it appeared that the Council were not at all pleased that the District Council had not taken up the question of right of way with Messrs. Fairclough on legal grounds. He (Mr. Sutton) replied that it was a common act on the part of people using rights of way to remove the ob- struction when the roadswere stopped.lf in this case the obstruction was restored the 'Council would probably take it in band. If as they said,the inhabitants had had the right of way for sixty years they would be justified in removing the obstacle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>SANKEY LIGHTING.</head>
        <p>SANKEY LIGHTING.</p>
        <p>The CLERK read a letter from the -Clerk to the Great Sankey Parish Council asking for the erection of a lamp in Cemetery-road on the Sankey side (the Penketh Council having asked for one to be erected on their side) and also for the lamps in Sankey to be fitted with No. 3 Kerns incandescent burners. The matter was left, in the hands of Dr. Murray and Mr. Thompson who were empowered to make the -necessary arrangements. THE POLLUTION OF SANKF.Y BROOK. SUGGESTED SCHEME OF SEWAGE TREATMENT. THE GREAT SANKEY REPORT. The CLERK,reported the receipt of a copy of the report of the deputation from Sankey Parish Council (Messrs. Furness and Jefferson) ye tbe pollution of Sankey Brook. He remarked that the report was a very able one. What the Council did at their last meeting was to try to have clean hands themselves. They instructed the Surveyor to endeavour to find means -of treating the sewage as far as they were responsible for it, instead of allowing it to flow into the brook. Mr. Newcombe would present a report on the question. The report of the Sankey deputation concluded: "Mr. J. Lyon Whittle, the town dlerk, has assured me that if the offenders above Warrington would discontinue the offence he would at once give an undertaking on behalf of the Corporation -to at once take measures for the treatment of the offence of Which the Corporationare guilty." That was simply an absurdity, simply rot as he heard Mr. Brooks remark —as if the onus was to be thrown upon that Council not only to have clean hands themselves but to call upon the other districts to do the same. They had no power over the other districts. All they could do was to perform their own duty in the matter. It was un- fortunate that the deputation spoke of the very success- ful effort made by the Winwick Asylum authorities to get rid of theirzeWage, and stated that now not the least harm was dine to the brook by any discharge at that point. If Great Sankey bad left alone this ques- tion, that remark would have been made applicable in a vary short time to the whole brook. At a later stage of the meeting the surveyor, in accordance with instructions, presented a report -on the suggested treatment of sewage in the district. He</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>CHARMING WEST COUNTRY. The GREAT WESTERN RAILWAY COMPANY</head>
        <p>THE CHARMING WEST COUNTRY. The GREAT WESTERN RAILWAY COMPANY</p>
        <p>have made important additions to the facilities for travel to:</p>
        <p>THE BOLD &amp; ATTRACTIVE COASTS OF DEVON</p>
        <p>THE ROMANTIC EXMOOR</p>
        <p>THE GRANDEUR ot"rriy. ENGLISH</p>
        <p>AND V.LIGCED</p>
        <p>BRISTOL CHANNELS.</p>
        <p>DARTMOOR,</p>
        <p>CORNWALL,</p>
        <p>THE CHARMING HILLS AND VALLEYS</p>
        <p>SOMERSET AND DORSET.</p>
        <p>Full particulars can WI obtained free ;on application to The Superintendent of the Line, Great] Western Railway, Paddington Station, London JAMES C. INGLIS, General Manager.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <p>stated that the main points along the Sankey Brook and within the Council's district at which pollution occurred, were as follows :—Collins Green sewer, Win- wick sewer, Longford Depot, and. Sankey Bridges sewage generally. At Collins Green he found there was ample fall, and a most suitable site to treat the sewage at the present outfall by the bacteriological system. The cost would be trifling in such a suitable place. The Winwick sewage would be much more difficult to deal with, owing to lack of fall and a suitable site. Its present outfall he did not consider at all suitable, and if treated there it would only be a temporary measure and in a few years to come it would devolve upon the authority to lay additional sewers falling away from the present °titian and thus requiring a separate treatment system. The most feasible system which commended itself seemed to be to remove the present outfall at Clam Brook, and to carry on the present Winwick sewer to a site close to the main brook and to treat the whole of the Winwick, Longford and Orford sewage there. At this point might he suggest a rather wider scheme and farther reaching than they had instructed him? The outli,.e of it would be as follows :1 —Taking the suggestion of producing the Winwick sew- age to the lowest point at Orford, they would have three different and separate sewages brought into close proximity, Winwick and Orford being common, crude sewage. One of the chief causes of trouble at Orford had always been the tannery effluent. The system which very much appealed to him would be to join the crude sewage into a main sewer, and then to precipitate this in two tanks with the effluent from the Orford Tannery. Some discussion took I,lace with regard to the pollution of the brook at Orford, and the Main Roads Committee, with Dr. Murray and Mr. Thompson, the surveyor and inspector were deputed to visit the place. Mr. Newcombe's report will be printed and circulated among the members. HEALTH OF THE DISTRICT. Dr. SEPHTON reported that during the month there had been 20 births and 42 deaths. The death rate was equal to 44.2 against 34 the average for the past three months and 32.7 the rate for the corresponding month last year. Twenty deaths occurred in Winwick Asylum and one from small-pox in Sagkey sanatorium. There were also two cases of drowning of persons not belonging to the district. Exclusive of all these, the death rate proper for the district was 20 per 1,000. The report was adopted. LONGFORD DEPOT—IMPROVEMENTS PROCEEDING. Dr. Sephton reported that along with the Inspector he met the Chairman of the Sanitary Committee and the Town Clerk at Longford Depot on August 31st and was shown the new machinery and the work in pro- gress for abating the nuisance. It was proposed to condense the vapour before it passed into the chimney, by water taken from Sankey Brook after being stowed in a reservoir, and before this water, after condensing the vapours,was returned to the brook it was to be passed through bacteria beds which would not only purify it but cool it and remove the nuisance from the end of the pipe opening into the brook. He thought if the work was properly carried out, it would reduce the nuisance so long complained about. The doctor produced a letter from Mr. F. G. Rud- dock, borough analyst, giving the result of his analysis of samples of gases taken from the depot. Mr. Rud- dock sought to show that there was little or no smell from the chimney. Dr. SEPH'FON added that when he visited the depot he .was shown a sample of water which had passed through the bacteria bed after being used for condensing pur- poses, and it was as clear as possible. COMPLAINT YROM BURTONWOOD. Mr. R. DEL WORTH, Yew Cottage, Burtonwood, wrote comnlainin. of the offensive smell arising from the broOk at the back of his house. Last week the stench was so bad that he could not sleep for it. The INSPECTOR reported that the brook was now quite clean.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <p>Workhouse numbered and known as 99, Guardian-street, for birth registration purposes. Owing to the short- ness of time, it was resolved to postpone the adoption of the separate contract system until March next. In the meantime, the Chairman of the Board,and the Chair- man and Vice-Chairman of the House Committee, the Chairman and Vice-Chairman of the Schools Committee together with the Master and Superintendent were appointed a special committee to deal with the revision and the changes which the new system would entail, and report to the House Committee. • At a meeting on September 2nd, the hospital porter's resignation was accepted and the Master was authorised to advertise the vacancy at a salary of £25 per annum, uniform, lodgings, ezc., subject to deductions for superannuation purposes. On the motion of Mr. Jolley. seconded by Mr. Flannagan, the minutes were passed unanimously. INCREASE OF THE COUNTY RATE. SUGGESTED STRIKE</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>OF OVERSEERS.</head>
        <p>OF OVERSEERS.</p>
        <p>_ The CLERK reported that since the last meeting of the Board he had received the county rate precept, and it was more than £2,000 over last year. It was the usual rise. The amount was £7,315 9s. sd. for the townships outside the borough, which formed more than half the Union. Last year the precept was for £5,255 18s. 3d., and theprevious year it was £2,965 Gs. That would give them an idea of the enormous increase which was going on. Mr. MATHER remarked that he supposed they had no power in these matters. All they had to do was to pay. The only way to deal with it would be for the overseers to combine and go on strike. They heard a lot about parliamentary finance and municipal finance, but nobody seemed to have any control over county council finance. The CLERK: lam sorry the officials of the county have to deal with it ipstAad of your own officials. Mr. MATHER : I have heard that a number of over- seers are going to resign their positions. They won't have anything to do with collecting the rates. Mr. ASHCROFT : And I don't wonder. Mr. JOLLEY : They could be re-elected and then they would have to serve or be sent to gaol. THE APPOINTMENT OF DR. SEPHTON. The CHAIRMAN gave notice that at the next meeting he would move the re-appointment of Dr. Sephton as medical officer for the ensuing three years. This was a purely formal matter. The meeting then terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>LADY "SANDWICH-MEN."</head>
        <p>LADY "SANDWICH-MEN."</p>
        <p>A . &lt;- . --,:::ty. il ...,,i;.1,. ! -, ----.1-. ,/:;&gt;.• ,</p>
        <p>.</p>
        <p>7`'</p>
        <p>D al !( ' Ci</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>PEN KETH.</head>
        <p>PEN KETH.</p>
        <p>1 THE VICAR'S SAD BEREAVEMENT.—We have to record I with deep regret the 'death of Aneuryn Parry Jones, I eldest son of Rev. J. R. Jones M.A. the respected vicar iof St. Mary's Church, Great Sankey. The deceased,who was in his 21st year, had had a long painful illness,which he had borne with remarkable fortitude. He passed away on Monday afternoon last. When in health, he was a bright and attractive young fellow, and was for some time a prominent member of the Penketh Football . Club, and also served a term in the Cyclist Company of the Warrington Volunteers. He was articled to a firm of shipping engineers until his health broke down, and caused his return home. The deepest sympathy Is ex- I pressed with the Vicar and his family by all in the 1 parish. The funeral took place on Thursday afternoon at 3 o'clock The funeral cortege left the Vicarage, the coffin being borne by young men friends of the deceased I , and preceded by the churchwardens., sidesmen and other mourners. The members of the family and friends following were Rev. J. R.Jones, M.A., Miss A. Jones, Mr. J. H. Jones, Mrs. Whitington, Rev. A. P. H. T. Wilson, Mr. J. B. Aitken and Dr. Batchan. 1 The cortege was met at the Church gates I by the Rev.J.Holden,vicar of Antrobils, who had charge • of the service,assisted by the Revs. Hilton (Farnworth) J. H. G. Hollingworth (St. Barnabas'), and F.W. Barber (St. Paul's'. A short service was held in the church, and the hymn "Jesu, still on "was sung. Miss .E. Jeans presided at the organ and played the "Dead March "as the cortege left the church. The grave at the cemetery had been lined with flowers, green ferns, &amp;c., those responsible for this being the sexton, Mr. J. Plumpton, and Mr. J. Silvester. The concluding rites were soon over, and the coffin was lowered in the grave amid much ' sorrow from the large crowd of sympathising friends. Mr. James Hayes had charge of the arrangements, and supplied the coffin and furniture, and the inscription I• read " Anenryn Parry Jones, died September sth, aged 20 years." There was a large number of beautiful wreaths and crosses. The names of the carriers . were • Messrs. Fred Burton, H. Miles, George Owen, Fred • Owen, A. Hodgkinson, R. W. Houghton, Allan Houghton, H. Ambler, A. Clarke, P. Knowles, and W. Whitfield, who represented the football club. Mr. James Woodward marghalled the carriers, and the whole of the arrangements were carried out in perfect order. ST. MATtY'S CHURCH TRIP.—The annual parochial outing arranged in connection with the choir and con- gregation attending St. Mary's Sankey Church, took place on Saturday last. The party, in charge of the wardens, left Sankey Station at 9.30 a.m., bound for Southport. The Vicar was, unfortunately, unable to bp present. The day was very fine, and the party enjoyed themselves to the utmost at tho various places of amuse- ment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <p>Councillor STorr said certain clauses might be objectionable to some members, and the consequence would be that they would vote against them all if they were put as one motion.</p>
        <p>Some discussion ensued with reference to the Town Clerk's ruling, but The TOWN CLERK still advised that it was open for the Council to adopt the whole of the resolution or clauses of it, as they chose. Alderman SurroN did not seehow they could dissever the clauses. It would be necessary to take the whole resolution, or none at all. If Councillor Stott did not see fit to do that, he recommended him to defer the matter until the next meeting. Councillor PEMBERTO'N said that while• he was in agreement with the greater part of the motion there was a penalty clause which he could not agree to for a moment. A very heavy , penalty was imposed for a trivial thing. t Councillor STOTT decided to submit all the clauses as ' one motion. There was not, he contended,a recognized rate of wages in any district except that agreed upon by employers and employed. There was no nen-society rate. The only rate was the trade union one which was agreed upon by employers and employed. There was nothing in the clauses that should be objectionable to any reasonable business man. Similar clauses were in force at Manchester and Liver- pool and other important cities, and he was imformed that they worked exceedingly satisfactorily. If the Council adopted his motion it would prevent very much guibbling and dissatisfaction in the future. He was hoping that they would have more labour representa- tives on the Council in time, and then he did not think motions would be out of order quite as easily as they were at present. Councillor P. PEACOCK seconded. He thought the resolution would put an end to all the disputes they had from various districts and satisfy the workingmen who _ . . . .</p>
        <p>formed the biguest number of die electorate. Councillor Lo4o supported. Alderman SUTTON said he had often stated that he sympathised with the workingmen as much as anyone. He believed in them having as much wages as it was possible to give them, and he believed in them having everything as fair as possible on their side. At the same time he did not overlook the fact that they had others in Warrington besides the workingmen. While they were so desirous of the workingmen's good, they must consider the heavy ratepayers and manufacturers of the town; and if there was anything which in his opinion would prevent the manufacturers from accept- ing contracts which would be to the benefit of the public at large, he did not think it would be right and proper for the Council to interfere in matters of that kind. He believed it would be to the benefit of the 'work- ing-classes if they could see their way at times for the manufacturers to pay even a smaller rate of wages in order to get a large contract, than that they should have no work at all. He always held that it was better to have three parts of a loaf than no loaf at all. They had already, as part of their byelaws, clauses which referred to the question of contracts, and he thought they had gone as far as it was possible for them to go. Councillor BURTON said he had not intended to speak on the question. He stood in a position between masters and men which, he thought, ought to prevent him from giving an opinion on the matter. After what Alderman Sutton had said, he felt bound to reply. The Alderman contended that in order to assist manufacturers to get contracts, workingmen should work for a little less—but to his ;the speaker's) mind that was altogether out of the question. (Hear, hear.) If they adopted a state of things like that, the working man's wages would be reduced time after time, until in the end he would come to starvation. He had been a union man in principle all his life. He was con- nected with the iron trade when their union was founded, and he was one of the first to join. He did not see anything in the clauses which was very objection- able. They were practically what had been in force before, but they were set out in a way that the Council would understand better and they would probably prevent a great deal of quibbling and talking in the future. To his mind the union men of Warrington were always found to be the best men to rely upon, and they were worthy of atten- tion. If the clauses would satisfy the workingmen he was going to support them. Before the union com- menced it was a common thing to have lock-outs of a month, six Weeks, and two or three months in the iron trade, and many families were thrown on the streets and suffered very keenly. He congratulated the officials of the Ironworkers' Association upon the way in which they had conducted their business, and upon not having had to resort to a strike or lock-out for so many years. (Hear, hear.) . If the trade union officials of Warring- ton could conduct their business like that, he thought the Council would act wisely in accepting the resolution. Councillor PEMBERTON asked whether the Council would be compelled to carry out the clauses to the letter if they were passed. The TOWN CLERK: I suppose so. Councillor PEMBERTON said that while he was in _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>sympathy with the resolution and with the chief object of Councillor Stott, he considered clause 6 was very arbitrary. The standing orders were to apply to sub- contractors,•and that meant that if a man in executing an order for 10,000 tons of coal happened to employ a carter on non-union terms to cart only a part of it, he would be debarred from contracting for five years, and Warrington would not have the benefit of his competi- tion. He was perfectly in accord with the other clauscs. There was not the slightest doubt that unless compulsion was brought to hear on a great many people the standard rate of wages would never be paid. Councillor ASHCROFT, as a trade unionist of thirty years standir g, supported the resolution. He believed it would stop a great deal of "sweating" by people who contracted with the Corporation. Couhcillor WILKINSON said he was very pleased to see that standing order 46 (already in force) covered the I whole of Councillor Stott's motion with the exception of clause 6. He heartily supported the motion with the exception of that clause. He thought it would scarcely work and would be rather hard. The resolution as a whole would be to the advantage of manufacturers who hitherto might have been beaten by cheap labour. Councillor DODDS said he received a copy of the clauses from Councillor Stott and on comparing them with the Standing Orders he came to the conclusion that in as much as the Council decided some years ago to endeavour to meet the trade unionists in the matter and the present standing orders did not satisfy them, they ought to be willing to go far enough to adjust the standing orders to meet the requirements of the trade unionists. He did not think the resolution would affect the large employers at all. He believed the large employers practically worked in accordance with trade union ideas. They employed trade unionists, and they preferred to deal with them. He personally was quite prepared to support the clauses as they stood, although five years was perhaps a heavy penalty. He suggested that that part might be modified. Councillor TINNION said the only clause he had any objection to was the sixth. He thought Councillor Stott would be perfectly satisfied if they carried all the other clauses and omitted that one. The penalty seemed to be rather heavier than in some cases the sin might warrant. The "sweating speech of Alderman Sutton certainly charmed him. If he (Councillor Tinnion) had had no idea as to which way he should vote, after hearing that excellent speech in favour of sweating he would certainly have voted for the resolution. It was agreed to vote upon each clause separately,and clauses 1,2, 3, 4 and 5 were carried unanimously. In regard to clause 6, Councillor STorr said it was I not within his power to withdraw. If any cases arose in which the application of the clause would create hardships, he was sure the Council would take a reason- able view. He believed if the clause was allowed to stand it would prevent many employers from breaking the fair wage conditions, whereas if the term was two years they would be inclined to take the risk. Councillor BARTON seconded.</p>
        <p>Aiii.;;;;S—u—rr—o—s—siiici he objected to the assertions of Councillor Tinnion who was so ready to get up and , talk on all occasions and did it in such a superior way. (Laughter.) He objected altogether to the in- sinuation that he was in favour of sweating, because he was not. He was in favour of the workingmen, and in proof of that he did not come to the meeting with any intention of oppo sing the clauses. He simply wanted to express his opinion and endeavour to bring them down to something less than they were, but under other circumstances he would have supported them. "There- fore" concluded the alderman "Don't make remarks that you are not justified in making. We have heard quite enough of you and don't want to hear any more." (Laughter.) Councillor Tissfox said there was no reason why they should not have a clause covering all the ground but with a smaller penalty. He denied that he was "having go" at Alderman Sutton whose speech was a 'sweating" speech. He said that if a man could get a contract by paying something less than the standard rate'of wages— Alderman SUTTON: I did'ut say anything of the kind. Councillor TINNION : He said if we passed this motion. we should prevent a man doing that sort of thing, and therefore it was wrong, to pass it. If he did'nt make a "sweating" speec h,I don't know what he did make. If he is .sorry and repentant, I accept his repentance, and hope he will be a good trade unionist all his life. (Laughter). Councillor WILK INSON said that in a conversation that morning, Alderman Sutton assured him that he entirely agreed with the bulk of the motion, but he would like it modifying. Councillor PEMBERTON said the penalty proposed was such'as no trade union in the country had ever tried to extract. Councillor STorr said a similar clause was enforced by the Leicester and Sheffield Councils. Councillor PinlnErrroN said if the clause only applied to contractors he would not object so much. A vote was then taken as follows : FOR THE CTAUSE. AGAINST. Councillor Stott Alderman Fai rclough If Davies Hutchinson Ashcroft Councillor Pemberton „ Barton Thornton P. Peacock Forshaw Lord-6 oberts Tinnion •• \Vilkinson—</p>
        <p>Clause G was therefore defeated. 1, Clause 7 was unanimously passed.</p>
      </div>
