      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <p>THE BATTLE OF LIAOYANG.</p>
        <p>The sole topic of the week has been the situation in the Far East, and since the Franco-German war no such battle has been fought as the great fight round Liaoyang and the railway line to Mukden. Port Arthur has ceased to be the centre of attraction, and after the great assault which though by no means unsuccessful in many details, failed in its main objective, things, so far as can be gathered, have been com- paratively quiet round the great fortress. It even seems possible that some of the attack- ing force may have been drawn off tc strengthen the armies round Liaoyang just as it is said that General Liuievitch with 40,000 men is marching from Vladivostock against the Japanese rear, but the JapanesE are a model of repression and announce, of -allow nothing to be announced, until it it accomplished. The great battle of last week in which practically half a million men seem to have been engaged, though ending in the Japanese favour, was indecisive and has only led up to an even greater one nearer Muk- den. At the time of writing Liaoyang has • practically been taken, inasmuch as the main Russian army has fallen back, but it is doubtful whether the Japanese have fully occupied it. General Kuropatkin, in chang- ing his front to meet General Kuroki, has left a strong rearguard to hold Generals Oku 4nd Nodzu in check as far as possible, and General Stackelberg is reported cut off with twenty-five thousand men to the west. General Oku commands the Japanese left and General Nodzu the centre, while Field- Marshal Oyama is throwing his army across the Taitse River to take General Kuropatkin on the left. General Kuroki with a large army is heading for Mukden and the railway, and has outstripped the Russians. General Kuropatkin, who has made Yentai his head- quarters, has reotganised his army and its disposition so as tactically to concentrate his front against General Kuroki, on whose brilliant march ,depends the whole of the Japanese scheme. If Kuropatkin can crush Kuroki the victory will be his. On the other hand, if Kuroki be strong enough to cut him off and hold him, his army will have to surrender or be cut to pieces. Thus the situation is highly critical and fraught with the most intense interest, and the world is. watching breathless. The battle of Liaoyang has developed into a bigger affair than Sedan, and should Kuro- patkin have to surrender it will be the most dramatic event within the memory of living men. The casualty lists have been terrible, making our losses in South Africa pale almost into significance.</p>
        <p>The above map shows the position of the contending armies at Liaoyang. East of Liaoyang, on the Taitse River, an arrow indicates the spot at which General Kuroki crossed, and the fight he is making to cut off the Russian retreat along the railway may be gathered from the position of his forces as indicated by the black oblongs, which reach as far as Yen- tai, where an engagement took place on Thursday, with result so far unknown. South of Liaoyang is the Centre Army under General Nodzu, and west of it is the Left Army under General Oku, pressing the Russians back on the Taitse. Shusan, the hill mentioned in con- nection with the desperate fighting on Thursday at dawn, is immediately below the position occupied by the Centre Army. From Liaoyang to Mukden is forty-five miles, twenty-three miles of the road being shown on the map, and from Liaoyang to Sinminting is fifty miles, of which.half is indicated. These are the two possible lines of retreat for the Russian Army.</p>
        <p>some years, eventually succeeding his uncle in the peerage in 1894. Meanwhile he had travelled widely, and he had formed a close friendship with Mr. Cecil Rhodes. In 1896 after the Jameson Raid he became Adminis- trator of Rhodesia at .an anxious time and quelled the Matabele rising with conspicuous success. He is now Vice-President of the British South Africa Company and is one of the trustees under Mr. Rhodes's will. He is also the originator of. a model public-house trust, which is proving a promising and practical attempt to tackle the drink prob- lem. Another important appointment is that of Sir Edwin Egerton, British Ambassa- dor to Spain, who is to leave Madrid for Rome, where he will succeed Sir Francis Bertie. It is only last year that Sir Edwin Egerton after forty-four 'years in the diplo- matic. service was raised to ambassadorial rank, when Sir Mortimer Durand was trans- ferred to Washington. He has served in his younger days in all the capitals of Europe, constantly moving from one post to another. Ultimately from Egypt he was moved to Constantinople, and in 1892 he was appointed British Minister at Athens, where he re- mained eleven years.. The appointment is popular and on all sides admitted to be a good one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>THE. BRIDGEWATER :TRUST.</head>
        <p>THE. BRIDGEWATER :TRUST.</p>
        <p>The Earl of Ellesmere has celebrated his accession to the Bridgewater estates and colliery properties by entertaining 600 tenants, farmers, heads of the various departments, and members of his staffs at his Lancashire residence, Worsley New Hall. At the same time the whole of his work- people were also given a general holiday. These celebrations, it is interesting to recall, mark the closing chap- ter of a little romance which has given rise lA', many pictur- esque but by no means accurate stories. Only last year Lord Ellesmere had occasion to con- tradict the re- port that was EARL OF ELLESMERE. circulated to the effect that on March Bth, the centenary of the Duke of Bridgewater's death, he had become, under the Duke's will, one of the richest men in Great Britain. The present Lord Ellesmere is fifty-seven years old and one of the greatest men on the turf. He is also something of a novelist, among his books being "A Broken Stirrup-leather" and "A Sapphire Ring." It was his great uncle who founded the canal systems of England and left the will by which the Earl became his heir.</p>
        <p>CANADA'S NEW VICEROY AND NEW BRITISH MINISTER</p>
        <p>AT ROME.</p>
        <p>It was widely rumoured that the Duke of Marlborough was to succeed the Earl of Minto aS Governor-General of Canada, but all doubt was set at, rest last week by the -announcement that the King had appointed 'Earl Grey to . succeed his brother-in-law in the Dominion. Lord *Grey's appointment is a far more popular one than the other would have been, as he is a considerably older man with - -ter experience: His career</p>
        <p>EARL GREY. SIR EDWIN EGERTON. has been closely connected with the Royal Pamij tram birth upwards. Born in St. James's Palace fifty- hree years ago when his lather, General th:: Hon. Charles Grey, was Private secretary to Queen Victoria, the Prince Consort stood as his godfather, and in 77 he married the sister of Captain Hol- rd, the King's Equerry: After a dis- nguished career at Harrow and Cambridge '434 Grey sat in the House of Commons for</p>
