      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>ELOPEMENT OF PRINCESS LOUISE OF COBURG.</head>
        <p>ELOPEMENT OF PRINCESS LOUISE OF COBURG.</p>
        <p>There is little doubt that in England public sympathy is all on the side of Prin. cess Louise of Coburg in her ..romantic elopement, which might more properly be described as a rescue. For many years the facts of this unfortunate royal scandal have been before the public, and a book dealing with the whole case appeared as recently as last January. Princess Louise, who is the eldest daughter of the King of the Belgians, and on her mother's side a grand-daughter of the Emperor of Austria, was married at</p>
        <p>!TINCESS LOUISE OF COBURG.</p>
        <p>the age of seventeen against her will to Prince Philip of Saxe-Coburg-Gotha, the head of the Roman Catholic branch of the family. After many years of unhappiness the Princess met Lieutenant Mattusich in 1896, and an attachment sprang up between them. A year later she left her husband and went away with him, travelling in Europe and iving in great style. A duel took place at Merau between Prince Philip and Lieu- tenant Mattusich in which the former was \slightly wounded, a fact which only served to advertise the scandal. Shortly after that they were both arrested in Croatia, and in May, 1898, Lieutenant Mattusich was sen- tenced to six years' imprisonment on a charge of forgery, said to have been trumped up, while the Princess was confined in a lunatic asylum on the ground that she was of unsound mind. About a year ago the Lieutenant was pardoned and released, but the Princess was strictly kept under guard. However, this summer she was sent to Ulster in Saxony, and from the hotel there a second elopement was managed last week. The Princess escaped by a ladder from her window at night, and her lover was waiting below with a motor, in which they got away without leaving a trace.</p>
