      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>DEATH OF THE BISHOP OF SOUTHWELL.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF THE BISHOP OF SOUTHWELL.</p>
        <p>Another distinguished member of the church has been removed by the death of the Right Rev. George Ridding, D.D., Bishop of Southwell, who died at Thurgaton Priory in his 76th year. The deceased prelate was the third son of the late Rev. Charles Ridding. Rector of An- dover, and he</p>
        <p>was educated at</p>
        <p>St. Mary's Col- lege, Winches-</p>
        <p>ve-r, and at</p>
        <p>Balßol, Oxford. Here he took</p>
        <p>honours</p>
        <p>both in classics and mathema-</p>
        <p>tics, and after</p>
        <p>his ordination in</p>
        <p>1856 he acted as</p>
        <p>Exeter College,</p>
        <p>and was.a junior</p>
        <p>proctor .6f the</p>
        <p>University. He</p>
        <p>subseqiiently THE LATE DR. RIDDING. became second</p>
        <p>master at Win-</p>
        <p>chester College, and during the sixteen year he was there his administration was marked by the sound judgment and capacity for organisation which were so conspicuous in him as first Bishop of Southwell. He was selected as first Bishop of Southwell on the creation of the see in 1884, and governed it for over twenty years with judgment and tact. A few weeks ago Dr. Ridding an- nounced in a pathetic farewell letter to his diocese his resignation of the bishopric, to date from the 2nd of next November, on the ground of his failing health and the illness from which he had then only partially re- covered. In his letter Dr. Ridding stated that he was glad to hope to be able to dispense with an application for a pension, which, in the exceptional circumstances of the diocese, he felt woulo he a serious embarrass. meat to his successor-</p>
