      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>HULL v. WIDNES.</head>
        <p>HULL v. WIDNES.</p>
        <p>After the great changes made in the Hull team,a good deal of interest was taken in their first appearance, and there was a great crowd at the Boulevard to witness the opening contest with Widnes. The visitors kicked off against a lively breeze, and in its preliminary phases the Oaf was by no means cheering, until the Hull men accomplished some fine play from a scrummage near the centre. Oliver got the ball, and, through Goodfellow and Cook, it passed to Hall, who made a grand run and passed it to Lewis, who scored the first try. Gooefellow failed at goal. oft On resuming,there were many stoppages for injuries, and several pauses for distributing cautions and protests. Three-quarter time was reached before Widnes scored. At this time Hull was suffering from the loss of Hall, who had retired. Towards the end of the game Hughes scbred eleverly under the posts, and he followed up his success with a second try, which was converted by Tilley. The home team were clearly outplayed. Result :—Widnes 1 goal and 3 tries (11 points), Hull 1 try_(3points). Teams:— _</p>
        <p>ttylor, back; Northrop, Goodfellow, Cook and Hall, threequarter backs; Weir and Oliver, half backs; Ritson (captain), J. Lewis, G. H. Lewis, Wade, Harrison, Kilburn, Hufto ,n and Hargreaves, forwards. Widnes—Tilley, back; R. Hughes, J. Taylor, Peacock and Barber, three-quarter backs ; Kennedy and Howard, half backs; Cockram Curtis, Leyshon, McCabe, Rigby, Webster, Turner, and Argent, forwards. Referee. Mr. W. M'Cutcheon,Oldham. Touch judges, Messrs. W. Pritchard, York, and J. H. Sewell, Leeds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>ST. HELENS V. OLDHAM.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS V. OLDHAM.</p>
        <p>Played at St. Helens. Oldham started play and Devereux marked. Oldham were penalised, i-nd Jones kicked a goal two minutes after the start. On the kick-off St. Helens were brought back for offside' and Mooney headed the St. Helens forwards in a rush down the field which led to play being in the Oldham quarters where Jones tackled Gardner, while Thomas was forced to kick into touch. A strong wind helped St. Helens considerably, and they kept Oldham penned in their own half. At last they got away, but Foulkes returned, and another free kick was given against Oldham. By dint of good work, Oldham reached the St. Helens half, where Spottiswoode got away, but Foulkes barred his progress. However, he managed to pass to Dixon, who ran well, but Liversedge brought him down. Towards half-time S. Helens attacked again, and Jones placed another goal. Lees put in a good run for Oldham, and then passed to Cash, who scored. Half-time score : St. Helens 4 point„. Oldham 3 points.</p>
        <p>In the second half St. Helena had the wind against them and play opened in their half, but a breakaway by the home forwards took play to Oldham's half,where Thomas was well tackled. Ferguson kicked a grand goal, thus giving Oldham the lead. Sam Lees kicked away from a scrummage, and Foulkes was tackled before he could clear. The ball was dribbled over the St. Helens line. where Ferguson had little difficulty ir scoring, and he also kicked a goal. Result :—Oldhar 2 goals 2 tries (10 points), St. Helens 2 goals (4 point, WIGAN v. SWINTON.</p>
        <p>At Central Park, Wigan, before 14,000 spectato Play throughout was vigorously contested, and Hectk placed a goal from a free kick. Wallwork scored fo Swinton, Molyneux goaling. The resumption sa,l Wigan pressing in determined fashion, and the Swint( line had in any narrow escapes. Result—Swinton, points ; Wi gan, 2 points.</p>
        <p>tm sfl s). IS. or )1* on 6W • 5</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>LEEDS v. BATLEY.</head>
        <p>LEEDS v. BATLEY.</p>
        <p>Played at Leeds. Phil Thomas, the crack three- quarter from Oldham, appeared for Leeds for the first time. In the first half Evans scored a couple of tries, and Wormald converted one of them. In the second portion Llewellyn (Leeds) and Abbott (Batley) scored tries, the former's being improved. A man on each side was injured. Result—Leeds, 13 points; Batley, 3 points.</p>
        <p>BROUGHTON RANGERS v. WAKEFIELD TRINITY.</p>
        <p>At Broughton, in fine weather, and before 7,000 people. Playing with the wind in the first half, Metcalfe, for the visitors, kicked three goals, two being from marks by Walton and Jacques. In the second half the Rangers attacked strongly, and from penalty kicks Wilson and James failed. Riley kicked a goal. Result —.Wakefield Trinity, 3 goals (6 points) ; Broughton Rangers, 1 goal (2 points.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>HALIFAX v. S.kLFORD.</head>
        <p>HALIFAX v. S.kLFORD.</p>
        <p>At Hanson-lane Halifax, before 9,00 'spectators. Halifax kicked off, Thomas replying. After give-and- take play the homesters worked to tht visitors' line, Riley breaking through, Little failing. Salford then attacked, Lomas scoring a converted try. Salford restarted, and pressed immediately. The home forwards repeatedly wheeled the scrums, but left the ball. Halifax had the worst of matters in the second half. Result: —Salford, s,;points ; Halifax, 3 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON V LEIGH.</p>
        <p>The opening match between these "A" teams at Wilderspool was witnessed by fully three thousand spectators. The non-appearance of the new half, J. D avies, occasioned some disappointment, but his credentials had not satisfied the Northern Union executive and the permit was refusocl. Almost from the start Warrington pressed hard, and a fine effort by Savage was checked just inside. The Leigh forwards rushed the ball out of danger, but their premature tactics got them into ttouble, and Knight had an easy shot at goal, but failed miserably. Leigh again played up briskly, and another capital forward movement was frustrated by Kenyon. Leigh had once more to resort to defensive tactics, and after Savage had been pulled up on the line, the same player had a chance a minute later, and utilizing it to the full, raced through the opposition and opened the scoring. Warrington did not relax their energies in the least, and another fine Movement culminated in Watkins scoring another unconverted try. Warrington led at the interval by six points to three. In the second half Leigh had the advantage of the breeze, and were soon on the aggres- sive but the defence was exceptionally sound, Bate, Kenyon and Jordan all rendering yeoman service. From a forward rush Leigh scored, but this proved to be their last chance. Savage, receiving beyond the centre line, scored a magnificent try near the posts, but Knight failed to add the extra points. Final : Warrington, three tries (nine points) ; Leigh, one try (three points).</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCH.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD ATHLETIC V. FAIRFIELD JUNIORS.</p>
        <p>Latchford were victorious in this match, pressing almost. from the outset. A scrimmage in the goalmouth was witnessed, from which T. Ford kicked a 'goal. Latchford ran down the field and the Fairfield fullback met the ball and twisted it into his goal. Latchford were leading at half-time by two goals to nil. Lowe scored for Latchford early in the second half. Two more goals followed in quick succession, one from Taylor and one from Lowe. Latchford won by five goals to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>-.._ WHERE DOES IT END? .</head>
