      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>SANKEY BROOK.</head>
        <p>SANKEY BROOK.</p>
        <p>Interesting Report on th 3 Pollution Question CONSIDERED BY SANKEY PARISH OHM,.</p>
        <p>A meeting; of the Great Sankey Parish Council was held in the I3oard School, Great Sankey, on Monday evening, Mr. G. Thompson presiding. There were also present :—Messrs. W. Taylor, W. Furness, W. Jefferson, J. Higgs, H. Fairclough. and Joseph Smith (clerk).</p>
        <p>SANKEY BROOK.-WFIAT THE RURAL COUNCIL IS DOING.</p>
        <p>At the last meeting, Messrs. Furness and Jeffer- son were appointed a deputation to wait upon the District Coancil with respect to. the pollu- tion of Sankey Brook. The following report, prepared by Mr. Furness, was now presented: Gentlemen,—ln accordance with a resolution passed by this Council, 5 appointing Mr. Jefferson and myself as a deputation to wait upon the District Council, with the object of laying before them the absolute necessity of taking steps to minimise the offensive character of the effluent which is allowed to enter Sankey Brook, your deputation before waiting upon the Distlict Council took the trouble to satisfy themselves as to the various sources of pollution entering the brook, a description of which may be of interest to your Council. Sankey Brook, as we know it, has its origin in the township of Windle and is there known as Windle Brook. The brook passes through the Borough of St. Helens eastward to a district called Parr, after leaving which it is known by the name of Sankey Brook, and this receives the waste product from chemical and other works, also the drainage from considerable alkali waste heaps ; after leaving St. Helens it passes through the Newton district and enterathe Warrington Rural Sanitary District close to Penkford Bridge,Burtonwood. It is bounded on the west side by the sanitary district of Prescot, and on the east side by the sanitary district of Haydock, coming under the District Councils' jurisdiction. It passes within a few hundred yards of Winwick Asylum, through Dallam and Bewsey and on to Sankey Bridges close to which it empties itself into the Mersey. We found the principal sources of pollution are the various chemical works in St. Helens who allow their effluent to enter, the brook without treatment. The effluent emitted by the St.Helens Corporation is also of a very offensive character, and from this point we may say the actual pollution of the brook commences, as it is here that the offensive nuisance of which residents in the vicinity complain, is caused by the constant go- ing off of sulphuretted hydrogen, gas, caused by the action of hydrochloric acid on alkali waste liquor, and impregnates the atmosphere for miles around. We visited the sewage farm of Winwick Asylum where every facility was given for our inspection. We found the method of treatment here—which is called the bacteria treatment—to be of a very satisfactory char- acter. About 130,000 gallons are treated by this process per day, acid the effluent after treatment, is all that could be desired. One of the filter beds, while we were there, was discharged into the brook, and was clear and gave no offensive smell. We also saw samples of the effluent two and three months old, and these n ere clear and emitted no smell. We are quite satisfied that the Winwick Asylum is not contaminating the brook. Coming nearer Warrington we found that the Winwick- road sewer outlet, near Orford Brook, was entering the brook in a crude state and is of the most offensive character—being practically raw sewage. This is also •supplemented by an effluent from the Warrington Gas Works, which while contaminating the brook somewhat minimises the offensive character of the crude sewage. Near this point there enters waste liquid from the Orford Tannery which is also of a very offensive character and gives off a most objectionable smell. The District Council is also responsible for a slight pollution of the crook, which, were it not for the liquid turned in from the gas works, would constitute a nuisance of a very objectionable character. We thought it well to visit the various sources of pollution before meeting—as your deputation—the District Council. Your deputation met the District Council and the con- dition of the brook was fully discussed. We found the Chairman and Council very anxious to do all in their] power to stop the nuisance; and after fully discussing the matter with Mr. Jefferson and myself the District Council resalved tirst'of all to stop the offence which they are committing, and they at once gave their Surveyor instructions to prepare a scheme for that purpose, after which it is their intention to compel all offenders above the stream to treat their sewage in like manner. It was felt by the Council that this could only be done after they had put their own house in order. When this is I done, pressure will beThrought to bear upon the Local I Government Board to compel all offenders to stop or minimise the offence. Mr. J. Lyon Whittle, the town I clerk, has assured me that if the offenders above War- I rington would discontinue the offence, he would at once I give an undertaking on behalf of the Corporation ' to at once take measures for the treatment of the offence of which the Corporation are guilty. In conclusion, '.your deputation are of opinion that their interview with the District Council has been of a very satisfactory character, and believe it is the initial step' towards purifying the brook, and will therefore be in- I strumental in.doing away with what has been for a great I number of years a standing menace to the health and ' comfort of the west section of the town of Warrington and the district of Sankey. The Chairman said they were very much indebted to Messrs. Furness and Jefferson for the great deal of 1 trouble they had taken and the splendid report they had issued. It was a pleasure to read the report, and he thought it would do much good. Mr. Furness said the deputation found that at Long- ford Depot the Corporation were extracting from the brook something like 120,000 gallons daily. The water was used for condensing purposes and when it was again turned into the brook the water was heated arA was in a far worse condition than when it was extracted. This matter was not re- ferred to in the report, because the manager of the depot told them that the Sanitary Committee were experimenting with a view to minimising the offensive character of the effluent. He (Mr. Furness) thought that before very long they might expect some improve- .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION. NORTHERN LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION. NORTHERN LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>WARRINaTON OPEN WITH A VICTORY.</p>
        <p>Warrington's opening match of the 1904-5 season was played on Saturday at Leigh and resulted in a hand- some victory gor the visitors, who maintained the form which carried them into the Cup final only four months before. The Warrington side was a very strong one, three fine forwards in Preston, Shugars, and Belton—all importations during the close season—turning out for the first time. Great interest was shown in the game by supporters of both clubs, and there was a "gate" of about 5,000,fu11y one-fifth of whom had come from War- rington. Leigh were handicapped by the non-arrival of Messer, but Burgess took his place at three-quarter, the full back's position being well filled by Pearson. Mr. R. H. Moore of Oldham, was the referee, and the teams lined out as follows:— Leigh—E. Pearson, back; S. Johnson, B. Gartland, H. Bennett (capt.),and A. Burgess,threequarter backs; J.H. Dunbavin and P. Brady:half backs; G. Boardman, W. Roberts, J. Kelly, J. A. Blackman, P. O'Neill, A. Lee, R. Silcock, and R. Berry, forwards. Warrington—Hallam (capt.), back; Fish, Isherwood, Dickensorr, and Harris, three-quarter backs ; Davies and Hockenhall, half backs; Thomas, Cook, Shugars, Belton, Preston, Naylor, Jolley,and A. E. Boardman, forwards. Leigh were nine minutes late on entering the field. There was a strong wind blowing down the field,and the homesters winning the toss naturally elected to play with it in the first half. They were soon on the attack owing to poor fielding of Dunbavin's, kicks by Hallam at back and for some minutes the ball hovered in the vicinity of the Warrington goal, but the efforts of the Leigh men to make full use of their advantage did not give cause for any anxiety, and the home backs were permitted to exhibit their kicking abilities to the full, Hallam and Fish in turn saving from injudicious play by Bennett and Dunbavin. Burgess, whose mission was evidently to " shadow " Fish was tricked well, and Leigh were compelled to retreat to near half-way where Pearson replied. Isherwood retali- ated splendidly, and this brilliant effort was seconded by the Warrington backs showing fine combination, Harris missing scoring by the narrowest of shaves. Scrummaging on the line followed, but to the relief of the Leighites the referee penalised Warrington. A long kick soon gave Leigh the tipper hand again, only to be repulsed by a dashing forward rush headed by the burly Preston. Dunbavin next took a shot at goal from a penalty but the kick went wide. Burgess was next safely held up by Fish. The spectators had not long to wait for a substantial score, for the visitors were again penalized, much to the chagrin of their sup- porters, and Dunbavin drew first blood by steering the ball between the posts. Preston restarted and Bennett was floored before he could reply, and Davies broke away splendidly from a pack,and Hockenhall,lsherwood, and Dickenson joined hands in the movement. Harris made a praiseworthy dash to cross, Johnson impeding his progress at the quarter flag. Leigh attacked again, and at half-time were still leading by a goal to nil.</p>
        <p>Warrington, in the second halt, hid trie assistance of the wind,but for a time Leigh showed stubborn defence, their forwards repeatedly gaining possession of the ball, and the half backs finding touch judiciously. At last clever play by Hockenhall and Dickenson ended in an opening being made for Harris, who outran the Leigh baclis and scored at the corner, no goal resulting. Strong forward play was next witnessed at mid-field, and for some informality Preston came under the ban of the referee, and was compelled to retire. Left with fourteen men Warrington continued to exhibit superiority,and after Fish had placed his side in a good position Davies and Hockenhall each in turn tried to break through. Their efforts were unsuccessful, but a better fate awaited another combined effort which was worked on similar lines to that which resulted in the first try, and Harris again crossed at the corner, but in his effort to make the position more easy for the goal kick he had the misfortune to wrench his right knee and took no further part in the ,game. Fish placed a magnificent goal. Thomas took the iacancy in the three-quarter line, and though their forces were now weakened by the absence of two players the visitors were always masters of the situation. The depleted forces of forwards worked like trojans, whilst Thomas showed rare resource when the ball travelled his way. Davies and Blackburn had a disagreement in mid-field and also received marching orders, so that the Warrington team had only 12 men against the opposi- tion. The visiting backs, however, were always too clever for the Leigh men and after Hallam had kicked to Pearson, the Leigh custodian made a poor return and Dickenson seizing the opportunity, slipped past several of the home backs and scored in clever style. Fish failed at the goal. Leigh's cup of bitterness was not yet quite full, but after Isherwood had broken away twice in quick succession and scores seemed certainties for Fish, this player next took advantage of a mistake by Burgess, and raced over, also placing a goal. Thomas was next hdld up after a rug from half-way and at the close Leigh were completely outclassed. Final: War- rington, two goals, four tries (16 points); Leigh, one goal (two points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>RUNCORN v. HULL KINGSTON ROVERS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN v. HULL KINGSTON ROVERS.</p>
        <p>About 2,000 spectators assembled on the Runcorn ground to witness the opening match with the Kingston Rovers. Walker commenced for Runcorn. Windle was caught before he could reply. After several minutes' stoppage with Watton hurt. Chambers got possession, and with a shart run transferred the ball to Gordon, who spoiled a fine chance of scoring by failing to take the pass. A moment later, however, Carmichael placed a goal for the visitors. Resuming, play was contested in the Rovers' half, where scrummaging followed. A fine round of passing, in which Jolley, Richardson, Warder, and Jones took part, 'aided in the last-named scoring a try, giving Runcorn the leadi Jolley failed at goal. The visitors attacked, and Starks placed a goal for the Rovers, who led by one point at the interval. After the i iterval Starks commenced for the visitors, but the home forwards rushed the ball to the Rovers' half, and Richardson scored a try, thus placing Runcorn ahead again. Jolley missed the goal. Chambers, Car- michael,and Winpenny took up the attack, but the home men soon got back to mid-field, where Houghton made two fine attempts to place a goal. The Rovers were then forced to defend, and the game ended in favour of the home team. Result :—Runcorn, 2 tries (6 points); Hull Kingston Rovers; 2 goals (4 points.) Teams : Runcorn.—S. Houghton, back ; Inglesfield, Warder, J. Richardson, and Jones, three-quarter backs ' • Jolley and Watton, half backs; Walker, Moulton, A. Richardson, Dixon, Padbury, Dobson, Higgins, and C. Houghton, forwards.</p>
        <p>Hull Kingston Rovers.—Sinclair, back: Winpenny, Carmichael, Gordon, and J. \V. Reed, three-quarter backs ; Chambers and Cork, half backs; Starks, Kemp, Windle, Gorman, Ellis, Osborne, Speakman, and Picker- ing, forwards. Referee, Mr. W. Robinson, Bradford. Touch :judges, Mr. F. Turner, Warrington,and Mr. E. Gordon, Widnes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <p>ELA.STIC STOCKINGS, KNEE CAPS, &amp;C.—For Varicos Veins an all cases of Weakness and Swelling of the Leg, Sprains, &amp;c. They are porous, light in texture, and inexpensive and are drawn on like an ordinary stocking.—Prices 4s. od., 7s. 64. and 103. 6(1. each. Postage free.—WHITE &amp; WRIGHT, Surgical Instrumen and Truss Makers, 98, Renshaw St., Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <p>ment there. If the Corporation could only treat water which they took from the brook it would mean a con- siderable difference in the condition of the stream. He thought the action taken at the recent Parish Council meeting had stirred up the authorities. Tile Chairman moved that the report be adopted, and that copies be sent to the Clerk of the District Council and the Town Clerk.</p>
        <p>Mr. Higgs seconded and the motion was agreed to. A vote of thanks was accorded to the deputation on the motion of the Chairman,seconded by Mr. Taylor. In responding,Mr. Furness said that while the duties had not been very pleasant they had been a source of education to Mr. Jefferson and himself.</p>
        <p>Mr. Jefferson said the report was not as strong as they might have made it. The Borough of Warrington was the great offender. The sewage from Orford Ward was abominable, as it was turned into the brook in as crude a state as possible.</p>
        <p>LIGHTING MATTERS. The Clerk read a letter from the Penketh Parish 'Council stating that if Sankey Parish Council would erect a lamp on their side of the Cemetery Road, Penketh, Parish Council would erect one on their's. It was decided to recommend the District Council to erect a lamp at the place suggested. The Clerk said he had had an interview with Mr. Taylor, secretary of the Gas Department at Warrington, with regard to the suggested reduction in the price of lamps at Sankey, and he had also seen Mr4l-laddock, the gas engineer. The result was that he had received a letter from Mr. Haddock intimating that the Corpora- tion were prepared to reduce the price if the Sankey Council would put up lamps intermediate with the present ones, which would save the Corporation part of the cost of lighting. At present this was costing them about 15s. 6d. per lamp. If the Parish Council had twenty-five lamps the cost would be about 12s. 6d. per lamp, if thirty about 10g. 6d., and the Corporation would be prepared to give them the benefit of this saving. They could supply No. 3 Kerns incandescent burners— the same:as were fixed in, Penketh, at 58s. perannutn for one light. This reduction was caused by the Parish Cotmcil having fixed extra lamps last year. For each extra jet per lamp the cost would be 255. per annum. It was decided to ask the District Council to adopt the incandescent lighting in the parish.</p>
        <p>THE DANGERS- OF SANKEY STATION. The Chairman said that some time ago a very sad accident took place at the Cheshire Lines station, and at that time the Parish Council took up the matter and suggested to the Cheshire Lines Committee that they should provide a booking office on the far side, or a subway or bridge by which the public could cross from one side to the other. He thought any gentleman who knew anything of the station would admit that it was a very dangerous. place. The railway company had no bridge of their own, and after booking, passengers had to cross to the other side by means of the highway. It would take an elderly person fully five minutes to do this. People generally had not much time to spare and they crossed the metals. One never knew what might happen and he thought , the Council should approach the new manager of the Cheshire Lines, and suggest that some- thing should be done to avoid any further accidents. He moved that the clerk write to the•manager, suggest- ing the provision of a booking ogee on the far side, or failing that a subway or bridge. Mr. Fairclough seconded. The station was very dangerous as there was a curve in the line at each approach. _ _</p>
        <p>The proposition was agreed to. HOOD LANE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Higgs asked if-th-e-D-iSiriCt C)uncil intended to proceed with the making of Hood Lane at an early date. The Chairman replied in the affirmative. AN UNPLEASANT SURPRISE-RATES INCREASED,. The Chairman said the Clerk and himself were very much surprised the other day to receive the county rate call. They expected the amount would be £275 and they were astonished to have a demand for R 342. The matter had been a source of great anxiety to the over, seers and assistant overseer, and the call would neces- sitate a rate of 2d. in the £ over and above what had been collected. There was a probability of a repetition of the advance next half year.so that there would bean increase of 4d.in the rates for the year. The new call reduced their balance by which was equal to the 2d.rate,and that balance had to be made good. They were trying to keep the rates as low as they could, but they could not see their way to do anything less than add 2d. in the R. He supposed edu- cation was thecause. The Clerk said that if Mr. Sutton's estimates for the Warrington Union were increased it was possible that another penny in the 2 would be require]. He did not think there was much possibility.ef a reduction. The Chairman : It is my idea that the rates will go up all round. Mr. Fairclough : It is a very serious thing. The Clerk said that in conversation with several other assistant overseers, he found that they had been treated in just the same way. Their precepts were increased in proportion to those for Sankey. The advance would be applicable to the whole county. Mr. Higgs: I have no doubt it is going for educa- tion.</p>
        <p>At this stage the Council resolved into committee to consider other business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>GUESTS HOT, AND DISHES COLD.</head>
        <p>GUESTS HOT, AND DISHES COLD.</p>
        <p>"Guests hot and dishes cold "is now the rule. There is no more delightful dish for a hot summer's day than a Brown and Poison Blanc Mange. It is made with Br wn and Poison's " Patent" cornflonr and sweet milk. There is quality in this Blanc Mange, there is flavour, daintiness, food value. Served with stewed fresh fruit it is a cooling, appetizing and refreshing dish for a hot summer day. Recipes for serving corn flour and fruit in twenty-one different ways are given in Brownand Poison's booklet. Summer Sweets," to be had for a penny stamp from Brotyn ani PAsou, Paisley. Send for it now, else you miy forget.</p>
        <p>RAILWAY MYSTERY AT LYIVINI. Clerk's Pathetic Warning to his Children. Mr. R. Dobson, coroner, held an inquest on Monday afternoon at Lymm, on the body of Charles Venables Harper, aged fifty-eight, clerk, who resided at 80, West High-street, Salford, and who was found on the side of the line of the London and North-Western Railway at Statham, near Warrington, on Saturday morning. Charles Harper, 231, Stockport-road. Manchester, said the deceased was his father, and he last saw him alive about three months ago. He then appeared all right And had no tendency towards depression of spirits. He had given way to drink more or less habitually. Witness then handed the coroner a letter which he found in his father's desk. It WM.' dated August 28th, and was addressed to his employer, as follows :—" Dear Sir— I most kindly apologise for having stayed away without due permission, but really I was taken with a severe attack of diarrhcea, which. together with being so thoroughly run down,sent me off my head, and I have been tramping about the country and sleeping in open fields,and now have returned worn, weary, and footsore. I (lire not expect to keep the position after such treatment, and so beg to hand you all the keys I halve had. Should you be inclined to be charitable,a letter will fetch me as soon as I am better, but lam very ill. Anyhow, a day's rest will make all the difference.—Yours obediently, Chas. Harper." A letter, dated August 30th, from his employer, was also read, to the effect that if he was not there at 10.30 on Wednesday the matter would be placed in the hands of the police. Witness said that he understood that his father col- lected an account for 225., and had not accounted for it.</p>
        <p>In a letter to his wife, deceased wrote: "I acknow- ledge my defects and also my defeats. Now at the last moment I tell you I trust in my Saviour.—Your loving Charlie."</p>
        <p>II Another letter,dated September Ist, was read: "It is now about 4 p.m. lam at Lymrn, hungry, thirsty and footsore, having been walking since 6 a.m. I have not a farthing on me, and nearly, if not altogether, out of my mind. The mental and bodily suffering lam undergoing is too much, and will kill me. I have not ' had a bite to eat to-day. What will become of me the good God alone knows. I pray for His forgive- ness.—C. H." On September 2nd deceased wrote: "6 a.m. Here am I still at, Lyrnm, or rather nearer Latchford, to where I have &amp;turned after sleeping four hours on wet grass on the banks of the canal. I don't know what to do, as I have no money, and nothing to sell. My feet are so blistered and footsore that I can scarcely put them to the ground. From 6 a.m. yesterday to 8 p.m. I was on the tramp, and lam sure walked fifty miles. I must be moving about to get away from myself, but cannot rest above three minutes, but have mercy and take me home to rest. I am so tired of my life. What must I do? I have just begged a pint of water, which is all that has passed my lips since Thursday morning. I think I am the really wandering Jew." After alluding to some gambl- ing losses and to the letter from his master, the deceased concluded : " I arn being terribly punished and starved to death. Good-bye all, and let my children and grand- children have proper warning never to toach intoxicat- ing liquor, never to begin, and they will eealize the great and happy aspirations Iliad when young.—Chas. Harper." Another piece of paper contained copies of advertised i situations, on which was also written : "Here am I I alone, not a friend in the world, and if I had the money that is owing to me I could pay all my debts, and have a balance sufficient to start me in business. Such is life. •Man's inhumanity to man makes countless num- bers mourn.—C. H." I John Lowndes spoke to finding the body on the line. The deceased was lying on his back, and his head was about a foot from the line and his feet towards the I bank. There were marks as if someone had been rolling on the grass on the embankment. Acting Sergeant Frost said that'the body was warm when he saw it. There were bruises on the hip and elbow, which indicated a fall. Dr. Lunt, who made a post-mortem examination, said that death might have been due to shock inconsequence of the injuries he had received, or to cerebral hemorr- hage. The jury returned an open verdict.</p>
      </div>
