      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>Sold Wherever Civilization Has</head>
        <p>Sold Wherever Civilization Has</p>
        <p>Penetrated.</p>
        <p>Millions of the world's best people use Cuticura Soap, assisted by Cuticura Ointment, the great skin cure, for pre- serving, purifying and beautifying the skin, for cleansing the scalp of crusts, scales and dandruff, and the stopping of falling hair, for softening, whiten- ing and soothing red, rough and sore hands, for baby rashes, itchings and chafings, for annoying irritations, or too free or offensive perspiration, for ulcerative weaknesses, and many sana- tive, antiseptic purposes which readily suggest themselves to women, espe- cially mothers, as well as for all the purposes of the toilet, bath and nursery. Cuticura Soap combines delicate emollient properties derived from Cuti- cura, the great skin cure, with the purest of cleansing ingredients and the most refreshing of Bower odours. No other medicated soap ever compounded is to be compared with it for preserv- ing, purifying and beautifying the skin, scalp, hair and hands. No other foreign or domestic toilet soap, however expensive, is to be compared with it for all the purposes of the toilet, bath and nursery. Thus it combines in one soap at one price the most, effective skin and complexion soap, and the purest and sweetest toilet, bath and nursery soap.</p>
        <p>Sold throughout the world. Cuticura Resolvent.. 2s. 64. (In form of Chocolate Coated Pills, Is. lid. per vial of 60), Ointment, 2,. 6d., Soap, Is. Depots: London, 27 Charter- house Sq.; Paris, d Rue de la Paix ; Roston, IV Colum- bus Ave. Potter Drug &amp; Chem. Corp., Sole Props. ear Sendfor "Bow to Cure Every Humour."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>' MOTOR NOTES.</head>
        <p>' MOTOR NOTES.</p>
        <p>The absence of the only four-cylindered car entered for the Small Car Trials was a matter of very general ree,,,ret,for there was considerable conjecture concerning its probable behaviour in the matter of hill-climbing.</p>
        <p>The "expert" opinion, however, recently published to the effect that four cylinders are unnecessary com- plication for a 10 h.p. car, is absurd. In our opinion twin cylinder engines should be employed upon quite the smallest voiturettes, whilst four cylinders would be naturally even more satisfactory, especially with a 10 h,p. motor.</p>
        <p>r AS a matter of fact, thftrnistake lies in using single cylinder engines for light 'cars, although it is no un- common thing to find a four-seated car of eight or nine h.p. merely fitted with a single cylinder engine. The Decauville Co., did the right thing when, years ago, they built a little 5 h.p. voiturette with a twin-cylinder engine. That this proved to be absolutely efficacious and reliable in practise was shewn by its record made on the Crystal Palace track—one thousand miles without. a single stop of road-wheels or engine. With this per- formance, which dates from 1900,the recent trials com- pare unfavourably. Only fifty miles were required to be covered at a stretch without a stop; and yet, in spite of four year's advance in motor knowledge and con- struction, there were few indeed, that half-way through the trials week,could lay claim to six non-stop runs of fifty miles each. The majority of the competing cars were fitted with single-cylinder engines.</p>
        <p>In spite of the Local Board regulations upon tAe sub- ject, some of the large cars are supplied with acetylene lamps of such overpowering brilliancy that upon a dark, night it is really dangerous for any cyclist travelling to- wards them, since their effect is such as to temporarily blind them. This unpleasant result could easily be avoided and yet the road could be sufficiently illuminated if the upper part of the lamp were to be hooded, so as to throw the light forward and downward rather than diffuse its glare all over the road.</p>
        <p>A photograph had just been published in one of the trade papers showing the remains of a car which appears to have been complete.' wrecked through trying con- clusions with a brick wall. It is stated that the engine itself is not hurt in the least. It does not seem to occur to tbo-ie responsible for the statement that the car itself is beyond redemption, and that, as an engine, the motor which is not supposed to be injured, would not be of very much use now. The extent of the damage that is inflictqd by such a collision can never be properly estimded, and the motorist who would fit such an engine with a new body would be better advised to sell it for what it would fetch, and buy a completely new car altogether, for there would certainly be no satisfaction derived from the old one in a pitched-up condition.</p>
        <p>The wheels, too, after a bad collision are bound to suffer, especially if they are of the usual artillery type. If not, apparently, too mach damaged to be used again, there is no doubt that they are materially weakened and it will take but a very slight impact later on to start the spokes or perhaps even cause the wheel to collapse entirely. It is neglecting to evAmine them atfter some such occurence as that referred to above hat frequently leads to a sudden fracture later on and, consequently, to a more or less serious accident for the collapse of one of the wheels when a car is being driven at even a moderately fast pace is bound to be rather tragic in its consequence—as witness the accident which recently happened.. in the case of Sir William Rattigan.</p>
      </div>
