      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <p>-</p>
        <p>into surrender. The Japanesd would be vastly more numerous than the Turks, and they could not be starved. Under such conditions the war might go on for several campaigns, neither side achieving a decisive victory. On the other hand, apart from financial considerations, neither combatant could sustain the enormous waste of men without some compromise. It is believed that the losses at Liao-yang alone have already equalled tha losses in some of the bloodiest battles of the Napoleonic wars. When this process of exhaustion has lastei a little longer, there should be a chance for mediation, on the plea that Russia must recognise some limitation of what she is pleased to call her " historic destiny" in the Far East.</p>
        <p>Judging by the amount of special business before the Town Council on Tuesday, some of the members must have been thinking hard during the holiday month. Three hours were required in order to get through the agenda and most of the time was taken up in discussing special motions. These resulted in the George Hotel licence being sold to Messrs. Peter Walker and Sons, and a number of Trade Union clausss added to the standing orders governing the letting of the '...-lor- poration contracts. A motion by Councillor Dodds aflecting the payment of costs on a land purchase was closured by the Deputy Mayor after an acri- monious debate.</p>
        <p>The Paving and Sewerage Committee is on its mettle—not its road-metal—and seems inclined to mend its ways both metaphorically and actu- ally. For weeks past the committee has been undergoing a castigation in the Press and elsewhere owing to the number of unpaved streets and pas- sages, and ne Medical Officer's report lent fuel to the fires of criticism. Having taken most of the hard knocks lying down the com- mittee seems to have had enough, and on Tuesday the chairman, Councillor Tinnion,, hit back with vigour and precision in several directions. First of all he tackled the Medical Officer's report. "Exaggeration of language and mis-statement of facts" wss the chief count in the indictment, and under this head the Paviur-in- Chief made out a strong case. Children are not,as a regular thing, "trudging to school through slush and pools of water," and apparently the Doctor has allowed his pen to momentarily outstrip his judgment. But if the com- ment does exaggerate the true condition of affairs, there is sufficient that is wrong to justify very considerably the Doctor's diatribe, and even the Borough Surveyor's report does not absolve the committee from a charge of dilatoriness extend- ing over some years. It may be the fact, also, as Councillor Tinnion argued, that the unsatisfactory health of the town is not consequent upon the backwardness of street paving, but it cannot be contended that this is not a very probable contri- butory cause. Sanitation cannot be perfect under conditions which prevail in most parts of the six miles of streets and passages which are yet unpaved' and we would urge the committee to put forth extraordinary efforts to get more nearly level with the work before it.</p>
        <p>Alderman Sutton made a suggestion, and it was favourably received by Councillor Tinnion, which we venture to think was far from being wise. In effect it amounts to a revision of tha Medical Officer's report by the committees interested be- fore it is issued to the public. Doubtless such a course would avoid many subsequent heartburnings but what the public has a right to expect is the candid opinion of the Medical Officer as a respon- sible official. If the opinion is found fault with in some quarters, no great harm is done. Knuckles have to be rapped occasionally and the ratepayers will rely on the medical officer giving them the benefit of his honest opinion and judgment. We have no doubt that he will be pleased to co-operate with the committees of the Corporation but we hope he will resist any sug- gested modification of his views. The plain unvarnished truth is preferable to any colourless imitation.</p>
        <p>The Corporation is not going to emerge from its latest licensing deal with very much credit. After soliciting offers for the George Hotel license and accepting that of Messrs. Peter Walker and Sons. Ltd., the Finance Committee changed its mind and decided to wait and see what the Government's Licensing Bill would bring forth in the way of compensation. There was some con- sistency in this course seeing that purely mercenary considerations had weighed with the committee from the commencement. In order to be equally consistent and not too humble, Messrs. Peter Walker and Sons, Ltd., withdrew their tender. That was the position of affairs when the Council met on Tuesday, and the minutes of the Finance Committee were confirmed. Later in the evening Councillor Maginn moved that the ofler of Messrs. Walker be accepted, quite ignoring the fact that it had been withdrawn. The Council also shut the other eye and by a large majority passed the motion. Exactly how small and silly the mem- bers will feel if Messrs. Walker still keep their offer in their pockets we cannot imagine, but we hope we shall have an opportunity of seeing. Neither the brewers nor the Corporation can fall on each other's Leeks at this juncture without considerable loss of dignity. The licence had better be allowed to lapse, It is not required in the old neighbourhood, and to allow a new district to be exploited in order that the Corporation may pocket £2,550 will be a disgrace to the magistrates and all concerned in the government of the town.</p>
        <p>Councillor Stott seems to do good and bad work on the Town Council alternately. The mistake he makes is in not fully appreciating the fact that in addition to being the workingman's representative he is, along with his fellow councillors, a large employer of labour. Questions affecting master and man must be considered from the point of view of each, and up to now Councillor Stott has concerned himself solely with the workman's ease. On Tuesdsy he inquired closely into the reasons for the disinissalof an assistant stoker at Winwick and suggested his reinstatement. Could anything be more likely to undermine the authority of the Borough officials over their employs than this making an appeal court of the Council Chamber? Councillor Stott is entitle,' to his information, but he should never have asked for it publicly.</p>
        <p>By the introduction of his omnibus motion relating to the letting of contracts by the Corpora- tion he has done excellent service for the Trade Unionist cause. Previously the standing order governing the relations between the Corporation and contractors stipulated for a "fair wage" being paid ; now the Trade Union rate of wages for the district concerned is an invariable condition. The difference is probably more apparent than real in so far as it concerns the majority of contractors, I but Trade Unionists are now assured that there is less possibility than ever of any sweated labour , being paid for by the Corporatien.One clause,No. 6, was rejected, and we think there is reason in refusing to sanction a penalty of five years' prohi- bition from tendering being impmsed on every contractor who may even unwittingly make a breach of his agreement regarding the conditions of labour in his employ. The clause might be mcdified so that the Council would have some option in the matter. In other respects Coun- cillor Stott's motion will make for fairer dealing between employer and employed.</p>
        <p>A lot of time was wasted and not much infor- mation imparted in the discussion mlll3h followed Councillor Dodds' motion re the payment of costs to the executors of the late Mr. John Hartley. The resolution on the minutes that" 2() for costs be paid," etc., has certainly never been carried out. If the word " costs " should have been " commission " it is a pity the error was not acknowledged, and particulars given as to who re- ceived the commission. The transaction was pro- bably bona fide at the time, all the trouble arising from an erroneous wording of the resolution on the minutes. Either the terms of the resolution must be altered or another £2O paid by the Co- poration "to the_ executors of :the late John Hartley."</p>
        <p>So frequently has the pollution of Sankey Brook been discussed that one would think the topic was worn threadbare. Unfortunately there has been an abundance of argument, but, up to the present: no improvement in the condition of the foul stream. Now there seems to be a probability of the Rural District Council, as one of the offending bodies, taking determined steps. The Council is wisely acting upon the principle of sweeping its own doorstep before pointing out the uncleanliness of its neighbours. The Sur- veyor has prepared an important scheme for the treatment of sewage before it enters the brook, and it now rests with the Council to give authority for the execution of his plans. Of course there are difficulties to be surmounted, but wile° the work is done the Council win, in the words of the Clerk, have clean hands. Even then the nuisance will not by any means net abated, for the Council is responsible for only 9, small part of the pollution. Before any satisfac- tory result is arrived at, joint action by the neigh" bouring aetherities will be necessary. The rtis- trict Council will, if they remain firm to their intentions, be free from reproach.</p>
        <p>The Revising Barrister has been sitting in War: rington during the past week hearing claims and objections. The list in course of revision is all important one, for it is probable that the next Parliamentary election will be fought upon it. The political agents have been ine,re than usually alert and some very fine points na registration law have been brought before the Revising Barrister for decision. The lodger has again fared badly. The rule that he must have two rooms for his exclusive use has proved a stumbling- block in many instances. In the case of a sort living with his kinsfolk this is well-nigh impossible for it is hardly natural to expect him to live like a hermit, apart from the rest.of the family. The further stipulation that the premises occupied must be of not less value thau £lO per annum has been equally fatal to the claims of other lodgers with aspirations to became electors. The difficulties which beset even a householder from maintaining his position on the burgess roll are so many and intricate as to call for an early reform of the registration laws. was the late Mr. IVlcMinnies who once remarked that it ought to be as easy to get t oa the voters list as on the rate book: Under the existing regulations it seems much more easy to. keep a man off the register than to get him on,and until we have something approachini, manhood suffrage the present disqual:fications respecting continuity of tenure, property qualification, and other irritatieg conditions will continue to proVe a bone of contention in the Revision Courts.</p>
        <p>The Master of the Workhouse (Mr. A. F: Awbery) has shown a commendaNe thoughtful- ness for the future weltare of children born in the Workhouse by pointing out the disadvantages le after life which attaches to them owing to their birthplace being stamped on their birth certificate. To avoid this he has suggested that the permissine of the Registrar General be obtained to have the Workhouse numbered and known as 99; Guardian- street so that no child in its progress through life need feel the handicapping effects of the birth certificate as now issued: The Board of Guardiaee has wisely decided to act upon the sug- gestion. The suggestion is not entirely ee original one, for we believe similar steps lisle been taken by other poor law authorities, bat gr• Awbery deserves credit for bringing the matter before the Guardians. It is one more step towards humanising the poor laws and removing the badge of pauperism which has so long attached to anyone who has been obliged to accept the hospitalitY offered by the Board on behalf of the ratepaYers-</p>
        <p>Index to Inside Pages. PAGE. 2. Town Council: Street Paving; Trade Utile° Wages Clause. Warrington Rural Council and Sankey Brook. Penketh News. Warrington Board of Guardians. 3. Events of the Week (Illustrated.) Football and Cricket. Sankey Council and the Brook. Railway Mystery at Lymm. Motor Notes. 6. Police News. A Clerk's Tragic End. Runcorn Rural Council and Board of Guardians. The St. Helens Mystery. 7. Trades Union Congress. Women's Chat and Fashions. Billiards,</p>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>The Gymnasium. The classes at the Borough Gymnasium will re-01)el/ on Monday.</p>
        <p>Volunteer Long Service Medals. Long service medals have been awarded to Colour' Sergeants G. Bailey and W. Spencer, and Quarter- Master Sergeant J. Wilcock,of the Ist V.B. South Lancashire Regiment. Local Choir at the National Eisteddfod. At the Welsh National Eisteddfod on Wednesday the newly formed West Lancashire choir which includee members of the Warrington Apollo Male Choir), cora' peted in the chief choral contest, under the conductor' ship of Mr. Harry Berrey. They were unsuccessful,t.he North Staffordshire choir being first and the Rhondda choir second. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KING or TONICS.-63, SANKEY-STREET, WarritlgtOß.—Advt. Music in the Park.</p>
        <p>The Borough Brass Band will play the following programme of music in the park on Sunday " underiey " ; march, " Ravenswood " W. 'Zimmer overture, " Poet and Peasant" Suppe ; selection, "D°l;i Sebastiano " Donizetti • selection, Hymn of Praised Mendelssohn ; "Hallelujah Chorus," Handel ; "G° save the King." Conductor, Mr. T. Griffin.</p>
        <p>ASK for CUN. NDIGHAM'S HIGH-CLASb WATERS.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Garden Fete at Padgate. In the pretty vicarage grounds at Padgate on Thal. day,the annual garden fete and sale of work in aid various organizations organizations and funds connected wit' Christ Church were held. Mrs. Lyon, of ApPlet°,3 Hall, performed the opening ceremony. Althour the day opened threateningly the weather was fine In the afternoon and the event was attended with succell" There were numerous attractions, and Prof. G. Maidment gave ventriloquial entertainments. lue proceeds amounted to about £6O. Death of Mr. T. Moss. um 1 We regret to announce the death of a well-kaowu and highly respected townsman, viz., tr. Thomas Moss a member of the firm of Messrs. Stepheii 31°8,5, and Sons, Academy-street, Warrington, which tout; , place at his residence, Knutsford-road, shortly afte 12 noon on Thursday after a somewhat protractecri illness. His unassuming and genial manner, won fed him the esteem and respect of all who knew him, nil he will be (Tread • y missed by a wide circle He leaves a widow and four daughters. The funer will take place at the Warrington Cemetery, 3t o'clock this afternoon. PUBLIC BENEFIT? UNDER the CLOCK for B°.'t BOOTSiII LANCASHIRE.—Advt. of friend.si</p>
