        <p>It does not seem to be PROSPECTS OF a very favourable moment</p>
        <p>INTERVENTION. to talk of intervention,</p>
        <p>but there are some hope- ful spirits in Paris who think the:time has come for the neutral Powers to offer their good offices for mediation. The GERMAN EMPEROR, it is sug7 gested, might give a friendly hint to the CZAR' We should say be was the last person to do anything of the kind. Russia I would fiercely resent any hint from any quarter, however friendly, that she is unequal to the task which she undertook with such a light heart. The victorious Japanese can- not be expected to listen to any peace proposals unless they come first from St. Petersburg. They did not go to war with a light heart, and they are not by any means intoxicated by their successes. The NIIKADO'S felici tations:to his armies in Manchuria are couched in a very sober vein. He says the end of the war is "still far distant." There has :never been any im- petuous assumption in the zalculations of Japan. From the outset she was prepared for costly and prolonged sacrifices. At the Japanese Legation in Paris it is calmly taken for granted that the war will last at least three years. Financial provision has been made for that con- tingency. As for the supply of men, Japan will have ample time to train 3nough recruits to keep half a million troops in the field. They may not be equal in point of number to the legions Russia may eventually send to Manchuria; but the qualities which the Japanese soldiers have already shewn in successful attack are likely to prove still more formidable when Marshal OYA3II adopts a defensive strategy to hold the positions he has won from the retreating enemy. It is improbable that General KITROPATKIN will attempt to make any stand at Mukclen ; but he may concentrate his forces at Tieling, forty miles further north. Or he may carry out at last what is said to have been his origir.al plan by retiring to Harbin. It is a very long way, and grave mishaps may happen on the road. But, assuming that KUROPATKIN makes good his retreat, or what he prefers to call his northerly " advance " to the ultimate base of ithe Russian military strength in Manchuria, ' there to await the precious reinforcements which come so slowly, it is conceivable that the Japanese may take up a definite position where they will defy the whole might of Russia to drive them out. Their communications with the sea would be ensured, and they would hold a Plevna or series of Plevnas with advantages which never belonged to Osmex PASHA. That commander, with 80,000 men, held Plevna for four months against the Russian armies, and was eventually starved</p>
      </div>
