      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <p>Warrington P.S.A. Mr. E. England presided at the meeting held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Sunday. Mr. E. Lloyd Jones gave an address on " Courage," which he divided into three parts, viz., "moral courage, courate of resistance, and courage of endurance."</p>
        <p>The Ostend Sensation. The report last week that Louisa Kindon, daughter of Mrs. Kindon, 22, Sharp street, Warrington, com- mitted suicide by throwing herself into the sea at Ostend is now confirmed. Mr. Ernest Kindon,brother of the deceased lady, returned to Warrington on Saturday night after making inquiries at Ostend. He found the news was perfectly correct, and the funeral had taken place when he arrived there. He was un- able to discover any trouble which led to deceased's tragic end, but it is now stated that Miss Kindon fell from a Manchester tram last Christmas, hurt her ankles, and was told by doctors she would always be nme. The Pearson &amp; Knowles Coal &amp; Iron Co., Ltd. The Directors have decided subject to a final audit to recommend the payment of the usual half year's dividend at the rate of 6 per cent. per annum on the Preference shares, also a half year's dividend at the rate of 4 per cent. per annum, free of income tax, on the Ordinary Shares. They also recommend that the increased capital expenditure of the year be written Off out of profits, that £lO,OOO be added to the Reserve Fund, and the balance £6,455 Os. 10d. carried to the next account.</p>
        <p>The Grand Liberal Bazaar. Preparations for the grand Liberal Bazaar "The British Isles," are proceeding apace, and when it Opens on October sth there should be nothing lacking to charm the eye, the ear, and the pocket. Nine large stalls will display some of the most beautiful goods ever collected together for a Warrington bazaar, and the other attractions promise to be amazing in their variety and excellence. The cream of the - vocal and instrumental talent in Warrington has already been secured for chamber concerts, and something novel iii the way Of side-shows is promised. Further particulars will be divulged later. • Election of a Guardian. Polling will take place on Montly for a representa- tive on the Warrington Board of Guardians to fill the vacancy caused by the death of Mr. J. Clarke, of Earlestown, who represented Viaduct Ward. There are three candidates for the ppsition, namely: Mr. Astley Sing, ironmonger, 31 and 33, Queen-street, Earlestown ; Mr. Matthew William Seton, commission agent, 98, Market-street,Earlestown ; and Mr. Nathaniel Siddeiey, gentleman, of Highbury House, Wargrave- Toad, Newton-le-Willows. The polling station will be at the District schools, Market-street, Earlestown, and it will be open at twelve o'clock noon and close at eight o'clock. The result of the poll will be declared the same evening. Mr. S. Sutton is the returning officer.</p>
        <p>Warrington Poachers Captured. About three o'clock yesterday morning Samuel Watkins and James Burrows, gamekeepers on Lord Newton's estate, were in the neighbourhood of Rob- lane, Newton, when they espied a gang of night Poachers. The assistance of Sergeant Bancks and constable was obtained, and a search made for the gang. At 4.30 they were traced to Ashton-road, and after a long chase over fields and through woods, Alfred Prance and James Clare, two well-known poachers, of Warrington, were captured, but the other men, numbering about five, made good their escape. On the ground being searched where the men had been seen, no less than 33 dead rabbits were found, and also three long nets, six bag s, and a number of pegs. Later in the day the two prisoners were brought up at Newton Police Court, and remanded until Monday. The Productions of Gartons Limited. t.This enterprising firm of seed growers and merchants have forwarded us a copy of their newly-published autumn catalogue coqtainmg particulars of their new breeds of wheats, as well as other seeds for autumn sowing. The booklet is neatly and attractively pro- duced and agriculturalists would do well to consult it before placing their orders. ,It was in 1880 that Messrs. Gartons commenced experiments With the object of solving the question " Can the quality of English wheats be improved?" The result of their work has been entirely satisfactory, and their breed of wheat "New Era," introduced in the autumn of 1902, is certified to have produced 8 Per cent increase in strength value over several Canadian wheats. The numerous other specialities of the firm have also been a distinct success, as the letters published in the catalogue testify.</p>
        <p>Warrington Polyglot Society. By the kindness of Sir Richard and Lady Brooke, a visit was paid to the gardens and grounds of Norton Priory on Saturday. The doubtful weather probably kept some members away, but those who went,spent a raost delightful afternoon. Under the guidance of Mr. Slater, the courteous head gardener, a visit was first made to the rose garden, the beauty of which, alas, was fast fading.A.fter a glance at the herbaceous borders, rich in the glory of the autumn flowers, the various houses were next visited, containing many splendid examples of fruit, flowers and foliage plants, all testifying to the care and attention of Mr. Slater and his staff • in fact much greater attention than usual must be necessary, when the harmful chemical fumes of Widnes are taken into account. A short .Walk through the ornamental grounds brought a most enjoyable afternoon to a close, and many wishes were expressed that a similar pleasure might be forth- eoming in the distant future. A small party of cyclists iourneyed on to Frodsham, where tea was had, to the accompaniment of the Town band. This is the last excursion of the season, and no doubt these little picnics will stimulate the members to greater exertion in the forthcoming, winter.</p>
        <p>- Kubelik is coming. Kubelik, described as the world's greatest violinist is coming to Warrington at the end of the month. The date is September 30th, and the place the Parr Hall, and it is through the exertions of Mr. R. H. Pearson and Mr. J. P. Johnson, the Kendal impressario, that Nre are to have this treat. This is Kubelik's first tour in the English provinces since he returned to Eng- land after his marriage with the Hungarian countess, and it has been enormously successful. Recently at London the receipts at one concert were £1,200 and at Buxton his concert was the most fashionable ever known in the history of the town, and at Llandudno and other places the same thing has to be said. We do not doubt that Warrington will give Kubelik a Rreat welcome, since it is his first visit to the town. The plan of reserved seats opens at Mr. R. H. Pearson's on Monday morning next. Dearth of County Magistrates.—Case</p>
        <p>Adjourned. At Warrington County Sessions, on Wednesday, Thomas Cook, of Burtonwood, was summoned to answer a charge of alleged adulteration of milk. The Magistrates on the bench were Messrs. G.Comber and it Halton. The defendant did not appear. Mr. Arthur Browne, who appeared to prosecute on behalf of the Warrington Rural District Council, said it placed Mr. Halton, who was chairman of the Council, In an invidious position even if the defendant had appeared. He (Mr. Browne) thought it was another ease of the shortness of magistrates, by which Lord berby might be impressed with the necessity for appointing more magistrates. Mr. Comber remarked That the magistrates had done their best. The case 147 as adjourned to the Newton Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>Latchford Baptist Church. Special services were held at this church on Sunday, -in celebration of the Sunday school anniversary. A children's service was held iu the afternoon, presided ever by Mr. S. Albinson. Councillor Brongh gave an .eddress on" The Prodigal Sun," and music wits con- tributed by the children. The preacher at the morning and evening services was the Re,v. J. F. Matthews, who began the eighth year of his ministry. The morning service was well attended, and in the evening the church Was full. A notable feature of the congregation was the Presence of a large number of young men.-4nst before Ihe sermon on Sunday evening Mr. Matthews said: start to-day my eighth year as minister of this church. On a rough calculation and an anproximately accurate 2ne, I have made during the past seven years 'earlV 5,000 visits. The prospects for the future ke bright with hope and rich in promise. I 4111 grateful for the 'large congregations which attend so regularly, and for the number of 'nung men associated with us; men who take an Intelligent interest both in literiture and life. This %11 not but have a good effect bath upon the church Pld minister. Of course, the success of the work here Le ?ours. A hardworking church is naturally both a 14.,ealthy and a happy one. Moreover, lam glad to believe at a warm affection unites minister and people. It is .9nr common desire that the endeavour societies, the Institute (which has proved valuable and useful in this neighbourhood), the week evening service, and the de- ufttlng society, shall all reach a high state of efficiency, 411(1 make a great move forward during the coming year. must never be forgotten, however, the main purpose 4or Which we exist. We are here to invite men into r"Kingdom of God, to make known His wise and . Kingdom rule, and to proclaim the gospel of Christ's 'qemptive sacrifice.</p>
      </div>
