      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <p>Personal. The Rev. A. Inglis, until recently curate at Holy Trinity Church, has entered upon his new duties at Winwick. The Rev. R. S. INloxon, of the Grammar School, is giving temporary help at Holy Trinity, pending the appointment of a successor to Mr. Inglis. Miss Ellen V. Roddy, daughter of Mr. Charles Roddy, of the Bird in Hand public house, Bank-street, has succeeded in obtaining a double first in mathe- inatics and languages in the examination held in July last at the Mount Pleasant Training College, Liver- pool. She was educated at Blackburn and carried off the chief prizes at the Peel Institute two years in succession.</p>
        <p>FOR CLA.SS AND CHINA of every description try J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street. Warrington.—Advt. Wycliffe P.S.A. At the first meeting of the new half-year held on Sunday, 10 new members were enrolled. Mr. B. Farrar presided and referred to the progress of the society since its inauguration. The Rev. John Davies was the speaker and Mr. R. Wood the soloist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>BEST BOOTS FOR ALL.—UNDER THE CLOCK.—Advt</head>
        <p>BEST BOOTS FOR ALL.—UNDER THE CLOCK.—Advt</p>
        <p>Liverpool Cathedral Fund. A. meeting of the executive committee of the Liver- pool Cathedral Fund was held on Monday at the Church House, Liverpool, when it was reported that, following the ceremony of the laying of the founda- tion stone by the King, the funds at the disposal of the committee had been augmented by nearly .i 60,000. Donations of windows and other parts of the fabric had also been offered and accepted. St. Mary's School Scholarships. The Scholarship Committee in connection with St. Mary's School Buttermarket-street, has awarded two scholarships to be held at the Secondary school. They were gained by Joseph Malone of 57, Willis-street, and Thomas G-roarke of 30, Oxford-street, who suc- cessfully passed the public entrance examination and religious examination of the Scholarship. Committee</p>
        <p>ASK for CIYANDIGHAII'S HIGH-CLASS I.4I.INELIA WATERS.—Advt. Presentation to a Works Manager. The foremen at Messrs. Richmond's works invited the works manager, Mr. John F. Davey, to an outing on Saturday. A pleasant drive to Lymm, via High Legh,lioe Green, Bucklow Hill, and Bollington' was followed by tea at the Plough Hotel. The meal over several speeches were made, and Mr. W. Loftus' on behalf of himself and fellow-foremen, presented Mr. Davey with a handsome silver-mounted walking stick, on which was engraved the recipient's monogram and a suitable inscription. Mr. Davey, who has been the manager of the works for about six years, is leaving to take up an appointment at the Carron Works Falkirk.</p>
        <p>UNDER the CLOCK for GOOD BOOTS.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Accident to an Athlete. Charles J. Straw, the well-known long distance runner, met with a nasty accident on Sunday. He was cycling from his home at Winwick to Warring- ton about noon, ancl when near Winwick Church, at the junction of the Is ewton-le-Willows and Lowton- roads, he collided with a motor car belonging to Mr. Jordan, of Sankey-street, Warrington. He was thrown heavily to the ground and sustained injuries Co the head, right leg and shoulder. The front wheel of the bicycle was smashed to pieces. Straw remained unconscious for five hours, but we are pleased to state that he is now progressing satisfactorily. FIRE GRATES of every description.—J. S. GAR LAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warringion.— Advt. Presentation to Mr. F. Atkinson. There was a pleasing ceremony at the rehearsal of the Warrington Male Voice Choir on Monday even- ing. Mr. Hassall on behalf of the members presented Mr. Fred Atkinson, the ex-secretary and accompanist, with a framed group of the choir. He made fitting allusion to the untiring efforts and energy which had been put forward by Mr. Atkinson, to bring about a successful choir. Mr. Atkinson in reply thanked all his friends most ssncerely for the beautiful ctu re He said it would remind him of the many happy hours spent among them. He always took a deep interest in the welfare of the ehoir. His only regret was that he was not able to devote the time to them he had hitherto devoted. He wished all the members every possible success in the future. BASSLNETTES AND MAIL CARTS in latest art shades.—J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Warrington Woman's Suicide. - -</p>
        <p>At the—Chapel House Inn, Burtonwood, on Wednes- day afternoon, Coroner Brighouse held an inquest touching the death of Lilian Leonardy (22), 18, Appleton-street, Warrington, whose body was re- covered from the St. Helens and Sankey Canal at Bewsey on Tuesday.—Patrick Leonardy, deceased's husband, said that deceased had for some time been suffering from gathered breasts, and on :tlonday night complained that the pain was very acute. When he awoke on Tuesday morning he found she was not in bed and went in search of her along the canal bank. He saw her shawl and cape floating in the canal and informed the police. There had been no trouble between deceased and himself, and he had behaved well to her ever since their marriage. He had been out of work a great deal.—Betsi'Spruce, deceased's mother, said deceased had always been well treated by her husband.—P.C. Dtigdale said he recovered the body from the canal. There were no marks of violence.—A verdict of "Suicide whilst of unsound mind" was returned. LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS and SHOES, UNDER the CLOCK. BRID GE-STREET.—Adr‘ The Late Mr. J. Shaw Green.</p>
        <p>A memorial of exceptional interest was to be seen ,in Silver-street school on Sunday, in the form of a framed portrait of the late Mr. J. Shaw Green, J.P. The idea originated in the minds of two of the young men of the school to perpetuate the memory of one, who it may be said, was beloved by all who knew him best. It will be remembered that Mr. Shaw Green passed away on October 29th of last year. Early this year an opportunity was afforded every member of the school to take part in the memorial by means of a col- leation,of which the above is the happy result. At the close of the afternoon schnol, Mr. J. W. Hayward, J.P. (the present senior superintendent), made fitting refer- ence to his departed colleague's life and work. Mr. Shaw Green's connection with Silver-street school, he said, extended over a. period of 27 years, and in that time he became endeared to every member of the school and was an inspiring example in bright Christian service. He sought in serving God to serve his fellows, as was evidenced by his extensive public work in the town. Mr Hayward in concluding, hoped that the portrait now to be before them Sunday by Sunday would tend to inspire all who worked in the school. It may be added that the portrait is a most excellent one, and bears at the foot the following suitable inscription :--" In loving memory of the late J. Shaw Green, Esq., J.P., for 11 years superintendent of the Silver-street Wesleyan School and leader of the Men's Bible Class." H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KING Or TONICS. 63, SANKEY-STREET, Warrinaton.—Advt. Death of a Former Warrington Journalist.</p>
        <p>Many Warrington people will regret to hear of the death of Mr. Sam Harrison, one of the senior members of the reporting staff of the "Liverpool Mercury" and formerly connected with the " Warrington Examiner." Less than a fortnight ago, Mr. Harrison left his duties to enjoy a short holiday. Unfortunately he contracted pneumonia, to which he succumbed, at his residence, in Seacombe, on Sunday afternoon, at the compara- tively early age of foi ty-one. A nativ9 of Yorkshire, Mr. Harrison commenced his journalistic career by going out to South Africa. During the troublous - times of the Zulu war he exnerien:ed many thrilling adventures, and one of his smartest achievements was an interview which he secured with Cetywayo on behalf of the Cape paper which he repre- sented. After the -war was over Mr. Harrison returned to this country and joined the reporting staff of this journal. He followed the initiation and progress of the Manchester Ship Canal works throughout, and his descriptive articles dealing with that gigantic under- taking were widely read. On the completion or the works Mr. Harrison proceeded to Liverpool, and re- mained on the " Mercury " until the time of his demise.—ln private life he was one of the most amiable of men, and his quiet courtesy and thoughtfulness for others endeared him to a wide circle of friends. His untimely death will be deeply deplored not only by his colleagnes, to whom he was greatly attached, but among many other sections of the community. Mr. Harrison leaves a widow and one daughter. The funeral took place on Wednesday at Wallasey. Cemetery. The service in the church and at the grave- side was conducted by the Rev. W. Eaton Thomas, of the Congregational Church, Seacombe. The Chief mourners were Mrs. Harrison (widow), Miss Edie Harrison (daughter), Messrs. John and Tom Harrison (brothers), Miss Harrison (sister), Mrs John Naylor and Mrs Robert Widdop (sisters); Mr. ,Robert 'Widdop Mrs. Johnson, and Mr. H. Isherwood (" Warrington Observer.") The general mourners included a large number of Pressmen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, September 10, 1904.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, September 10, 1904.</p>
        <p>A PROPERTY PURCHASING TRANSACTION.</p>
        <p>QUESTIONS BY COUNCILLOR DODDS.</p>
        <p>Warm Discussion.</p>
        <p>Some considerable time was spent by the members of the Corporation at the Town Council meeting on Tues- day evening in discussing a motion submitted by Councillor Dodds, having reference to the purchase of property on Wilderspool-causeway and River-road. At the outset, Councillor DODDS asked the following questions of the Chairman of the Street Improvement and Lighting Committee :—(1) Is the following a correct copy of a resolution or minute of the committee passed on the 28th April last, namely, "That the sum of £l,OBO and £2O for costs be paid to the executors of the late John Hartley for the purchase of property in Wilderspool Causeway and River-road." (2) Has the "£2O for costs" been paid to the executors of the late John Hartley in harmony with the minute quoted above and if not, why not? (3) If the £2O has not been paid to the executors of the late John Hartley, has it been paid to any other person or persons, and if so,to whom? Alderman SUTTON, in reEply, said there was no objec- tion to do so, although he did not think Councillor Dodds would be any wiser after the questions had been answered. Councillor Dodds had already had the fullest information about the matter, and knew all about it. The answer to question 1 was" Yes." With reference to question 2, the answer was that £2O for costs had been paid. Costs were never paid to the vendor person- ally, but to his solicitor or representative. With reference to question 3, he was informed that Mrs. Hartley had personally received £6 6s. Bd. out of the £2O which was paid to Messrs. J. Shaw Green and Co. Councillor DODDS moved the following motion : "That the arrangement set forth in a minute of the Street Improvement and Lightinc, Committee, dated the 28th April last, and under which £2O for costs•was to be paid to the executors of the late John Hartley in connection with the purchase of property in Wilders- pool Causeway and River-road by the Corporation, be forthwith carried out." He was very sorry, he said, to be in conflict with their chief official in regard to this matter. Alderman Sutton said that he (Councillor Dodds) had seen Mr. Whittle in regard to this matter but the explanation and information which Mr. Whittle gave was so contrary to that he had put before him by the executors of the late Mr. John Hartley that he felt bound in some way to bring it again forward, and they asked him to do so. It seemed to him that the motion was clear enough in its terms. The resolution passed by the Town Council in April last said distinctly that £l,OBO and £2O costs should be paid to the executors of the late John Hartley. His point was that the costs had not been paid to the executors and that the resolution, had not been carried outin accordance with the decision of the committee and the Council which confirmed the action of the committee. Mr. Sutton said that the costs were never paid to the vendors personally. Then why was the resolution drawn up in that way? The DEPUTY MAYOR: Because the committee don't understand it.</p>
        <p>Councillor DODDS (continuing) said the vendors all along asked for £l,lOO for the property. The repre- sentatives of the Corporation went to the vendors and said they could not get £l,lOO. The executors said they wouldnot sell the property at less than that figure. The representatives of the Corporation returned with an offer and said "We won't give you that but we will give F you £l,OBO and transfer the property without any charge." The executors replied "No. We want our own solicitor to do the work and we want £l,lOO to cover the costs." The representative of the Corpora- tion then came back and said, "We are prepared to give, £l,OBO and £2O to pay the costs." The vendors dis- tinctly held out that they would have their work done by their bwn solicitors and by nobody else. They insisted upon it. The Town Clerk was bound to act for the Corporation. That was the undertaking they had, and the resolution bore out what they said. When the order was signed the Borough Treasurer was ordered to pay £l,OBO to the vendors and £2O to some other firm. He did not want to say who it was. He wanted to say the officials did not carry out the instructions of the committee when they paid that £2O to Mr. Shaw Green's company or whoever it was. He only asked that the resolution of the Council that £2O costs should be paid to thee executors to pay their own solicitors, should be carried out. He knew what would be said on behalf of the Corporation. Alderman SUTTON: I don't think you ought to anticipate what will be said. Councillor DoDDS : My only object is to save time The DEPUTY-MAYOR: Why don't the lawyers get the money back again. The Corporation have paid £llOO for the property. Councillor DODDS (proceeding) observed that Alder- man Sutton had said in his reply to the questions sub- mitted that the executors had got one-third of the £2O. That was not the point at all. His point was that the Corporation had passed a resolution that £2O should be' paid to the executors for their costs and the Corpora- tion had not paid it. They had paid £2O to someone else. The Town Clerk told him that there was a mis- take in the wording of the resolution. It should be £2O commission not costs.</p>
        <p>The DEPUTY AIAYOIL : The Corporation have paid £2O.</p>
        <p>Councillor DODDS : They have agreed to pay £2O to the costs of the other parties. Continuing, he said he had no feeling in the matter personally. He only desired to get at the truth. The DEPUTY MA.YOR: You must let the rown Clerk explain. Councillor DODDS: I must say the case as put to me has been consistent and in harmony entirely with the resolution of the Corporation. The payment has not been made in accordance with the resolution.</p>
        <p>The TOWN CLERK: In the negotiations I agreed to pay Mrs. Hartley £l,OBO for herproperty. The committee said they would not go higher than that. I was asked what would be the costs and commission. Mrs. Hartley knew nothing of the £2O. We agreed on £l,OBO and she agreed to accept that and that sum was paid. I deny her statement absolutely. Councillor PEMBERTON seconded the motion. He did not believe the Corporation would be a party, he said to that whiCh was in the least shady. Councillor WILKINSON: " Shady ? " Councillor PEMBERTON : On the face of it it looks rather shady when an Alderman of the Borough was the man who got part of the £2O. The TOWN CLERK: Alderman Shaw Green was dead at the time. Councillor WILKINSON: Do reverence the dead for the Lord's sake. It is becoming cruel. Say John Chappell and you are right. Councillor PEMBERTON : It was a mistake in the name of the company. It says J. Shaw Green and Company. I wasn't aware of the change: lam very sorry. It was a very natural mistake. The TOWN CLERK: Withdraw the word" shady." Councillor PEMBERTON : No not quite. This resolu- tion distinctly says £2O costs had to be paid to the executors.</p>
        <p>Alderman 6UTTON : You must not make accusations. Councillor PEMBERTON : Ifyou cannot take the wording of the resolution what lathe name of goodness can you take. These people wanted £l,lOO for the property. The TOWN CLERK: They never asked for it. Councillor FORSHAW (producing the bill of costs) : Here is proof that £l,OBO was agreed to. It says that distinctly here. COURCinOr PEMBERTON : There is a sum of t2O in addition to be paid to the executors of John Hartley. A VOICE : " Notno." The TOWN CLERK : If I had to word it again it would be more explicit. Costs are never paid to the vendor. Councillor DODDS : Why did you put it in the reso- lution ? _ _</p>
        <p>- Councillor PEMBERTON : Why is this resolution drawn up differently from others. We must assume there was some special reason. The TOWN CLERK: It is exactly like others. Councillor PEMBERTON : Name a specific sum to be paid to a eartain party. I understand this lady's solicitors were the people to whom this money should have been paid direct. The TOWN CLERK: We knew nothing about it, nor did the solicitors know anything about it. Councillor PEMBERTON : Well it ought to have been paid to the executors and no one else. The TOWNCLERK : Here is a document relating to the purchase of property stating that the sum of £450 and £l2 costs be paid. It is the usual form. Councillor WILKINSON : Let ushave the name of the property. The TOWN CLERK: The School Brow property. Councillor WILKINSON observed that it had been amusing to see how educated men would listen to scandal and cant and old women's tales. (Laughter.) Mr. Doddshad listened to an old lady—(a laugh)—and in the words of the poet she said" Listen and I will enchant thine ear." (Laughter.) He wished he was a Dickens and he would be a little more pathetic. The fact of the matter was the Corporation wanted to acquire certain property in River-road. A certain gentle- man was approached and told to acquire the property and he would get his costs. Mrs. Hartley agreed to</p>
        <p>accept £l,OBO and there was a bill of costs to prove it. Mrs. Hartley was quite satisfied with the pricepaid. The wording of the resolution was only a matter of book- keeping. (A Voice: "IN,Tonsense.") There was nothing shady about it. It was the construction of the mind which studied what was shady. • They as business men knew that everything was honest, square and above boarsl. Councillor SToTT : In regard to this transaction may I ask the Town Clerk whether the executors expected to receive the full £l,lOO. The TOWN CLERK: Mrs. Hartley knew nothing whatever about it until the transaction was completed. Councillor FORSHAW said the account of costs had been sent in in the usual way and there it was. It was complete answer to Mr. Dodds. The account stated the transaction as follows: "1904, April 27, purchase money £‘080." The Bill went on to make the ordinary apportioAlents. £1084145. 10d. was paid to the executors on the completion of the transaction, which was the usual way. Councillor DODDS asked Councillor Forshaw if a lawyer put particulars of costs on accounts or did he send separate accounts. Councillor FORSHAW : Sometimes he sends in separate bills and sometimes he puts on the commission account for the account to be paid over. Councillor DODDS : Your argument 'proves nothing because it is sometimes done one way and sometimes another.</p>
        <p>Councillor FORSHAW : It is something because you are distinctly told by the solicitors what was the exact amount to be paid for the property. Councillor MAGIN!s: said there was no mystery about the affair at all. Tt looked like one of the weakest things he ever saw a man of business ability bring forwardit was a shame that a member of the Corporation should bring such an accusation of dishonesty and hanky- panky against the Council. Alderman SUTTON said he looked upon the matter as one of the lamest and most absurd questions that had I ever been brought forward by a member of the Council. The whole thing was so plain that he could not conceive how it was possible for an intelligent man to open the question. In wording resolutions they were not supposed to word them in order that school children might under- stand them. Any man of experience ought to under- stand exactly what it meant. There was often too much a desire on the part of members of the Council to try to bring someone into trouble or a difficulty. It was all very well for Councillor Pemberton to come forward and say that everything was shady. Jouncillor PEMBERTON : I didn't say everything. Alderman SUTTON: Well you mentioned the word shady. • Councillor PEMBERTON : Yes for this specific thing. Alderman SUTTON: I say such a charge on your part is a reflection on the committee,and I say it is unworthy of anybody. The Alderman proceeded to read a letter from Mr. J. Green Chappell in which the writeestated that he had received a letter claiming £2O as costs. He had paid a third of the money to Mrs. Hartley. He courted a full inquiry into his conduct of the negotia- tions with regard to the transaction. Alderman Sutton added that the whole of the circumstances showed that there had been no deception or intention to deceive. Councillor TINNION said Councillor Dodds had not convinced him that the resolution had not been carried out</p>
        <p>Councillor DODDS : I never could. Councillor TINNION : I don't know what you mean. I suppose it is a kind of personal reply to those who differ from you. Councillot Tinnion proceeded to give a resume of the negotiations, and contended that in the first instance Mrs. Hartley *as satisfied with the bar- gain. Councillor LORD, when commencing to speak, was interrupted by the DEPUTY-MAYOR. lie retorted: "I don't talk as much as you do." Proceeding, Councillor LORD asked who Mr. Chappell represented in the trans- action? Alderman SUTTON: He represents a party to the transaction. Councillor DODDS : Councillor Wilkinson said Mr. Chappell was sent to buy this property, and was told that he woula get his commission paid. Councillor LORD: If he was sent the Corporation should pay him. Councillor WILKINSON pointed out that in all pur- chases of property by the Corporation they always paid the purchase money over,and attended to the legal part of it themselves. Councillor DODDS said he had not allowed any feel- ing to move him in the matter, but he was asked to bring it forward. He was perfectly prepared to stand by what he had said to the letter. Mrs. Hartley had had to pay her lawyer's expenses out of The £l,OBO paid by the Corporation for the property. Alderman Sutton said she had to have that amount clear. Alderman SUTTON: You make a statement, and I say it is altogether incorrect. (Cries of "Sit down.") Alderman St—rroN : No I won't sit down. Mrs. Hartley could have had the deeds drawn up for nothing by the Town Clerk, but she preferred independently to go to her solicitor. The TOWN CLERK: I didn't offer to do it. I was asked to do it. Councillor DoDns : I am smking of the facts as they were put to me. I believe the case put to me reason- able and accurate. The resolution agrees entirely with the case as put to me. There has been no hesitation in the story of the executors. You are to pay £l,OBO and £2O costs as appears in the resolution. I want you for your own sake to carry out the resolution. Councillor WILKINSON: You know it is only a matter of book-keeping. You know it is well as we do. The Tows CLERK: I make a flat denial to the statement of Councillor 'Dodds respecting the .C2O costs. The DEPUTY MAYOR was about to put the resolution to the vote when Councillor LORD objected to his ruling, as Councillor Dodds was desirous of continuing the discussion. The DEPUTY MAYOR (warmly) : You may object. You have spoken to me as I won't be spoken to by any member of the Council. The vote was then taken. 'Councillors Dodds and Pemberton were the only persons who voted in favour of it and the Deputy Mayor declared the resolution lost. The Council then rose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
