      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>FORTHCOMING OPERA BY MEMBERS OF THE MUSICAL SOCIEfi:</head>
        <p>THE FORTHCOMING OPERA BY MEMBERS OF THE MUSICAL SOCIEfi:</p>
        <p>"The Gondoliers."</p>
        <p>• From an announcement to be found in our advertising columns it will be seen that the arrangements for the second opera by members of the Warrington Musical Society are now virtually complete. Messrs. Gilbert and Sullivan's vivacious collaboration "The Gondoliers" is to be performed in the Parr Hall on five nights com- mencing the 18th of October; theylan of reserved seats, in charge of Messrs. R. H. Pearson Ltd., being announced to open on Wednesday October sth: By those familiar with the Gilbert-Sullivan series of operas it will be remembered that "The Gondoliers," while characterised by refreshing brightness and liveli- ness is peculiarly also a spectacular work. The action taking place on the Venetian piazzetta and in the brilliant court of that happy Barataria which many have doubtless longed to discover,is unflagging in the energy of its movement and in its merrymaking. A feature of the opera is,of course, the performance of the choral dance" The Cachucha," and as almost the entire com- pany of principals and chorus will, we understand, be engaged in it, a most fascinating scene may be looked forward to. The drilling of principals, chorus and orchestra is now thoroughly in hand, under the joint tuition of Mr. F. H. Crossley and Mr. E. rhornley- Dodge. Below we are enabled to give a revised list of the dramatis persowc, +MIMMIM</p>
        <p>THE GONDOLfERS. The Duke of Plaza-Toro Mr. C. P. Gough T..ui z Mr. J. E. Hannan Don Alhambra del Bolero Mr. J. W. Bibby Marco Palmieri Mr. J. Rabbitt Giuseppe Palmieri Mr. A. Fieldhouse Antonio , .Mr. W. Moores Francesco Mr. J. Parkinson Giorgio Mr. J. Knowles Annibale ..Mr. J. H. Fisher The Duchess of Plaza-Toro . Miss Emily Jeans Casilda Miss Arline Harrison Gianetta Mrs. Arthur Booth Tessa Miss Henshall Fiametta Vittoria Miss M. W. Collin Giulia) Miss Wilcocks Inez f Recalling the generous measure of support and appre- ciation accorded to the opera venture last year, it is safe to predict crowded houses for the full run of "The Gondoliers," the performances of which can scarcely fail to be less highly meritorious than those of its predecessor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>SAD DEATH OF A CLERK.</head>
        <p>SAD DEATH OF A CLERK.</p>
        <p>Continued from page 6. THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>The funeral took place on Thursday amid many man festations of regret. The remains were interred in Warrington Cemetery, the officiating clergyman being the Rev. G. W. Dickinson, B.A. Amongst those present were Mr. Joseph Henry Houghton and Mr. Frederick Houghton (brothers), Messrs. John. Thomas, James and William Houghton (uncles), Mr. Joseph Wright (uncle), Messrs. Percy Bennett, Herbert Houghton, Harry Wright,Peter Birchall,H.Welch, and H. Walsh (cousins) Messrs. George Johsnon, Joseph Birchall, Pemberton, Thompson, Roberts and Woods. Messrs. Peter Walker and Sons Limited were represented by Messrs. Wilson,Scotson, Wol lacott,Rnbsell and Mackey. The coffin was covered with a beautiful collection of wreaths. Amongst those who had sent floral tributes were "His sorrowing mother' " " Harry and Alice:" "Fred and Alice," "his little darlings, Annie, Lettie and Charlie." There were three wreaths from the firm, namely, the Directors of Peter Walker and Sons Ltd., the office staff, and the brewer and workmen of King, street. A large number of friends gathered at the graveside as a last tribute of respect.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>GEORGE HOTEL LICENCE.</head>
        <p>THE GEORGE HOTEL LICENCE.</p>
        <p>COUNCIL ACCEPT MESSRS. PETER WALKER AND SON'S TENDER. Arguments in Favour of Lapsing.</p>
        <p>The most interesting feature of Tuesday evening's meeting of the Warrington Town Council was a debate with reference to the licence of the George Hotel. At a meeting of the Finance and Estate Committee on August 22nd the following letter from Messrs. Peter Walker and Son, Limited, was read: "The Brewery, Warrington, 10th August. Dear sir,—ln reference to our tender for the George Hotel licence, and the pro- ceedings of the Town Council on Tuesday evening,we have decided under the circumstances to place the withdrawal of our tender in the hands of the Council. —Yours faithfully, PETER WALKER AND SON (Warring- ton and Burton), LIMITED. J. Reid Walker, managing director. J. Lyon Whittle, Esq., town clerk." It was resolved that the consideration of the matter be de- ferred until definite information can be obtained as to how the new Licensing Bill will affect the licence. The DEPUTY-MAYOR moved, and Councillor WILKIN- SON seconded, the confirmation of the minutes. Councillor MAGDIN (who had given notice of his intention to move that the offer of Messrs. Peter Walker and Son, Limited, for the purchase of the licence be accepted) asked how the reference in the minutes would affect his resolution to be submitted at ailater period of the evening. Would it clash with it? The MA.voR : The Town Clerk informs me that if your motion is carried it will supersede the minute altogether. Councillor AIAGINN : It does not block my motion? The MA.YoR. : I don't think so. The minutes were confirmed. _ _ .</p>
        <p>Before Councillor Maginn submitted his resolution the Mayor withdrew from the meeting with the remark that as there had been some trouble about the matter he had better have nothing to do with it. Alderman FRANCOMB proposed that the Deputy-Mayor take the chair. _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor ROGERS seconded, and the motion was carried. _</p>
        <p>Councillor MAGINN then- moved his resolution as follows :—" That the offer of £2,550 made by Messrs. Peter Walker and Son, Limited, for the purchase of the licence of the George Hotel, subject to the conditions named in their letter of the 9th July, be accepted." He said there was no need for him to explain his reason for bringing the motion forward, as all the members of the Council knew the position arrived at a month ago. Nor was it necessary to state that this controversy had caused a great deal of feeling among the ratepayers,who where the people to be considered. The ratepayers were the interested parties either to lose the £2,550 offered. or to gain by the Corporation getting it Therefore they were all the better for havinc, had a month to consider the matter. There had been a great deal of controversy in the Press, as the members were aware, but one of the week-end newspapers stated that two members of the Council had offered £3,000 if the Cor- poration would allow the licences of the Baths Hotel, the Queen's Hotel and the George Hotel to lapse. He had every aduairation for Press criticism but he did not think it was wise to publish an article like that,las he thought it, could not be a fact. He felt certain there could not be. a member of the Council so silly as to offer £2,000 or £3,000 for pro- perty that was worth £25,000 to the town. (Hear, hear.) Surely it was an insult to the town's intelligence as well as to the Council, and if the purpose was to upset the clear issue, if it was a brewer's dodp, a teetotal dodge, or any other dodge he maintained it was an insult to the ratepayers' intelligence to ask the Council to lose £25,000 to oblige any member or any party. By their letter of August 10th, Messrs. Walker's threw the onus on the Council of either accepting their offer as business men, or of making themselves a laughing stock to business men in the North of England. Messrs. Walker's offer was £1,050 over the highest offer. It was a reasonable offer, and it was only common-sense that the Corporation should accept it. From the minutes of the committee he saw it had been resolved that the consideration of the matter had been deferred until definite information could be obtained as to how the new Licensing Act would affect the licence. Was that a commercial action? Was it the a,ction of a commer- cial man who had an article in the public market to sell? He contended that it would not be honourable for the Corporation to refuse Messrs. Walker's offer. It was not worthy of them and that was the reason he submitted his motion, which he hoped the members would see their way to vote for. If the magistrates in their wisdom—for they were a wise body—(laughter)—for some reason refused to trans- fer the licence, the Corporation would still have the licence to deal with. If however, they refused Messrs. Walkers' offer and owing to the new Licensing Act the Corporation got no compensation, they would lose all. Therefore it would be wisdom to accept the bird in the hand. He would say this for the credit of Messrs. Walker, that they were one of the biggest of the rate- payers, if not the biggest, and to allow the Corporation to be put in the mean position of refusing the offer only because it was Messrs. Walker's was unfair. Such insults had previously driven some of our best manu- facturers out of the town, and he would not be sur- prised if what had been done hal a tendency to cause Messrs. Walker to spend as little as possible in im- provements and in the extension of the brewery. Councillor PEMBERTON seconded. While he supported temperance, he did not believe that the ratepayers ought to bear the burden of giving up licences. The ratepayde gave up the licence of the Eagle and Child, but for whose benefit ?—the benefit of the remaining public-houses. That was not a position the Council ought to place the ratepayers in. The George was another free house, and if the licence was given up it was not known whether they would get anything from the Licensing Act or not. If they rejected Messrs. Walker's offer, and if they got nothing from the Act there would be a loss to the ratepayers. Surely the ratepayers of Warrington had paid enough during the past few years! That was from a financial point of view; now let them look at:the matter from an honeur- able point of view as business men. The Finance Com- mittee were aware that the Licensing Act was before the House of Commons, and yet with that knowledge they thought proper not to advertise, as they should have done, but sent to people likely to tender. The people who tendered thought it was going to be a legitimate transaction and that the highest offer would I be accepted. Councillor ROGERS : It was accepted. Councillor PEMBERTON said that the Town Clerk acted in the usual business way—there was no blame attached to him because he only followed out the usual custom in Corporation transactions—but it was another convincineproof that this was out of the usual way of conducting ordinary business. The Town Clerk wrote to Messrs. Walker,and said that, subject to the minute being pasted, their offer would be accepted as it was the highest. He contended that the members of the Coun- cil would be lowering themselves if they ffeparted from what an ordinary upright business firm would do in a , matter of that sort. He appealed to everyone, , teetotalers or otherwise, to carry out' the transaction 'in a proper business-like spirit. Messrs. Walker con- ducted their houses very well, perhaps better than any- one else in the town,and one could get teetotal refresh- ments as quickly as he could intoxicating liquor. There was also civility and despatch. (A Voice: "Tea and coffee.") Yes, tea and coffee at any moment. After the generous way in which Messrs. Walker had behaved to Warrington when it wanted money—money that could not be raised in any other way—he hoped they would be treated in the way they deserved. They might not be legally bound to carry it out, but the ordinary business of the country could not be conducted solely on legal lines. Councillor WILKINSON did not think Messrs. Walker would swallow this very nicely with a grain of salt— one of the largest brewing firms in England having to submit to Councillor Pemberton or any man begging the question for them. If the Corporation agreed to sell the licence and the magistrates refused to trans- fer it, would it be jeopardized? The TOWN CLERK : I don't think it would. Councillor WILKINSON: I have had a legal opinion to the contrary. Councillor ROGERS : Is not the licence sold subject to certain conditions?</p>
        <p>The TOWN CLERK: Subject to the magistrates agreeing to transfer .the licence to some other place. If the magistrates refuse you will still have the licence. Councillor WILKINSON: If the magistrates refuse the transfer, are we as a Corporation prepared to rebuild the George Hotel? Councillor LORD asked the Town Clerk if there had been anyalteration in the Licensing Bill since the offer of Afessrs. Walker was accepted by the Committee that would materially affect the disposal of the licence. The TOWN CLERK: I don't know that any alteration has been made.</p>
        <p>Councillor THoRNTON, in the course of a long Speech, I said he was proud to be one of the teetotallers to whom a reference had been made that evening. These ques- tions Were not decided byshouting, storming, and blow- I ing the roof off. (Laughter.) He liked to hear a man speak up and plainly too, but when he got to smoke, fire and powder—(laughter)— Councillor WILKINSON: To whom are you alluding? (Laughter). Councillor THORNTON (continuing) said one felt inclined to ask him to get a cooling draught before he came to the meeting. (Renewed laughter.) What he should like to do was to reason the matter over fairly. If there was justice to be done either to Messrs. Walker or to anyone else in the trade, brewer or publican, he would be one of the first to do it. (Hear, hear.) Strong teetotaller though he was, he defied anyone in that meet- ing or outside to say that he had ever attempted to do an unjust thing even to publicans and brewers But why should they rush the matter,which was a very important one ? What was there behind it ? Was there some private interest to serve? Was there something behind it that the members did</p>
        <p>Councillor THORNTON, continuing, said there was the £s.d. of the matter. Take a £l3 house, which was a fair one to take. It would be, say, assessed at £lO. As they knew a thousand pounds meant a penny to the rates. Therefore the selling of this licence for £2,500 would make a difference upon the £l3 house of 2s. id. for the first year only. The DEPUTY-MAYOR said that if the house was allowed to lapse the loss to the rates would be equal to 2-Id., and there would be a lot of grumbling about any- thing like that. Councillor.Thows-ros argued that £2,500 could not be put to the relief of the rates. The money would go to the borough capital account and would cover a period of 50 years. Further, they ought to consider the people of the town. The people, he took it, spent their money willingly on baths,parks, &amp;c., and he thought they would willingly spend it, or allow the Council to spend it, for the Bridge-street improvement—and decidedly it was an improvement. Considering all those points with the weight—and he put all the emphasis he could upon it— of condemnation on the consciences of those who voted against the lapsing of this licence, he thought they could not support Councillor Maginn's motion. Councillor &amp;rola' said the discussion on the motion seemed to have resolved itself into whether they were going to lapse the licence or not. He understood that the question was whether they were to accept Messrs. Walker's offer or to defer the matter. The DEPUTY-MAYOR: So it is. Councillor Srorr : When the matter comes up for decisive settlement are we to have all this discussion over again. It ought to be clearly borne in mind that we are only discussing whether to close with the offer. That ought to be borne in mind by the speakers so that they could keep to the point. - The DEPUTY-MAYOR added that no matter how the question ended so far as the licence was concerned the land had not been sold.aad the Corporation could rebuild the George if they liked. They were discussing now whether Messrs. Walker's offer for the licence should be accepted. Alderman Surrom said he could quite understand Councillor Thornton holding the views he did regard- ing licensed houses, and gave him every credit for advocating teetotal or temperance principles, but he thought that in a case of this sort they ought to put aside sentiment and bring common sense to bear. (Hear, hear.) Let them understand that this public-house did not belong either to the temperance party or any other section of people in Warrington. It belonged to the ratepayers, and they had no right to sacrifice the rate- payer i e nterest in the slightest degree. He considered it only right and fair to Messrs. Walker that their offer should be accepted. (Hear, hear). He looked at it in this way. Tenders were asked for in the usual way,and Messrs. Walker made the highest offer, and it was accepted by the committee. It was quite unfair that any shortcoming on the part of the committee in not considering the Licensing Act should affect Messrs. Walker. Councillor Thornton had said he did not see why the matter should be rushed, but they were not rushing it. (Hear, hear.) It had already. been put off for a month, and the reason was obvious why they should get the thing settled as soon as possible. It was in order that the premises at the top of Bridge-street might come down. Had Messrs. Walker tendered, and then when the tender was accepted have withdrawn it the Council would have thought it very unfair. If the Corporation spent £2,500 there would be a great fuss made about it, and it would be considered that they were robbing the ratepayers. Would not the Council be robbing them just as much by refusing to accept a sum which would relieve the rates to a very large extent? Councillor FORSHAW thought Councillor Maginn might be asked to withdraw his motion. He was sure he did not want altogether to ignorqpthe Finance Com- mittee, and if the matter was sent back to that com- mittee with instructions to carry out the bargain he thought it would meet the case. Councillor ROGERS : I hope it will not be withdrawn I want it to be settled to-night.. _Meer,</p>
        <p>Alderman SmETHurtsr : I think Councillor Forshaw only wants the matter referred to the Finance Com- mittee with instructions to them to carry out the bargain. Councillor RoGuas : Let's have it out properly to- night. Councillor FORSHAW : I mean to show that we don't altogether ignore the Finance Committee. It is their matter, and they might be allowed to carry it out on our instructions.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS: I think it ought to be settled to-night one way or the other, and I am going to vote that Walker's have it. They have made a good and honourable offer, and I don't think we, as a Council, would do right at all to refuse it after going so far. Councillor LORD remarked that the resolution adjourn- ing the matter said that it was to be deferred until definite information was obtained. The new Licensing Act had been passed and there had been no alteration in it since the tenders were asked for. He thought the committee were in honour bound to carry out their bargain with Messrs. Walker. The mistake was made in not looking at the Act before they asked for tenders. Councillor DODDS said he wished to make it clear that throughout the whole matter he had voted for the extinction of the licence, as he believed it would be in the best interests of the ratepayers to reduce the licences in the town. There were too many already. Councillor TINNION said he was another teetotaler, and, like Councillor Thornton, it was one of the mildest things of his life. But he was not going to give them a temperance lecture, as it was not in his line. He had given one or two to some of the members privately when they richly deserved it—(laugh- ter)—and he would reserve himself again until some of them were in such a condition that they would require it. (Laughter.) He was pleased he was not present at the last Council meeting, but he could congratulate the Council on its improved behaviour that night. He thought they ought to have some explanation as to why the Finance Committee, after carefully considering this matter, and going so far as patina' the acceptance of a tender on the agenda had turned right about, and asked for it to be withdrawn, because, in consequence of the new Licensing Act, they could get more. Per- sonally he would not vote either one way or another, ,as he could not do so and then have a say in the final I stage before the magistrates. If he could act on both</p>
        <p>5</p>
        <p>!sot know about? If so he did not understand occasions he would -feel bound that night to vote in, people holding a brief of that kind. Business should favour of Messrs. Walker. On the other hand he held be done in a straightforward way. (Hear, hear.) He very strong opinions that the town might lose by having . felt that he did not want to be an owner, either directly £2,550 of drinking facility put down in some neighbour- or indirectly of a public-house. He was not saying a hood whether the residents wanted it or not. At any word about those in the trade, but he did not want rate, the Council were entitled to an explanation from himself to be the owner of a public-house. There were the Finance Committee as to its change of mind. some conscientious, respectable and solid business men ' Councillor MAGINN, replying to the discussion said in the Public-house trade in Warrington. He wished he was very multi obliged to his friend Councillo2r For- to state that in a straightforward manner. This was sham for his advice. It was about the first free advice not the case "f a Provision shop, a draper's shop, or he had ever had from a lawyer. Councillor Thornton, even a chemist's shop which was likely to improve the , had accuSed him of making a noise. Well, he was a, condition of the People either temporally, socially o i noisy being. (Laughter.) It was.a peculiar thing that physically. (Alderman Francomb : Question.) Weli Councillors Thornton, Forshaw, and Dodds were mem- that was his (Councillor Thornton's) opinion, and he , bers of the Finance Committee, and yet took such did not. wish to rob other people of their 1 opposition to Messrs. Walker's tender being accepted.. opinion. He was as broadminded as any man in town Councillor FORSHAW : I was not present at the but what were they doing in other towns? meeting, so you need not refer to me. He had letters from Chester, Bolton, Crewe, St. Councillor Dos: I have opposed the thing all Helens. London County Council,