n the Council Child was allowed to lapse and they got nothing for it. who were the real cause of that not being accepted. They gave it up honourably for the benefit of the town. Therefore any loss that the ratepayers and the town In the case of the Green Dragon the Corporation bought suffered in the muddle of building that hotel and then the property alone and notthe licence. Therefore they selling it on trust was avthe teetotalers' door. As lost nothing by the licence, although he contended that business men, commercial men and men dealing with the transfer was one of the greatest mistakes the town business, they were in honour bound to accept Messrs. had ever made and was to the disgrace of the town. Walker's offer. (Hear, hear). Of course they all know his views. The Baths Hotel Alderman FRA.NCOMB said that as a point affecting the was bought in 1894. It was let quarterly for £35, vote he wanted to know if anyone taking part in the annually for £l4O. They had that now. Then they had division on that question would be debarred from voting also the Queen's Hotel bought at about the same if he was a magistrate, when the matter came up finally time, let at a rent of £270 per annum. The at the Police Court. last named two houses the Corporation still owned, as The TOWN CLERK replied that anyone voting that well as the George, so that they had three licences in night would be precluded from exercising jurisdiction their hands. He would like the Corporation to give on the matter when it came before the Bench. them up. He had had as much experience in regard to Councillor TEN-NroN : That is why I am.not voting. the effects of the drink evil in Warrington as any man in The voting resulted as follows : the town. He supposed £3,ooohad been offered for the FOR THE MOTION. AGAINST. licences, and if they got that money absolutely in the Alderman Evans Councillor Barton borough till they need not care whether the magistrates 99 Fairclough „ Dodds were ma frame of mind to grant the licences or not. Councillor Ashcroft Brough Warrington ought to set an example to other toxvns,and 9P Burton ~ when the Council was thoroughly purified on these 21 Craik ~Stott matters—(langhter)—he hoped the purification would 97 Davies 79 Ellison-5 take place very soon, he did not mean by change of mes If Duncan but of principle—they would see that they were bound 27 Forshaw . to legislate locally for the people,and when they remem- Lord bered the degradation and the misery caused by drink 9P Maginn and the homes that had been ruined he did not know „ P. Peacock how anyone could conscientiously vote for the transfer ~Pemherton of the licence. ~ Roberts The DEPUTY-MAYOR: We don't want a teetotal Rogers lecture. I don't drink cold water always and lam not Sankey-15 going to. (Laughter.) Tire DEPUTY-MAYOR declared the motion carried. Councillor THORNTON : I don't want— The DEPUTY-MAYOR: And we don't want that. Councillor TflonsToN : It is no use asking you to sign the pledge. (Renewed laughter.) 1 The DEPUTY-MAYOR : Let us get on to business. Continuing, Councillor THoRNTON said he did not I wish to give a teetotal lecture, but they were on the Iquestion of drink and public-houses and the wisdom or otherwise of transferring licences,and they could not go I into it without considering the whole matter. This was really his question and they must bear with him—. A VOICE: I think we have done already. Councillor TINNION: They will enjoy their whisky and soda after this. (Laughter.) Councillor THORNTON: Well, I don't think you are one of those. (Laughter.) Councillor TINNION: No; I don't take it, but some of them do.</p>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. . Bishop of Liverpool at Warrington. On Sunday the Lord Bishop of Liverpool (Dr. Chavasse) visited Warrington aud preached at Holy Trinity and St. Peter's churches, the offertories being on behalf of the Cathedral fund.</p>
        <p>Baptist Church, Golborne-street. The preacher on Sunday last was Mr. E. W. Jones, who based his evening sermon on Genesis 22-14, "Abraham called the name of that place Jehovah Jirah as it is said to this day 'ln the Mount of the Lord it shall be seen." The story of Abraham's faith was related, and the preacher explained that the faith which springs from love• is the highest, noblest and most heroic of all principles. Faith is the root of everything grand and good, but the faith that looks towards God is sublimer far than the faith 'manifested towards men. Abraham's faith received no stimulus from outsiders; it was purely the outcome of his love to God, and our faith should spring from love to God also. God pro- vided for Abraham, and ever reveals His might when human strength is well nigh spent.</p>
        <p>The Future of the Sunday Schools. Rev. H. Dunnico and the Religious Difficulty In Education. The Rossendale Valley Sunday School Union held its annual gathering at Waterfoot on Saturday. A large number of delegates attended the conference in the afternoon. In the evening the President, Alderman John Nuttal presided over a large gathering, the princi- pal speaker being the Rev. Herbert Dunnico, of the Golborne-street Baptist Church, 'Warrington. In the course of his address he said that they were all aware that for some time they had been in the midst of a bitter and angry controversy, and a controversy that was destined to continue until every vestige of privilege and private monopoly had disappeared. He did not. wish that night to attack the Education Act, but simply desired to point out that the sole cause of this con- troversy was a religious one. He did not hesitate to • say that if there had been no religious differ- ences there would have been no education controversy. He was not aware that he was misrepresenting their Roman and Anglican friends when he stated that their feverish anxiety to retain their control of the schools was due to their desire to use those schools to propa- gate their own distinctive tenets and peculiar dogmas. As Free Cuurchmen—and they represented all sections there thaVnight,—they could not agree to permit this in schools supported entirely out of public funds. Their reasons were two-fold. In the first place they looked upon such dogmas as being erroneous, unscriptural and detrimental to the highest welfare of the children, and secondly they represented and included that against which their very existence was a standing protest. The difficulty,however, lay in the fact that the Romanists and Anglicans were as much opposed to the teaching of the Bible without a sectarian interpretation as they were with it, and so far as he could see, the ultimate result would be that religious teaching of any kind would be excluded from the curriculum of all national schools, and all schools were national supported by public money. He did not say that such an outcome was desirable or that he agreed with it; in fact he did not. A large number of people, including Dr. Clifford, urged that as a logical solution, but life and experience were larger than logic, and these had already proved that the abolition of the Bible from our public schools might be attended with the most deplorable results. But if this did come to pass, and he firmly believed it would, then it would not be Free Churchmen who were responsible for it, but the clerical party who would insist upon a bigoted sectarian interpretation. What was the moral of all this for them as Sunday school workers ? Surely it was this,that when religious teaching was banished from their day schools by what was in his opinion clerical bigotry and priestly intoler- ance the vast majority of the children of the country would depend almost exclusively upon the Sunday schools for such religious teaching as they might receive. The responsibility of Sundatlschool teachers great as it was now would be infinitely greater then, and it was their bounden duty to see that their Sunday schools were made as efficient as possible. He (the speaker). had heard it said that the Battle of Waterloo was won on the " playing fields" of Eton. That might or might not be true, but he was quite sure that the battles of the church would be won or lost in the Sunday school. The future of the church and their country hung upon the wisdom and efficiency the insight,and enthusiasm of the churches' ministry to the young. How then could they make their Sunday schools more ? Mr. Dunnico then laid before the gathering certain proposals which evoked much interest. At the outset he criticised the proposals of Dr. Aked in, a friendly spirit. With much that his friend had said he was in entire agreement but he was afraid that Dr. Aked's solution meant practically the abolition of the Sunday school and the substitution of a children's ser- vice. If that were so he could not assist in any scheme which proposed the destruction of "England's glory." He then referred to the work which might be accom- plished by local Sunday School Unions, and urged a system of mutual visitation. The importance of visitation could not be over estimated.He condemned the officious manner adopted by some Sunday school visitors and said that visitors were not sent out as Factory inspect- ors to spy out the nakedness of the land and point out the defects, but to help and- encourage to listen as well as to speak and to learn as well as suggest By such a system of mutual and friendly intercourse, the weaker schools would receive ' a fresh impetus, the cast down would be cheered and they would recognize what they were so apt to, forget— that their difficulties and disappointments were common to each and all. He then urged upon Superintendents the importance of discipline. :Much of the lack of interest and diminished attendance was due to the lack of it. In conclusion, the speaker aroused the keenest interest by proposing that all Sunday school teachers be compelled to submit to an entrance examination. After pointing out that the teacher was as much called of God as a missionary or a minister and that the teacher who, was not conscious of the dignity and divine reality of his call ought to give his work up, he said that as there was a recognized standard of efficiency below which no man who professA to be a teacher of men ought to fall so in like manner there was a standard of efficiency below which no man who sought to teach children ought to fall. Generally • speaking the speaker contended that there were few men below the age of 40 who had not had some sort of an elementary education and an education which would enable him to pursue a course of study such as the one suggested. If a man refused to submit to the necessary discipline that at once proved his unfitness for the office. Many interesting details were dealt with and a resolu- tion asking Mr. Dunnico to submit a definite scheme for the consideration of the Union was carried with. acclamation.</p>
