      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>BENEFITS OF SCIENCE.</head>
        <p>THE BENEFITS OF SCIENCE.</p>
        <p>It is seldom that any discovery of science is such as benefit all classes of people. Some of the greatest discoveries of the age, while they have proved directly beneficial to thousands, have also, at least for a time, injured other thousands. Even some of the best medi- cinal preparations, however much they may do to remove one class of disease, or suit some constitutions, at the same time either deepen the hold of another disease, or leave it entirely untouched, or if they benefit one class of patients, they may injure another. Now -the great need of the age is some scientifically arranged preparation which will cope effectually with the pre- valent diseases of this country, which will be certain to do good when fairly tried, which will be equally adopted to the needs of the merchant and the workman, the professional man, and be who wins his bread by the sweat of his brow, the student, the clerk, the factory hand, the miner, and the roadside labourer. It should be, too, such a pre paration as contains no injurious ingrediepts, and which may be taken with, impunity by the weakly child, or the delicate lady, as well as by the stronger constituted man. Such a discovery would deserve to be called The Perfection of Medicinal Preparations, and would indeed be an Invaluable Boon to Suffering Humanity. Now this much needed boon has been found, has been tried, has been proved, and wherever proved, it has been found satisfactory. We refer to Gwilym' Evans' Quinine Bitters, The Vegetable Toni, which when once tried, has been always recommended, and has proved successful when all other modicine has failed to give relief, and we may say further, that it has proved per- manently beneficial, when other preparations at best gave only temporary relief. It is strongly recommended as The Best Remedy of the Age for Indigestion in its different forms, such as SiCk Headache, Pains in the Side, Giddiness, Loss. of Appetite, also for Nervousness and ;Nervous Disorders, Sleeplessness, Neuralgia, Low Spirits, and all kinds of Weakness. It has often proved very beneficial to persons suffering from great Weakness, either after an illness, long con- finement to ill-ventilated rooms or any other cause. it strikes at the source of the Disease, removing the cause of the illness, and strengthens those parts of the system which have been weakened by it, and therefore most liable to colds and• other ailments. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters being purely vegetable, it is a Natural Tonic, and suited to all ages at all seasons of the year. It is everywhere acknowledged to be a powerful aid in restoring the body to a healthy state when deranFed by disease. If you suspect that your health is beginning to fail, brace up your nerves, and fortify your constitution by taking now a course of this excellent Tonic, which is sold everywhere in bottles 2s. 9d. and 4s. 6d. each, hilt should any difficulty be experienced in procuring it, the Proprietors will forward same for the above prices, carriage free. Avoid Imitations. When purchasing,see the name, "Gwilym Evans," on Label, Stamp, and Bottle. Sole Proprietors: Quinine Bitters Manufac- turing Company, Limited, Llanelly;;South Wales.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>WHAT IS A HAWKF.R. ?—INTERESTING POINT RAISED. At the Warrington Police Court yesterday, Jack Moore, Berwick street, London, was summoned for hawking without a licence.—Defendant did not appear. Mr. Gaughan, supervisor of Inland Revenue, prosecuted and said the case arose out of an exhibition held in the Parr Hall on April 25th. According to the Act any, person travelling from one town to another and ex- posing goods for sale was ahawker. Councillor Tinnion: If a commercial traveller comes to an hotel, takes a stock room, and exposes goods for sale, will he come under the definition of a hawker.—Mr. Guaghan : If he sells goods he is a hawker. We take up every case of the kind that we can get.—Councillor Tinnion : You will find yourselves very busy.—Mr. Burgess: Can you quote a case in which a man has been convicted for selling goods at an exhibition ?—Mr. Gaughan: I cannot give you a case in point. Continuing he pointed out that of nine or ten persons exhibiting, nearly all had licences.. Defendant had the option of paying £1 to avoid bringing the case into court, but he would not. —William Price, Inland Revenue officer, said he visited Parr Hall on April 25th. At defendant's stall crochet and art work were displayed. Witness pur- chased an article and asked defendant if he had a hawker's licence, but he said he had not. He added that he had been with the exhibition from the commencement but business had been so bad that he had not beenable to get sufficient money to pay for a licence. He intended to get one at Halifax, the next town visited. He did not, however, obtain one there.—Mr. Gaughan pointed out that the Act was passed for the protection of local traders.—After the Bench had consulted in private, the Mayor said they were not satisfied that defendant was hawking in Parr Hall.—Mr. Gaughan: It is a question of fact then,and not of law.—The Mayor: I think it is a little of both. —Mr. Gaughan; If it is a question of law, I shall be obliged to appeal.—The Magistrates' Clerk (Mr. G. F.F. White) said the Bench were not satisfied that defendant was acting as a hawker.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>Warrington Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>Warrington Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>Minor Cases.</p>
        <p>MoNDA.v.—Before the Mayor (Alderman Bolton) Alderman Smethurst, Councillor Thornton, and Messrs. Birchall, Hayward, Wright, Roberts and Pilling. AN IMPUDENT BEGGING TRAMP.—John King, des- cribed as a weaver from Stockport, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Horsemarket-street on Saturday night.—Prisoner, who pleaded not guilty, was very voluble in the dock. He said he was a stranger. He had only one arm, and he made stockings for the public at large,and in that way gained his liveli- hood. It was a" beastly, dirty, vindictive lie "to say that he was drunk. Hehadjust travelled from London, where he had worked for over 20 years.—The Mayor: We don't want to know anything about London.—The Chief Constable: He goes about getting his living by his wits and begging. He is an impudent begging tramp.—Prisoner (indignantly) : How dare you stand up there in this 'ere court and vindictively charge me as a begging tramp? Do you know anything about me ? Give me ocular proof in this court or find out anything about me. How dare you to be so outrageous in a court of justice ? I have never been in this town before in my life.—The Chief Constable : And if you never come here again we shall not care. (Laughter.) —Prisoner was committed to gaol for 14 days.—The Chief Constsble : Remove him to the cells.—Prisoner: That is whereyou ought to go.—He was then removed below.</p>
        <p>SCENE IN JAMES STREET.—EIIen Griffin and Kate Murphy, of James-street, where charged with having been disorderly in James-street on August 29th.—P.C. Sweeney said. defendants were shouting at each other and using filthy language. They caused a large crowd to assemble, and the street was in an uproar—Dr. Peacock said he was in his surgery and heard the distur- bance going on. Such conduct was an almost daily occurence. Frightful language was used.—Defendants were bound over and ordered to pay the costs. GAMBLING. —Harry Garner, James Billington, and Richard Billington, youths, all living in Catherine- street, were charged with tossing for money in Hoyle- street on the 28th August.—They pleaded guilty and were fined ss.'each.—P.C. Hayes proved the case. • WOULD "Go DOWN."—Lowia Hargreaves, 88, Sharp- street, was charged with a breach of the peace by being guilty of disorderly behaviour in that street on the 27th August and was ordered to pay the costs. The alterna- tive was 14 days' imprisonment—Defendant(indigna.ntly): I will go down, but I go down innocent.—She was removed to the cells.—P. C. Metcalfe proved the case. REMOVING A FALSE IMPRESSION.—Winifred Hennes- sey, of Pocket-street, was charged with making use of obscene language in Pocket-street on the 20th August. P.C. Andrews proved the case, and stated that the defendant was standing on her doorstep at the time.— The Chief Constable: These people seem to be under the impression that they can, if they are standing on their own doorstep, "sound the deepest . depths of obscenity with impunity."—The defendant was discharged on promising not to repeat the offence. A FAMILIAR EXCUSE.—Joseph Gerrard, Foundry- street, was charged with fighting in that street on the 20th August.—P.C. Burton proved Idle case, and the defen- dant, who stated that he had to fight in self-defence, was fined ss.</p>
        <p>WEDN-EsDaY.—Before Messrs. Birchall (presiding), :Ind</p>
        <p>L. W. Reynolds. CARTER IN TROUBLE.—.George Leather, 12, Liver- pool-road, Sankey, was fined ss. for having been drunk whilst in charge of a horse and cart in Liverpool-road on Tuesday afternoon—P.C. Hesp proved the case.</p>
        <p>- - THURSDAY.—Before Mr. C. White (presiding), Alder- man Francomb, and Mr. J. R. Young. SLEEPING OUT.—Thomas Griffiths, a young man living in Wakefield-street, was fined is. for having been found sleeping in a shed at the rear of Mr. Dickenson's premises, Froghalnane.—He said he had been locked out of his home because he was not working. FRlDAY—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Alderman Smethurst, Councillors Roberts, Thornton, Tinnion, nod Wilkinson, Messrs. Burgess, Hepherd and Jolley. LENIEwcv REQUITED.—Mary Ann Roff, a stranger, was cnarge( with having been drunk and disorderly in Sankey-strett the previous night and with breaking a</p>
        <p>Washday</p>
        <p>Use Fels-Naptha and go by the book—addressed to women who don't yet.</p>
        <p>There's money in it, according to how much money there is in your clothes, your wash clothes.</p>
