      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, September 10, 1904.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, September 10, 1904.</p>
        <p>cell window at the Police Station.—P.C. Bennett found RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT CYJNCIL the prisoner very drunk and using flu thy language. As she refused to go away he had to take her to the Police Station in a cab. On visiting the cell on Friday morn- ing P.C. Howard found prisoner had broken the window i Stockton Heath's Sanitary Affairs. with a can. She complained that an officer had struck i her, but the Chief Constable said there was no founda- , Mr. S. Woodward presided at the fortnightly meeting tion for the statement.—Prisoner was before the court 1 o on Thursday for drunkenness and was fined is. " Shef this Council which was held at Dutton Workhouse requites the leniency of the magistrates" said Mr. on Tuesday. There were also present :—Messrs. G. i Pilling (vice-chairman), T. Baxter, T. Wright, C. E. Talbot " by comine here and breaking the ratepayers' . Linaker, C. E. Linaker, jun., J. Whitley, J. Hallows, J. property."—Prisoner on being sentenced to a month's imprisonment exclaimed" That nothing !" i Spruce, J. Mounfield, J. T. Collier, T. Antwis, J. L. AN OBSTINA.TR DRIVER. TRAMWAY TR,AFFIC Jones, J. Frye; %V. M. Morris, G. Egerton Warburton, H. M. Davies H. Eyes, J. Bolshaw, A. G. Guest, J. IMPEDED.—Richard Berry, Campbell-street, St. Helens, Smith, C. Horton, R. Billington, G. F. Ashton (clerk), was summoned for impeding traffic on the Corporation NV. E. Hough (deputy clerk), Dr. Adams (medical officer tramways on August 31st.-Ir. Stanley Wilson (deputy S town clerk) prosecuted and said defendant drove a horse of health), J. winton and W. Diggle (surveyors), and and cart on the track in front of a car which was proceeding J. Farrington (inspector of nuisances.) along Liverpool-road. Although the gong was repeatedly Dr. HE. Bowyer, district medical officer, wrote ask- and made use of filthy language to the conductor. The LEAVE OF ABSENCE. . sounded he continued the obstruction for a good distance, I ing the permission of the Eoard to be absent from home car was delayed seven minutes, and the whole tramway from the 14th to the 18th September. He stated that service was disorganised.—A fine of 20s. and costs (22 , Dr. Ryan'would undertake the duties in his absence. in all) was imposed. 1 The Board granted permission. UNSUSTAINED CHARGE.—Richard Clifton, a boy 1 WESTON SEWERAGE WORKS. living at 33, Mersey-street, was charged with stealing a 1 The Local Government Board wrote on August 25th quantity of timber value 75., the property of Win. sanctioning the application of the Council for the Cooper, 35, Mersey-street,—Mr. Browne defended.— 1 borrowing of £2,500 for works of sewerage in Weston., After hearing prosecutor's evidence,theßench decided that With regard to the proposed loan for house connections ' : it was asked whether it was proposed to carry out the Mr. Browne had no case to answer, and defendant was discharged. !,works at the expense of the ratepayers at large. Assum- DoG WITHOUT LlCENCE.—Frederick William Pear- ing this to be the case, the Board advised that such son, Lovely-lane, was fined 10s. and costs for keeping a , works (except where they come within the terms of dog without a licence. ! section 24 of the Public Health Act 1875 or of Section ASSAULT.—Benjmnin Roberts, 4, Carlisle-street, was 18 (3) of the Public Health Acts Amendment Act 1890) fined ss. and costs for assaulting Joseph Brown, 46, could only be carried out by the District Council under Fennel-street, on September Ist . 1 section 23 of the Public Health Act 1875 and that HOT WEATISER AND OVERTIME.—R. Stothert and where the works had been carried out by the Council Sons, mineral water manufacturers, John-street, were the expenses must be recovered from the owners or summoned for committing a breech of the Factory and • declared' to be private improvement expenses. On Workshops Act by employing nine women after the learning that Tay considerable number of notices had stipulated hours on July 15th.—Mr. A. Lewis, chief been served under section 23 and not complied with factory inspector for the district, prosecuted, and Mr. within the term specified, the Board, if the Council Lewis Carr, solicitor, Atherton, defended.—Mr. Lewis proposed to allow the owners to repay the expenses said that Mr. Eatock visited the factory at 8.40 p.m. within a term of years, would consider the on the date named and found the women had been work- question of sanctioning the borrowing fora like term of ing from six o'clock in the morning. The abstract of the sum necessary for the works. In any cases,however, the Act which was affixed in the factory, specified the coming within section 24 of the Act, i.e., cases where hours of employment as from 6 a.m. to 6 p.m.—The de- houses had drains communicating with sewers, whose fence was that during July the exceptionally hot drains, though sufficient for the effectual drainage of weather increased the demand for mineral waters to the houses, were not adapted to the general sewerage such an extent, that overtime had to be worked.—De- system of the district, the cost of providing connec- fendants were fined 20s. and costs in one of the nine tions to the new sewers appeared to be properly charge- cases and the costs (10s. 6d.) in each of the others. The able to the ratepayers at large. The Board should be total amount of the fine was £5 14s. 6d. furnished with particulars of the precise works pro- posed to be carried out and detailed estimates of the se. t cost.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH'S SANITARY AFFAIRS. The Clerk reported that at a meeting of the Stockton Heath Parochial Committee,the following resolution of the newly-formed Sanitary Committee was confirmed:— That this meeting is of the opinion that having regard to the greatly increased and increasing number of resi- dents and the constant complaints on sanitary matters, the time has arrived when more frequent and more efficient sanitary inspection must be given in this parish, and requests that the District Council will give, immediate attention to this urgent matter by the ap- pointment of an additional inspector so that more time can be devoted to.this and the immediate locality, . .</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Hallows said the time had come when something would have to be done. He did not thinkanyone would blame the present inspector who did his best, but he could not be in Stockton Heath all the time. It would be very helpful if they could have an inspector residing in the locality. Complaints were numerous and they were not all frivolous. . _</p>
        <p>The Chairman said the Council already had before them a suggestion that the position of the various officers, the surveyors especially, should be reconsidered by, committee. It would be fitting that the committee should at the same time consider the application of • Stockton Heath. Mr. Collier asked why the matter of a rearrangement of the surveyors' duties had been left in abeyance. ' The Chairman said he supposed it was through the impossibility of getting the committee together. The Clerk said the Committee consisted of the whole Council,and there was a difficulty in arranging a special • meeting. Mr. Linaker said he understood the matter 3was left in abeyance in order that the members could think over the suggestions. There would be a much better chance of carrying his point than if the question had been rushed straight away. Now that the Stockton Heath application had been received,it would ,be an opportune time to discuss the question. It was decided to consider the matter a month hence. MR. FLETCHER'S RESIGNATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. William Fletcher, District Councillor for Seven Oaks and Whitley Superior, communicated his 'regret that he was unable to reconsider his resignation as a member of the Council.</p>
        <p>The Clerk said it would now be necessary to lay the resignation before the Local Government Board. The Chairman said he was sure they all regretted the resignation, (Hear, hear.) • The Council accepted the resignation with regret. THE SUGGESTED PRODSHAM IMPROVEMENT. • Mr. Collier moved that the resplution passed on July 12th with reference to the proposed improve- ment of Church-street, Frodsham, be rescinded. The resolution was to the effect that the ' improvement should be carried out if the owner of the property (Mr. H. M. Davies) would undertake to cart , away all the soil that was necessary and give £2O to- wards the cost of building a wall. Mr. Davies con- sidered the Council asked foran excessive contribution, and offered to give £lO. Mr. Collier said his reason for moving the motion was that he considered the work would be a general improvement. It was the almost unanimous opinion in Frodsham that it ought to be done, and he did not think the Council could afford to ; set the matter lightly: aside. If they improved one part of the district they improved the whole district. They could not. look at it in the narrow sense that it was for ! the benefit of Frodsham only. The money, he con- ' tended, would be well spent. Mr. J. H. Davies seconded, and said the proposed ;improvement had the unanimous approval of the Frod- sham Parochial Committee. . .</p>
        <p>- - ---- ; Mr. Spruce said there was no need to hurry the matter, and the Council could give it careful reconsideration. . The improvement he considered, would be a step in the . right direction. Mr. Guest said the question was whether the terms offered were reasonable. As he understood it, the owner of the land was laying it out for building purposes and wished to make a certain improvement. He offered to give the District Council 58i yards of land, and he would take back 17 2-3, leaving 41 square yards for the benefit of the District Council in improvinz the road. The Surveyor's estimate for the building of a new wall was £57 17s. The owner offered to contribute £lO towards the cost, so that the Council would be paying £47 17s. for 41 square yards of land. The Dis- trict Council were prepared to build the wall if the ! owner would give £2O, which would leave a balance of £37 17s. The reason the wall would cost such a large amount was that the owner specified what kind gf a • wall be wanted to be built, and it would be more elaborate than other walls in the same street. 1 Mr. Linaker said he was in favour of public improve- , ments,but he did not believe in making them at a great . sacrifice of ' the ratepayers' money. At the last i meeting the i chairman said something to the 1 effect that Froittham had not had her share of improve- ments. He (Mr. Linaker) had represented the township 1 for six years and he could only say that he had been I fully satisfied with what the District Council had done. He did not think the Council should reject the proposed improvement entirely, because it would be to the benefit of the public. 'Mr. Davies's property would no doubt benefit very considerably by it. The better plan would be to adhere to the resolution or to approach the -natter in an entirely different aspect, and refer it back . to the Highways Committee. 1 Mr. H. M. Davies contended that the case had been misrepresented. He was giving 85 yards of land and receiving 6 yards, becausehe took it that the brook was I the boundary. The land he was giving was let at 3d. ; per yard which, capitalised at 25 years' purchase meant I £25. He was also giving £lO and would undertake the j carting which would cost £lO. Therefore he was giving I £45 towards a very good improvement, and he would not derive any benefit.</p>
        <p>On a vote being taken, Mr. Collier's resolution was defeated by 7 votes to 10. Mr. Davies: I beg to withdraw the offer. A RE-APPOINTMENT.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr. %right, Mr. James Farrington was reappointed inspector of nuisances.</p>
        <p>THE ISOLATION HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Isolationgospital sub-committee tenders for sewage disposal work were submitted from several contractors. The committee recommended that the tender of Messrs. E. Marshall and Sons, of Ashton- under-Lyne, at £260, be accepted. It was decided that the hospital should be lighted with acetylene gas, and that a further application be made to the Local Govern- ment Board to approve a loan for lighting purposes. The 'Chairman said that on the occasion ofi a former application, the Local Govern- ment Board declined to accept plant for acetylene gas as being approved by them for an institu- tion of that kind. The committee had reason to believe that the Board's position was now very much altered and that they would accept bhe acetylene plant as, at any rate, coming within the range of ideal things for use in hospitals in country places. The minutes were approved. WESTON MORTUARY QUESTION.</p>
        <p>The Council confirmed the minutes of the Weston Parochial Committee which included the appointment of a committee consisting of the Rev. D. S. Bowen and Messrs. T. J. Grounds and J. H. Rodiek to make further inquiries and report whether a suitable site for a mortu- ary could be obtained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>FINANCE.</head>
        <p>FINANCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wright reported that there was a credit balance at the last meeting of £B6 2s. 10d. The receipts during the fortnight amounted to £439 6s. 7d. and the cheques drawn that day to £304 17s. sd. leaving a balance of £220 12s. The unpaid oalls totalled £824.</p>
        <p>HEALTH OF THE DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>Dr. Adams reported one case of scarlet fever at Alvanley and a case of diphtheria at Helsby. Owing to an outbreak of measles he recommended the closing of Aston Council schools for three weeks. The Council agreed to this course.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>TRANSPORTER BRIDGES. Runcorn Bridge being followed by Another.</head>
        <p>TRANSPORTER BRIDGES. Runcorn Bridge being followed by Another.</p>
        <p>An illustrated article in the current number of the "Magazine of Commerce" describes a somewhat novel type of bridge, known as "The Transporter." About thirty years ago a Hartlepool engineer, Mr. Charles Smith, conceived the idea of this form of bridge con- struction for navigable waterways. Mr. Smith designed a bridge of this type for crossing the estuary of the Tees at Middlesborough, but it was never constructed, because it was feared that the storms on this exposed part of the coast might sweep away, or strain the fabric of the lofty towers. Subsequently the value of Mr. Smith's invention was realized elsewhere—the custom- ary case of the prophet being unappreciated in his own country—and Continental engineers unblushingly annexed it. During the last ten years- several " trans- porter " bridges have been built on the Continent of Europe and have proved a great mechanical and financial success. One of the great advantages is the compara- tive minimum olcost,and they are probably the cheapest and most efficient type attainable. It is a trifle morti- fying to our amour propre tc, find that one of the late Lord Salisbury's "dying nations" was the first to construct a "transporter" bridge. In 1893 Senor Palacio built the pioneer example over the river Nevvion,between Portugalete and Las Arenas, at a point nine miles north- west from Bilbao. The second pont transbordeur," as the French call it, was installed over the Seine at Rouen between the Quai Chevalier de la Selle and the Quai de Havre. The third " transbordeur" to be built was erected at the sea end of the canal connecting the outer harbour of Bizerte with the inner. French engineers are fairly enamoured of "transbordeurs." Quite recently others have been built and opened at Rochefort, and many more are contemplated throughout the country. To begin with, English engineers were rather inclined to regard the Continental "trans- porters "as freaks of bridge construction, but eventu- ally they realized their mistake, and two are now ' building at home. The first to be opened will be over both the Mersey and the Manchester Ship Canal, between Runcorn and Widnes. The collossal transporter bridge now building has been designed by Mr. John J. Webster, M.1.C.E., who was responsible for the "Great Wheel" at Earl's Court, and the various contracts for the works have been let to British firms. 'This bridge, when completed, will have the longest span of any bridge in the United Kingdom designed for carrying road traffic, the clear space over the Mersey and the Ship Canal being I,oooft. The other British " transporter" is being built across , the river Usk, at Newport (Mon.). between points where a clear span of 700 ft. is necessary. It only remains to add that as yet no hitch of the slightest des- cription has occurred in the working of the" trans- porters" already built, and that the croakers who pre- dicted collisions, attended by appalling consequences, between the transporter car and a vessel in the river have been false prophets. The journey in the car, skimming smoothly and swiftly a few feet above the water, is said to be an exhilarating experience. WIDNES AND RUNCORN TRRANSPOTER BRIDGE BYELAWS.</p>
        <p>The byelaws regulating traffic across the River Mersey and Manchester Ship Canal by means of the Widnestand Runcorn Transporter Bridge are published in our adver- tising columns, and the company intend to apply to the Board of Trade for their confirmation on October 11th The penalty i for offending against any one of the bye- laws s a sum not exceeding 40 shillings. It is evident that the company intend to take full precautions against accidents, and there is a byelaw prohibiting passengers from jumping on to or off a car whilst it is in motion. On the termination of each triy all passengers have to be landed before and other persons are allowed to ante the car. The officers of the company are empowered to direct passengers to occupy any part of a car that they (the officers) may think proper, and "a. person shall not refuse or neglect to comply with such directions." Loitering upon or obstructing the passage through the turnstile or other place leading to or from the landing places or other premises of the company is forbidden. The maximum load to be carried by vehicles in the cars, without special permission, will be 8 tons for two-wheelers and 13 tons for vehicles with more than two wheels. A vehicle of any description weighing with or without its load more than 15 tons will not be taken on to a car unless 24 hours previous notice has been given to the managerand his permission in writing obtained, and then only at such times as he shall direct. Loaded firearms have not•to be taken on to the com- pany's premises, and throwing stones or missiles is prohibited. Smoking will not be allowed except on parts of cars set apart for that purposq, and another byelaw prohibits betting and gambling on-the company's premises. Byelaw 27 is of a very comprehensive descrip- tion. It reads: "A person shall not be in a state of intoxication, or be guilty of any riotous, disorderly or indecent conduct, or spit;oi commit any other nuisance, or use any abusive or indecent language, or beg, or play upon any instrument, or sing, dance, pray, preach or read aloud, or offer for sale any matter or thing what- ever on any part of the premises of the company."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>ST. HELENS MYSTERY.</head>
        <p>THE ST. HELENS MYSTERY.</p>
        <p>THE BURNDREDS CHARGED WITH</p>
        <p>MURDER.</p>
        <p>PRISONERS REMANDED FOR EIGHT DAYS</p>
        <p>Another Exhumation.</p>
        <p>The excitement created in St. Helens by the decision of the authorities to charge Joseph and Ellen Burndred of 38, South John-street, with the wilful murder of Sarah Ann Jones, a girl who had been entrusted to them by the guardians, was amply demonstrated by the dimen- sions of the crowd of people in front of the Town Hall on Monday, notwithstanding the fact that there was no hope of getting inside to witness the brief proceedings in the police court. Mrs. Burndred was before marriage a servant at the Waggon Hotel, Haydock, and her husband is well known among the colliers of the neighbourhood as " Stafford- shire Joe." - - -</p>
        <p>When the girl Jones were first entrusted to them as foster parents the parties lived at 108, Parr Stocks-road. In February of this year they went to live at a shop 54, Rigby-street, and afterwards moved to 199, Sutton-road, from whence they moved to 38, South John-street, where the girl died.</p>
        <p>The prisoners were brought before the magistrates at a quarter to twelve, Burndred wearing a black shade over his right eye. A thrill of horror ran through the crowded court when Chief Constable Wood told the magistrates that the two were charged that they at 38, South John-street, St. Helens, on the 7th ult., feloni- ously, wilfully, and of malice aforeEltought did kill and murder Sarah Ann Jones by administering poison to her.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable stated that Sarah Ann Jones was thirteen years of age, and was admitted to the work- house in December, 1895. She was afterwards in the Cottage Homes, Fazakerley, up to August 29, 1901, and on that day she was boarded out by the guardians with the prisoners, who received a weekly allowance for her maintenance, and four days later she was insured in the Royal London Friendly Society, and on the first of February this year the prisoners also insured her life in the British Workman's Friendly Society. On July 29 last Dr. Bellew was called to the girl, who was ill. He attended her until death. She died on August 7, and he gave a certificate of death. The girl was buried at the St. Helens Cemetery on August 11. On the death of the child. the prisoners received £2O 12s. from one insurance society, and £9 12s. from another. On August 12, the day after the funeral, the police received information which caused them to make inquiries, and four days later they communicated the result of these inquiries to the coroner, and on August 20 he issued an order for the exhumation of the girl's body. Inspector Strong said that he saw the body taken up from the cemetery and taken to the mortuary. He was present at the post-mortem examination, and saw Dr. Unsworth place the viscera in six jars, which were sealed, and witness took them to the analyst. On Satur- day forenoon last he arrested the male prisoner, and on charging him with the wilful murder of the girl, he said, "I have nothing to say; I know nothing. That morning he charged the female prisoner with the same offence, and she said, "It is not correct." Herbert Edwards Davies, analytical chemist of 26, Chapel-street, Liverpool, said that on Monday, the 22nd ult., he received the six jars from Inspector Strong, and analysed the contents. He found in the stomach, intestines, liver, and kidneys considerable quantities of arsenic, sufficient, in his opinion, to cause death. Mr. Riley said he appeared for the prisoners, but would not oppose their remand. They were therefore, both remanded for eight days. OPENING OF THE INQUEST, _ _</p>
        <p>The inquest was opened on Wednesday onthe body of the child exhumed on Tuesday—Joseph Burndred, aged three months, who died on September 30th,1900. Only formal evidence ll of course, was taken. The organs of the body are now in the possession of Mr. Herbert E. Davies, analytical chemist, of Liverpool, who will sub- mit them to analysis and report to the adjourned inquest. Meanwhile Joseph Burndred and his wife Ellen, who have been living at 38. South John-street, are in custody on a charge of wilful murder. He and his wile have been married for twenty-nine years, and are stated to have had thirteen chilhren. Of these only one is living, a daughter, who is about twenty-one years of age. In view of the tragic turn which events have taken. the police have taken steps to locate the graves of the dead children of the prisoners. They are now in possession of the particulars of ten of these children. All of them are stated to have died in infancy. NEXT 3IAGISTERIAL HEARING.</p>
        <p>The two prisoners are to be brought before the magis- trates again on the 13th inst., when it is expected further evidence will be given. When they were brought up on Monday neither of them displayed any great perturbation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>RUNCORN AMD WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AMD WIDNES.</p>
        <p>LICENSING APPLICATION AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn Petty sessions oil R.i;Ta-i;.- William Gilbertson was granted temporary authority to sell un- til the next transfer day, at the Elephant and Castle Inn. Halton, lately occupied by George Herbert Andrews.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>ANTI-VACCLNATIONISTS AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>ANTI-VACCLNATIONISTS AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Monday, at Runcorn, Frank Littler, Waterloo - road, Joseph Lightfoot, Norfolk-street, and Frederick Duncalf, Balfour-street, were granted certificates of exemption from vaccination. Littler and Lightfoot were refused at the same court a week ago. APPLE STEALING AT FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn Police Court on Monday, Fred Yarwood John Yarwood, Thomas Allman, John Jannison and Harry Davies, youths of Frodsham, were convicted of stealing apples from the garden of Edward Jones, of Sutton, Frodsham Bridge.—Evidence was given that the trees had been shamefully damaged.—Mr. A.R. Norman said the bench had had numerous complaints of damage done in the district, and they were determined ,to stop it. Each defendant was fined 20s. or fourteen days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>ROWDYISM AT FUNERALS.</head>
        <p>ROWDYISM AT FUNERALS.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn Urban Council meeting on Wednesday, Mr. Handley said there was a feeling of rather more than disappointment at the closing of the cemetery gates to the public during the time of funerals.—The Secre- tary (Mr. Marshall said the superintendent of the ceme- tery had received instructions to close the cemetery on cne occasion only,and that#vas during the interment of a person who had died from typhus fever. If the super- intendent had closed the cemetery on other occasions probably he had good reasons. Disorder had got to such a pitch during funerals that even the clergymen had had to appeal to the people to keep order. Wreaths and flowers had been stolen. The people crowded round the grave to such an extent that even mourners could not get near the grave, and the superintendent had almost been in despair.—Mr. Posnett said it should not go forth that they in any way objected to the action of the superintendent. He would be backed up in any action he took to ensure good order. MERSEY MISSION TO SEAMEN.—SAILORS' GARDEN PARTY.</p>
        <p>Through the kindness of Mr. J. G. Rodger, of Liver- pool a member of the Executive Committee of the, Mission, a very pleasant party of workers and sailors in connection with the Runcorn Mission was held on Satur- day last in the recreation field. The party assembled at the Institute at 3 o'clock, and headed by the Hope Hall brass band, proceeded to the field where a variety of games, including cricket, football and rounders &amp;c. were indulged in. The weather was fortunately everything that could be desired. Afterwards all adjourned to the Institute and upwards of 70 were entertained to an excellent tea. They again went to the field and had a number of races &amp;c. for which prizes were afterwards given. In conclusion a meeting was held to distribute the prizes to the successful competitors. Each man was also presented with a packet of tobacco by Mr. Shaw who also informed the men that the treat was the kindly gift of Mr. Rodger, who had on sever.l previous occasions shown his interest in the sailors and a vote of thanks to him was moved and carried amid great applause. The services of the band were likewise cordially acknowledged. This pleasant meeting con- cludeckwith the singing of the Doxology.--At the open Sunday school on Sunday, there was a very interesting programme in which various hymns and recitations were given, and the handbell ringers of Mr. C. Tonks gave their services together with the orchestra. There was also a presentation of bibles,prayer books and other prizes to different scholars in acknowledgment of the interest taken in the Sunday school anniversary fund. On Friday the 2nd September a meeting of the workers was held, and various committee's formed for carrying out the plans for the winter's work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>THE SURETY SUFFERS.</p>
        <p>On Thursday at Newton, David Critchley, of Hay. dock, failed to appear in answer to a charge of being drunk in charge of a threshing engine, and the a urety's recognisances were estreated. LEFT WHEN IN DEBT.</p>
        <p>Henry Rimmer,a Wiganauctioneer,applied at Newton- le-Willows on Thursday for an order againt one of his former tenants, Thomas Maxwell, Athol street, Earles- town, for clandestinely removing his goods to avoid distraint. It was stated that whilst owing £8 6s. 6d rent at the rate of 4s. 6d. per week he "left at night- time." An order to pay double the amount of the value of the goods was made. _WORSE THAN ROBBING.</p>
        <p>At Newton Petty Sessions-- on --iliursday Thomas Cooke, farmer and colliery engine-winder, of Bold,near St. Helens, had to pay 10s. and analyst's and advocate's fees for selling adulterated milk. Mr. A. Browne, who prosecuted, said Dr. Campbell Browne's certificate showed that 10 percent. of water had been added,whilst a second sample. taken a fortnight later, showed the quality of milk to be higher than the Board of Agricul- ture's requirements, a difference between the two samples of 40 per cent. Mr. Browne said,it was worse than robbing a man's pockets to adulterate milk,because it was injurious to health. Defendant denied any adul- teration.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>RUN CORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUN CORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of this Board was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday, under the presidency of Mr. A. R. Norman. There were also present :—Rev. Father Donlevy, Messrs. T. Baxter, S. Woodward, T. Wright,C. E.Linaker,C. E. Linaker, jun.,J. W. Temperley, R.Morris,J. Sprnce,J. Mounfield, J. Hallows,-P. Pilling, J. T. Collier, T. Antwis, J. L. Pones, J. Fryer, qW. M. Morris,G. Egerton Warburton,' H. M. Davies, H. Eyes, J. Bolshaw, A. G. Guest, J. H. Davies J. Smith, C. Horton, J. Whitley, R. Balington' d. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (deputy clerk), T. Cook, T. Riley and W. R. de Prez (relieving officers), and J. Cunnah (master.)</p>
        <p>AN OBJECTIONABLE CUSTOM. The Local Government Board wroth stating that their attention had been drawn to the fact that in certain Poor Law Unions where the wives and children of prisoners had become chargeable to the poor rate, the practice had been adopted of sending the wives and children to the prison gates to meet the prisoners on their discharge. The Board desired to point out that this practice was not only very objectionable on general grounds, often entailing hardship on children, but actually illegal where the prison was situated outside the Union to which the prisoner's family was chargeable. The Board considered it desirable that the practice re- ferred to should cease in all Poor Law Unions. The Clerk said the custom was not ,in force in the Runcorn Union.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>NURSING STAFF.</head>
        <p>THE NURSING STAFF.</p>
        <p>The Clerk announced the receipt of a letter from the ! Local Government Board agreeing to the appointment of Miss Rhoda Bottomley as nurso at theiWorkhouse, at a salary of £3O per annum with the customary rations &amp;c.</p>
        <p>The Master reported on August 30th, that with the approval of Dr. Selby, Medical Officer, he had engaged Nurse Ruth Redfern to take office in about a fortnight. It was not proposed at present to engage a temporary nurse.</p>
        <p>HEALING INJURIES AND SORES.</p>
