      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>LEGISLATION OF INTEREST TO GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>LEGISLATION OF INTEREST TO GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>1 The Clerk announced the receipt of a circular- letter ! from the Local Government Board, drawing the atten- tion of the Guardians to the Outdoor Relief (Friendly I Societies) Act, 1904 which has been passed during the recent session of Parliament and which effects some;im- portant modifications in the law relating to the grant ! of outdoor relief to persons who are members of : friendly societies. The Board also adverted to some of !, the provisions contained in the Army (Annual) Act, 1904 and the Prevention of Cruelty to Children Act,1904, and to certain other measures (passed during this year's ; session) which affect the powers and duties of Guar- I diens or otherwise deal with matters in which they are , interested.</p>
        <p>DEPUTATIONS. It was decided that the Board should be represented at the forthcoming Poor Law Conference at Lancaster by Messrs. Revlance, Mounfleld, and Maw&amp;ley. The 1 Chairman and Vice-Chairman of the House Committee with Mr. Douglas were appointed a deputation to visit the County Asylum, and Messrs. Baxter, Collier and Spruce were selected to visit Buckley Hall and Panta- saph Orphanages. The Clerk will accompany the depu- tations.</p>
        <p>FINANCE-TRADE DEPRESSION AND THE NON-PAYMENT</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>OF CALLS,</head>
        <p>OF CALLS,</p>
        <p>I Mr. Woodward reported that there was a balance in hand at the last meeting of £1177 us. Bd. The receipts during the month totalled £1,019 and the payments that day £177 13s. 10d, leaving a balance of £336 ss. 6d. The total amount of calls unpaid was £1,083, the defaulting parishes being Acton -Grange, Alvanley, Antrobus, Budworth, Kekewick, Kingsley, Kingswood, Moore, Newton-by-Frodsham and Stockton Heath. The over- seer for Grappenhall wrote stating that a cheque had been paid in the previous night. 1 The Chairman said that if all the townships deferred I the payment of calls, the Board would be in a serious position. The overseers should be kept up to the mark. Mr. Woodward said he understood that the finances ' of the District Council were in a still worse state than , the Guardians' finances. 1 Mr. Whitley thought that considering the depression of tradeand the lack of money in the country the Board , ought to allow a little grace. I Mr. Woodward said he deplored trade depression as much as the member for Stockton Heath, and he also was in favour of extending as much grace as possible. The Board had to make their payments, and there was a, possibility of extending grace beyond the due responsi- bility of their position. When townships were .a month behind -some effort should be made. Mr. Hallows said the assistant overseer for Stockton : Heath told him he had the greatest difficulty in collect- ing the money. Mr. Woodward: If Stockton Heath had been an agricultural dommunity I could have understood it, but since it is an industrial and business community I can- not understand why the calls connot be produced. Mi. Woodward explained that the assistant overseer for Kingsley had been unwell. The Clerk was instructei to make application for pay- ment of the calls. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>CLERK'S TRAGIC DEATH. Found Shot in his Home.</head>
        <p>A CLERK'S TRAGIC DEATH. Found Shot in his Home.</p>
        <p>A sensation was caused in the neighbourhood of Mersey- street about noon on Monday, when it became known that James Henry Houghton, son of Mrs. Houghton, of Mersey-street, had been found shot dead in the dining room of his home. The circumstances were inquired into by Mr. Coroner Brighouse and a jury on Tuesday. Joseph Harry Houghton was the first witness called and said the deceased was his brother and resided with his mother at the pawnshop in Mersey-street. Deceased was 29 years of age. He came home to dinner shortly after one on Monday and appeared as usual. He had dinner in the kitchen, and then went into the dining- room. At about two o'clock witness was in the shop Ewhen he heard a loud report and rushed to the dining room. There he saw his brother lying on the floor bleeding from the head. A double-barrelled gun was by his side, and on exantination he found in the right barrel a discharged cartridge. He sent for Dr. Lang- dale, who pronounced life extinct. The deceasedhad been suffering from indigestion for some time and had been rather depressed, but had never threatened in any way to take his life. The gun in question was one of twelve bought for sale, and he and deceased had been cleaning and examining them. Eleven were taken into the shop, but the one in question was found to be a little stiff and was left in the dining-room for further examination. On the sideboard was a box of cartridges purchased for sporting purposes, and the discharged lone was one of them. Witness had been trying the gun with a cartridge to see how it fit, but he had taken the cartridge out. The deceased was brushing his hat as though preparing to return to business when he last saw him alive. Deceased was probably trying the gun with a cartridge when it went off. The Coroner: May I take it that you have no sus- picion that he took his own life. Witness: Absolutely none. He was quite happy, was in a good position, and there was nothing what- ever to do sueh a thing for. In fact I think he had his cycling clips on ready to return to work. P.C. Rylatt stated that at a quarter to two on Monday afteroon he was called to 28, Mersey-street. The deceased was lying on the dining-room floor, bleeding from a wound on the head. An empty cartridge was taken from the gun. He examined the pockets and found cash, keys, ttc. There were no letters or corres- pondence of any kind. , Mr. J. Fulton Smith, manager for Messrs, Peter Walker and Son, by whom the deceased was employed as a clerk, said the deceased had nothing whatever to trouble him so far as he knew. He came to the brewery a boy fresh from school and had been under his (Mr. Smith's) supervision ever since. He proved to be most trustworthy and painstaking in all his duties and could be relied upon that his work was conscientiously carried out. He was a steady, well-conducted young man and his quiet and affable manner made him very popular with the staff with whom he was on the best of terms. He had left his books and papers on the desk ready to go on with them in the afternoon. There was nothing to arouse the slightest suspicion that he had any intention of taking his own life. The Coroner pointed out that the question for the jury was whether there was any evidence to show how the unfortunate young man liad met his death. He had no trouble and nothing to upset him, andcame home to dinner and went about in the usual way. Then he was found shot. It might be said that it was suspicious that there should be a cartridge in the gun, but when they considered the matter they would see that the deceased might have been trying the gun, and fitted a 1 cartridge to see if it slipped in easily. The brother had examined it the same way before, and it would have been possible for an accident to have happened then. Of course it mightbe possible that he could Nave shot himself, but personally he thought the evidence was not such as any jury ought to commit themselves upon. Nobody saw the occurence, but it was for the jury to say whether it was an accident or not. The jury retired, and on returning the Foreman (Mr. T. Walsh) said they were unanimous in deciding to return an open verdict, there being no evidence to show whether death was caused by accident or suicide. Mr. J. Fulton Smith said he wished to express on behalf of the directors of the firm their senso of the great loss they had sustained in the death of so faith- ful a servant: and to convey to the widowed mother and relatives their sympathy with them in their great trouble. The Coroner added that both the jury and himself were honestly sorry for Mr. Houghton and the family in their loss. Mr. Houghton said he could only thank them all for their sympathy and the police for their great courtesy</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>"I WILL HAVE VI-COCOA."</head>
        <p>"I WILL HAVE VI-COCOA."</p>
        <p>Your grocer and every other grocer, too, can tell you of men and women who week after week regularly use Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa. and who, to use a common term "swear by it" as a nourishing and stimulating Food Beverage.</p>
        <p>Are you a regular drinker of Vi-Cocoa ? - If not, why not?</p>
        <p>Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa, by taking the place of stimu- lants of an ardent nature, has made happy many a home which otherwise to-day would have been wretched, and the ultimate effects of which it is impossible to foresee. For breakfast, dinner, supper—indeed at any hour of the twenty-four—Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa will be found light and refreshing; it leads the way among all beverages, and nothing can equal it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>BAND or HOPE.—On Monday evening the annual meeting of the General .Committee of the Hill Cliffe and Stockton Heath United Band of Hope was held. This society, which was founded ten years ago, represents Hill Cliffe (Baptist), Providence (Independent Methodist), and Ebenezer (Weslexan) Sunday schools, and has done a good work amongst the young of the district during that period, being the only temperance organization in the neighbourhood until recently, when a similar organization was formed in connection with St. Thomas's school. The report of the Secretary showed a substantial financial balance which was considered highly satisfactory. The officers were thanked for their past services, special reference being made to the work of the President (Rev. J. S. Hughes) and secretary (Mr. G. Whitfield). The following were elected officers for the coming session :—President. the Rev. J. S. Hughes: vice-presidents, Messrs. H. Brereton, J. P earse, A. H. Dunn, J. Roughsedge ; treasurer, Mr. Joseph Mounfield ; secretary, Mr. Georg!, Whitfield; assistant secretary, Mr. George Moss; registrar, Miss Lilly Hall, organist, Master G. Arthur Whitfield; accompanist, Mrs. J. S. Hughes; representative to the Warrington Band of Hope Union, Rev. J. S. Hughes, Messrs. H. Brereton, J. Pearse and G. Whitfield. The above, with other representatives from each school, form the cora- mittee. Meetings will be held weekly throughout the winter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
