      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <p>In Diagram 5, if a ball be played at an angle to the top cushion (position A). without side, it will rebound at a similar angle, as shown by the thin continuous line, but if running side (right hand side in t'his case) be imparted to the ball the angle of reflection will be increased, zaore or less as shown by the thick intersected line. If check side (left hand side) be used the angle of deflection will be decreased, more or less as shown by the thin intersected line, and Sot only will this happen, but as is not very generally known by inferior players, the speed qf the ball will also be greatly checked after •contact with the cushion. Hence the term check-' side. If, however, a ball be played full at a cushion Its shown in position B, neither side can be check Side, as there is no angle of reflection to diminish. In such a case either side is running Side, even though there is no angle of reflection to increase, for as just mentioned, check side checks- the speed of a ball as it rebounds from. a cushion, whereas in position B, side, whether "right or left, does not diminish the speed of the ball as it leaves the cushion. If the ball that is played from position B afterwards strikes the opposite side cushion, any side on the ball becomes check side as it Strikes the second cushion, and this is often xoticeable when playing cross cannons with ex- treme side</p>
        <p>DIAGRAM 6.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <p>'A cross catmon.—Showirqg haw running side off si aide cushion becomes check side off the opposite aide cushion. 4...</p>
        <p>Diagram 6 shows such a stroke. In order to get the cannon a great deal of right hand side must be given to the cue ball. This side is running side off the cushion on which the object ball lies. When, however, the cue ball strikes the opposite cushion, the side which it carries is Check side off this cushion, and this causes a rather square rebound as shown by the con- tinuous line. Were the side on the ball still running side when the second cushion is struck, the angle of reflection would be increased and the rebound would be more or less as shown by the intersected line.</p>
        <p>DIAGRAM 7.</p>
        <p>A. fancy miss in lraulk.—an %hot.</p>
        <p>-Diagram 7 shows an extremely difficult but very, tereeting stroke—a fancy miss in baulk, which q, nionstrates in a very marked degree the possi- 4ilities of check side. If a ball be placed at one glztremity of the D, as shown on the diagram, and aim be taken at a point on the opposite rhion, about twelve inches inside the baulk ue, it is possible (on most cushions, though not 141, all) to cause the ball to go out of baulk and 4,en come in again. In order, however, to be 441 e to .to make this curious stroke, a player loust be able to impart intense side to the ball. for the stroke to have any chance of success .tte ball must only just come out of baulk as "'"ovio on the diagram. Should it come out aux</p>
        <p>I • 4 Vi .P'• • ! : , extremely difficult</p>
      </div>
