      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT,</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT,</p>
        <p>English Nurses in Great Request.—The King has re- turned from the Continent with an added responsibility —that of being godfather to the son of the Czar. The fact that King Edward and Princess Victoria, always a favourite with the Czar,. were asked to become sponsors for the new-born heir, is of the happiest augury, as showing that the Czar sincerely desires to keep on the best of terms with our Royal Family. We hear a great deal about Anglophobia on the Continent, but there is no trace of this in the Royal nurseries of Europe, in which English nurses have always been in great request The late Princess Alice would have none other than a nurse recommended by Queen Victoria, and the Czarina has always entrusted her children to English nurses. The King and• Queen of Italy have adopted thirsame course which has also been followed in the German Royal house, the Kaiser and his brother Henry, and all the Emperor's children, having been brought up by nurses of the British nationality. The Queen of Holland and the King of Spain, are also indebted for their early training to English governesses. Women live longer than Men.—A lecture the other thy on the subject of women's emigration calls to mind the fact that women live longer than men, in nearly all civilised countries. The "expectation of life" at birth, is calculated to be 44.66 years for boys, and 4718 for girls. The net result of this greater vitality of women is shewn by the census returns of 1901, when in the whole of the British Isles more than a million and a quarter more females than males were enumerated. The proportion varies considerably in different parts of the United Kingdom, but the average is about 1065 females to 100 males.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <p>Women in Factories.—There are nymy who believe that the employment of women is not profitable to the community as a whole, and much more has been heard on this subject since women have entered into all the higher callings of life.—This development however, cannot be blamed for the state of things which is said Ito exist in the cycle trade at Coventry. From the early days of factories there have been factory girls, and there is nothing new in the employment of women in industrial pursuits. But in the cycle factories it seems the influx of women is having a serious effect in dis- placing men. The father at 355. a week, is discharged, and his daughter taken on at 15s. a week, and in this way many families are deprived of the support of the ' chief breadwinner. This is far from a satisfactory state of things, and yet it seems quite irremediable. The demand for cycles has largely fallen off, and is now chiefly for cheap machines, so that if the daughter did not work for her 155., it is doubtful if the father would , be kept on at his 355. a week. The Coming of the Cheap Motor.—The motor-car has already brought a large increase of business to surgeons and undertakers, and now that the cheap motor at £5O or .270 is practically attained, life out of doors will become more adventurous than ever. The lowering of the price for which so many people have been waiting, will bring the motor within reach of classes not distinT guished for manners or consideration for others. Per- haps nothing better could be said for the present community of motorists, but who at any rate have hitherto been limited to the well-to-do, and one would suppose the well-behaved classes. What will be the state of affairs when Tom, Dick and Harry, with their lady companions, begin tearing about the country, one can only imagine, as, quite apart from. management, the cheap car will be much more liable to get out of control, and perhaps blow up, with disastrous consequences to all or any standing near by. It is quite likely that under these circumstances, motoring will cease to be fashionable, but it is certain to become more and more dangerous to pedestrians, cyclists, and others who are entitled to the use of the roads without having their lives in peril. Cruelty to Animals.—The movement for the preven- tion of cruelty to animals seems happily to be spreading on the Continent, and we have been told of an enthusiastic Spanish committee who arranged a bull fight in aid of the funds of the new organization. Some effective protection of birds is certainly needed on the Mediterranean coast line, where thousands of migrating birds are shot, apparently for the mere love of taissing life, and Lady Meath testifies from her recent observa- tions in Holland that there is a good deal of cruelty to dogs, which the State ought to prohibit. In this country, the law prevents the use of dogs as draught- animals, but there are some continental countries, of which Holland is one, where no such prohibition exists. As a rule, the immunity from penalty is not abused, large dogs being employed to draw small carts,but Lady Meath's letter tells of ill-treatment, which is "terrible ,to witness." This statement is supported by several illustrations, including one of a donkey cart, containing Itwo men, being drawn by one dog. Autumn Millinery.—The woman who really needs a new hat, and does not want to go into felts in Septem- ber, should select one of the pretty models built partly of straw and partly of tulle, that are appearing in the show rooms of leading milliners just now. But the new felts are very tempting in their delicate pale colours, and look really better with early autumn tailor-made costumes. Wings, and alas birds, are adorning (?) many of these pretty felts, and another lamentable fact is, that the osprey is more in evidence than it has been for some months past. The Modes in Furs.—Regarding furs, it appears that I the darker varieties will reign supreme, during the coming season. Sable, sealskin, and silver fox are to be I first favourites, while mink and skunk will take a second place in our affections. White furs promise to be little worn, and as they are becoming only to the very few, this is not a decree to be regretted. The Linen Collar.—The more simple, and severe tailor made gown. has brought about a revival of the plain linen collar, either of one depth all round, .or dipping down into a point in front. These latter boast a line, or lines of open work, and fasten behind. Cuffs are donned to match. It is to be doubted however' if the idea is as becoming as the high collar affixed by the tailor, and a neat turned-over band of white French Cambric. There is a hardness about the white linen collar that renders it unbecoming to the generality of women.</p>
        <p>Fairy-like Coverings.—Withthe approach of autumn, dainty little coverings, intended for wear with a decollete bodice,have appeared, and very attractive and becoming they prove. One model takes the form of a large sailor collar, built of chiffon and lace. This fastens in a knot at the bust,the long ends falling almost to the feet. Quite thd favourite among these diaphan- ous wraps however, is the old-world lace, or gauze shoulder scarf. The home worker will have little diffi- culty in running up an imitation, say in silk and lace, in alternate rows of insertion, or in figured net, much rucheirwith chiffon, but those among us who possess the genuine old-fashioned article. are among the envied ones just now. _ _ .</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>MAD GE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
