      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>iv 'OA</head>
        <p>iv 'OA</p>
        <p>The Most Nutritious and Economical.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, September 10, 1904.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>I have sn this summer some really very pretty frocks for quite young girls math in (lead white taffeta trimmed with soft 'tulle or chiffon. The effect is good, although it would be apt to prove too severely trying tor( any but a very fresh flawless complexion showing the clear softness of early youth. These frocks wear very well indeed and clean well, which are two particularly good points in their favour, while they do not crease or crush in packing as is so often the case with softer fabrics. The trimmings, too, of tulle or chiffon are not expensive and can be easily renewed, while a soft scarf of silk or gauze worn loosely across the shoulders gives a very pietty finishing touch to a toilette of this description. These dainty old world scarfs are rendered nll the prettier if a few flowers are painted on the ends in soft toned water colours</p>
        <p>and they seem to have taken the place of the shoulder capes' and ruffles and are, of course, lighter than the feather boas and stoles, al- though as the season advances and the even- ings become chillier the last-named wraps will be preferred as they certainly afford the wearer a degree of warmth and protection. Silks of every kid from the thin washing variety to the thick ribbed material handsome enough to stand alone, have been, and are still, in great demand, the fornier variety being made with numerous) gaugings and lace insertions, and the latter with little trimming of any kind, good lace, indeed, being the only really satisfactory one. The pretty dress shown in my first sketch is of tussore silk trimmed with an embroidered yoke and frills of pale cream silk spotted with a darker tone. Bright brown velvet bows caught in the centre with a small gold buckle made a quaint but picturesque finish to the front of the bodice, while sundry. gaugings of the spotted silk and a frill of the tussore appear on the skirt. . The sleeves are made with a double puff, the second one being the larger, while a deep frill of the embroidery falls over the lower part of the arm to the wrist. The becoming hat which completes the toilette is of cream straw trimmed with a chou of pale brown chiffon and a long, loosely curled ostrich feather. A dress of this character is</p>
        <p>ikre4- rlig- ~,i'lli, " _---z----,-- . Av 3t, _l\ iiilt ‘st, .-- 11.'4 , &gt; ~,_ \ ~-,z-z,,,Asec,:li,, _ f I ,- .., • .„, . , i .•'..-N \,,,. -.714.,... =------418y \ Ali , A PRETTY DRESS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <p>SMART HAT.</p>
        <p>suited for either seaside or country use and would, besides, look very smart for morning wear in town. Tussore silk is a material which meets with warm approval from the economi- cally minded matron, for any amount of wear can be got out of it, and it can be made down into very auseful and nice little frocks for the children whenever there is any danger that the style of its fashioning is becoming at all anti- - - quated.</p>
        <p>Et- Very fashionable indeed are the hats with a gracefully curving brim which, although they lie flat on the coiffure at the back,! project well over the !face. A smart sample of this shape is given in my second illustration, the hat in this instance being of burnt brownish straw trimmed with a full band of cerise silk which finishes in a large chou at(3ite side of the front and has a little shaded foliage intermingled with it. For wear this month it is particu- larly smart as the colouring is exactly suited to the season of the year. All shades of red will increase in fashionable popularity as the season advances, and when the brightness ol the summer sun has changed into the dulness of an autumn afternoon, it is always pleasant to see a touch of bright rich colour introduced somewhere in the tout ensemble of dress. Red will be more than ever approved of, and whole toilettes of this warm colour will be many. I</p>
