      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>COMFORTABLE COAT.</head>
        <p>A COMFORTABLE COAT.</p>
        <p>think a red cloth costume strappea or °raided with black is particularly nice wear, though it is, indeed, early times yet to make up our minds. The English summer, however, is but a short time at its best and most of us are already meditating on a warmer costume than those we have of late affected as a cold spell sets in in this climate with startling suddenness.</p>
        <p>The comfortable coat depicted in •my last , sketch could be expressed in any and all sorts of material. In this itistance it was made of a very rough almost cream coloured frieze so thick as to require only a very thin silk lining, a; :d yet quite light in weight. It is made loosely so as to be easily slipped on, and is, long enough to come to a few inches of the hem of • the dress it covers. The collar is strapped with silk on mouse coloured velvet, and dull gold buttons form the only other decorative touch, for the success cf the coat is left to the excellency of its cut. This style of garment should be always executed by a tailor as it requires a certain style of pressing to make it fit, and is beyond the skill of most women workers to turn out such a garment in a manner which will ensure approval. There are some girls who do not care for rough look- ing material such as the frieze I have de. scribed, but prefer smooth dull surfaced cloths, of which there are always a plentiful tariety to select from.</p>
        <p>The 37th Annual Trades Union Congress commenced its sitting on Monday. Thera were 743 delegates present representing 1,422,518 members. An official welcome was given by the Lord Mayor of Leeds who was accompanied on the platform by the Lady Mayoress, Sir John Gorst, the Countess of Warwick and many other prominent ladies and gentlemen. Mr. R. Bell, M.P. the President, occupied the chair and in a few remarks -nye some interesting facts as to the attendance at the Congress. The Lord Mayor was cordially received on rising to address the Congress. He explained that he was suffer- ing from ill-health and his doctor had forbidden him undergoing any special excitement or exertion but that he could not refrain from coming home as he had done to meet and welcome them there that day and tender- ing to them on behalf of the citizens of Leeds the most hearty welcorae on this occasion.</p>
        <p>In the afternoon about 600 or 700 availed themselves of an invitation from the Marquis of Ripon to visit the beautiful grounds surrounding the ancient Abbey of Fountains. After tea the Marquis gave a speech, in which he said !he was glad they had accepted his invita- tion to come and visit the Abbey. It was to his ad- vant4ge to know what were the views and desires of the working classes. It would be impossible for men like him to discharge the duties they had to discharge if they did not know the views of others. Fifty-two years no. proceeded the Marquis, when he was a boy of 25 years of age he began his public life by fighting a battle of trade union. In a strike of the Amalgamated Society of Engineers he took a great in- terest and he got into a scrape about it but he had not felt very penitent. Since that time he had endeavoured to interest himself in the working classes—a term he did not like but he coild not find a better one. He said he looked on industri..l conflicts as he did on inter- national ones. War was a barbarous method, so were strikes and lock-outs. He was not prepared to say that all wars were not justified nor would he say there were not some strikes for which there was justification but what he wanted to impress was the fact that he had always advocated arbitration in International disputes. He did not advocate compulsory arbitration but he did think that the side which refused the fair offer in an industrial controversy wits in the wrong. Mr. BELL M.P., moved a vote of thanks to the Marquis and the meeting terminated. The visitors spent the remainder of the afternoon in an interesting study of the Abbey Ruins and the surrounding grounds. Tuesdays meeting opened with the President's address. Since their last meeting death had taken two prominent figures from among them namely Mr. Pickard M.P., and Mr. Cowey,both officials of the Yorkshire Miners' Asso- ciation and their sympathy was extended to the Miners' Federation over the loss they had sustained. Hesaid the Government continued their military operations in Somaliland and up to last month their peaceful mission in Tibet was in progress. In both places lives had been sacrificed-and money squandered, and it was particularly interesting to observe the title given to the Thibetan affair—a peaceful Mission in which over 2,000 lives had been lost. When the same people who supported that mission sneered at the term " peaceful persuasions "in connection with the trades dispute bill, they must evidently calculate other peoples methods by their own. The Parliamentary Committee had been active in the deplorable business of Chinese Labour. The Govern- ment had from the commencement to the end deceived both parliament 'and country. They must have had the employment of Chinese in view when they went to war with the Transvaal in 1899. If not, then greater credit must be given to this Congress for being more far-seeing, for at Huddersfield in 1900 the question was there discussed and an expres- sion of opinion given that when the war was over the wages on the Rand would be much lower than they were before the war commenced. They now offered white men the wages previously paid to Kaffirs. This was quite clear to everyone except the Government and their supporters who would tiling the same state of things into the industrial affairs of this country if they dared. The Taff Vale decision had played a very active part since our last Congress. The South Wales Miners Federation has been hit to the tune of £57,000. The posesion of trades unions is now serious. " Peaceful persuasion "is held to be illegal and criminal. If an officer of a local branch,however subordinate he may be acquiesced in such persuasion the union can be made answerable by heavy damages. Under all the disabili- ties fram whieh they suffered, brought about through!a policy newly and recently adopted by the judges, the Prime Minister and the Government remained quiescent. Meanwhile the Royal Commission on Trades disputes dragged leisurely on. Though this Commission was appointed in June last year it had only examined about 4 witnesses.ln con clusi on,he said,let them buckle on their armour, and be determined to guard their interests and protect their rights, and not remain indifferent to the action of those unjust and confiscatory persons. As they put this Government into power let them determine to chuck them out."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>TRADES UNION CONGRESS.</head>
        <p>THE TRADES UNION CONGRESS.</p>
        <p>Thirty-seventh Annual Gathering at Leeds.</p>
        <p>THE NEW MARDI. SHOT DEAD-</p>
        <p>A" Morning Post "tele': ram from Alexandria, dated Septenaber 2nd states :—lt now appears that the new Mandi, who recently proclaimed himself at Wad Medant. on he Blue Nile, was shot dead by Zeki Effendi, the Egyptian official .sent to investigate the matter. Zaki Effendi was subsequently beaten to death by the fanatic's followers. ST. PAUL'S CHOIR SERMONS.</p>
        <p>The annual choir sermons were preached in St. Paul's Wesleyan Church, Runcorn, on Sunday by the Rev. S. H. Ravenscroft, of South Africa. Special music was rendered by the choir. The anthems were" lam Alpha and Omega,"" Seek ye the Lord," and" 0 praise God in His holiness " (Clarke Whitfield). There were large congregations and a splendid collection.</p>
      </div>
