        <p>, ir' 41 ... --- '''' ' -;-" ----- - - -- - 717 ,!-;77741,..., AM,AL, V.,,W..-, -:.4.', 1., :,-.. -77.1,. - • ~.I'mr. e, uptuatri."./.. - -,,,,,trviraz,=. ,-,-,ziaram.:z..,,,:u ,i.,vz..,,,,,,, . -- - - :T; N ....</p>
        <p>REVISING THE LISTS. Light Business at Warrington.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. T. Foard, revising barrister, opened the annual Revision Court at the Town Hall on Thursday morning, and has held two sittings since. The Liberals were 'represented by Mr. Joseph Howes, jun., in the absence of Mr. 4. Clayton, who was tin fortunately too unwell to attend to the work, and the Conservatives by Mr. William Holbrook and Mr. Herbert Hayes. Amongst others present were Mr. Stanley Wilson (deputy town clerk). Mr. W. H. Woodcock (assistant overseer), Mr. E. C. Cooper, (assistant overseer for Winwick), Messrs. T. J. Garnett and T. H. Aukland (relieving officers.) The business was exceptionally light, there being 95 claims in divisions 1, 2. and 3, 13 lodgers' claims,'and 67 objections. 'A PECULIAR POINT.</p>
        <p>The Liberals made a claim for a municipal vote on behalf of Mary Roberts, a widow, of Heath Place.— Applicant said her husband died on March 4th. She had been a householder and paid the rent and rates for fourteen years. Mr. HOLBROeK contended that applicant was not entitled to a municipal vote, as her husband was alive during part of the qualifying year. She was on the list of parochial electors. Mr. Ho Was : I contend that she may go on the municipal list, although her husband has only been dead two or three months. Mrs. Roberts was the tenant all The REVISING BARRISTER said applicant could not have been entitled to be placed on the municipal list except by the \death of her husband. Could Mr. Howes quote any case bearing on the point? Mrs. Roberts was clearly entitled to the parochial vote, but he could not see how-she was entitled to the municipal vote unless Mr. Howes could quote a case or refer him to the words of the statute dealing with such cases. —Mr. HOWES : It is practically on a par with succes- sion hy marriage. The BARRISTER: That is just the difficulty to my mind. The words in that section of the statute do not appear to me to cover this particular case. I cannot deal with the equity of the case. I must deal with the precise words of the statute. After further consideratian the Barrister told Mrs. Roberts that he could only give her the parochial vote,. Mrs. ROBERTS: I think it is a very great injustice. I have been a ratepayer for seventeen years and paid £2O rates. The BARRISTER: Two persons cannot be on the list for the same house. Mrs. ROBERTS: My husband had not been on the list for seventeen years. The BARRISTER (to Mr. Holbrook) : I understood you to say he was. Mr. HOLBROOK : No. She has been the tenant for years, but her husband only died in March. Therefore TOR cannot transfer her from the parochial to the municipal list. The BARRISTER said it was very true this was a case. of apparent hardship, but he dill not know that he could remedy it. However, he would look into the statute and give his decision later. THE WORKHOUSE MASTER'S VOTE. Mr. J. T. AWBERY, master of the Workhouse applied for a vote—Questioned by Mr. Howes he said he took possession of the house temporarily on July Ist, 1903. He commenced his duties on July 19th. The date of his sppointment was June 17th and the Guardians being -without a master asked him to take possession on July Ist. As he had not completed his engagement in another town he was only able to take temporary possession until July 19th. Mr. HOIVES : Have you any furniture there at all? Mr. AWBERY : No. Our furniture is found by the ‘Guardians. The vote was allowed. LIGEIT BUSINESS. _</p>
        <p>Mr. HOLBROOK remarked that the courts would be very light. Unfortunately Mr. Clayton was•ill. The BARRISTER: lam very sorry to hear it. Mr. HOLBROOK said he had mat Mr. Howes anti com- pared the various claims &amp;c. The Court was then adjourned.</p>
        <p>'An Evening Sitting. Mr. Foard held an evening sitting at the Town Hall on Thursday evening. HIS MOTHER-IN-LAW THE TENANT.</p>
        <p>The Conservatives made a claim on behalf of James Worrall 32, Cartwright street. In answer to Mr. J. Howes,Mr. Worrall admitted that his mother-in-law was the tenant of the house.—The Revising Barrister: You will have to get that altered.—Mr. Holbrook contended that this was a case where a son-in-law was keeping his mother-in-law.—The Revising Barrister held that this was not a case like that .of a son supporting and keeping his mother. The only point was whether the mother-in-law would object to her name being taken •Off the register, because she WM actually prima facie entitled to the vote as her name was on the rent book. Do you think she would object? The Applicant: No, she would not.—The Revising Barrister: It is a difficult case. If you want to get on the voters' list you must get her written consent to her name being taken out of the rent book. THE ASSISTANT LIBERAL AGENT'S VOTE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Frank Plurnpton, assistant Liberal registration agent,applied for a lodger vote.—Mr. Holbrook: What rooms do you . occupy ?—The applicant: Two front roorns.-31r..Holbrook : What do you pay for them ? Applicant :- Sixteen shillings per week.—Mr. Holbrook: That includes attendance, washing, food and everything? —Applicant : Everything.—The Revising Barrister: Thel claim is inaccurate in the first place, and in the next I am afraid there is an actual deficiency in amount. I cannot allow the claim. A LODGER IN A PUBLIC-HOUSE.</p>
        <p>The Conservatives claimed a lodger vote on behalf of of Levi Riley, of 120, 'Buttermarket-street.—Mr. Holbrook explained that Riley worked at Manchester and was unable to be back in time to support the appli- cation.—A young lady who said she was a" friend' of the applicant, in answer to questions by Mr. Howes, said 31r. Riley came home each evening and had two rooms for his exclusive use.—The Assistant Overseer</p>
        <p>-said this was a public-house, of which Mrs. Riley was the licensee. It was assessed at £7O gross.—The Revis- ing Barrister said it was very doubtful whether a son •could have two rooms for his entire use in a public- house. He would leave the matter open for the appear- ance of the applicant. A SON .GETS A LODGER VOTE. The Conservatives made a claim on behalf of George Mackey of 60, Bewsey-street for a lodger vote.—Mr. Howes: Do you live with your mother ?—Applicant: Ves.—Mr. Howes: It is her house. What rooms do you keep -solely for your own use ? Applicant: The front sitting-room and the back bedroom.—Mr. Howes: Whose furniture is it ?—Applicant: Part of it is mine. —The Revising Barrister:' What is the gross value of the house ?—The Assistant Overseer: £22.—The Revis- ing 'Barrister (to applicant) : Are you the sole lodger? —Applicant : Yes.—The Revising Barrister: I shall be 'inclined to allow it. _lt is al very, very close case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>FATHER AND SOS.</head>
        <p>FATHER AND SOS.</p>
        <p>The' Conservatives supported a claim made by Thomas Prince for a lodger votein respect of a house in St. Mary- street.Mr.Thomas Prince,sen., appeared on behalf of his soa.lielpOinted out that 4t. was his wish to relinquish his vote in erespect of the, house at St. Mary-street and obtain one for his business premises in Horsemarket- street. His son assisted him in the business and at home 'he had the exclusive use of tw3 rooms.—Mr. Howes pointed out that Mr. Prince ,sen had already a vote for St. Mary-street.—Mr. Holbrook said he had filed a duplicate applying for Mr. Prince to be trans- ferred to Town Hall. ward for a vote in respect of his business premises.—The Revising Barrister allowed the transfer to be made and also allowed the lodger claim made by the son. RELIEF TO A 'FATHER DISQUALIFIES A SON. The Conservatives claimed a vote for Thomas Titchard, of 16, Birchall-street.—Mr. Howes asked if there had been any relief given in this case.—Mr. Garnett (reaming officer) said he had paid relief to the father of time applioant.—Mr. Holbrook contended that relief paid to the father could not affect a son's claim for a vote.—The Revising Barrister said relief paid to a father disqualified a son just as though the son him- self had received the relief.—Theclaim was disallowed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>Yesterday's Proceedings. LODGER CLAIMS DISALLOWED.</head>
        <p>Yesterday's Proceedings. LODGER CLAIMS DISALLOWED.</p>
        <p>The claim of Levi Riley for a lodger vote in respect of two rooms occupied 'by him at 120, Buttermarket- street, was again broughtforward. letter was handed to the Revising Barrister -from Mrs. Riley, the mother of the applicant, in which she stated that her son returned home from his work at Manchester each even- ingand had his meals bytimself in. his own room.—Mr. Howes said the applicant.could•hardly be a dutiful son if he had all his meals away from the family.—The Revising Barrister said the applicant claimed for the sole and undivided oocupation,of two rooms. The only suggestion was that it was an inaccurate description, which would be fatal to a lodgerolaim. The letter, .so far as it went, suggested that Mr. -Riley had the sole occupation of two .rooms and took his meals bv 'himself. On the face of .it it hardly looked probable that a son coming home should take his meals by himself, away from his mother and not inhabit the common room.—Mr. 'Holbrook pointed out that Mr. Riley left Warrington each morn- ing by the five minates to nine train and returned at six o'clock.—Mr. Hayes, assistant registration agent was called and stated that the reason why Mr. Riley dined by himself was because the room downstairs was a very small one. It was late when Mr. Riley returned 'home</p>
        <p>...._ . ....._...... and he had his meals by himself in his oxvn room.—The Revising Barrister said he must use his commonsense in the matter. He was afraid he must still refuse the claim.</p>
        <p>The Liberals supported a claim made by William Henry Howard, for a lodger vote. He stated that he paid 16s. for board and lodgings and occupied two rooms.—Mr. Howes pointed out that this was a case where the applicant had some of his meals out of the honse.—The Revising Barrister observed that the statute said the applicant must have premises of certain value and he Rota vote in respect of the premises he occupied —Mr. Howes said the 16s. a week covered the £lO for premises.--The Revising Barrister: We must- know what the tenancy ie for. It must be an absolute and bona fide tenancy. That is the difficulty, a lump sum pail for board and lodging does not enable us to deter- aline what the tenancy is.—The claim was disallowed. The Cou,rt rillst,again this rooming.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, September 10, 1904. NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, September 10, 1904. NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>tlx W, NODGKINSON LT'? BKWG TT,</p>
        <p>Last night I learned that Fish will be an absentee I J. Ashton had the best of his encounter with J. from to-day's team as well as Harris. He has been Unsworth. The last-named displayed good form, and at "shaking a loose leg"and is consequently not eligible a4h6os own ndlo the spores were .equal. Ashton had matters under the working clause. Hockenhall will probably- way in the remaining stages and claimed the play wing and Bate come in at half. Boardman and victory by 5 points. Naylor will also be missing through illness, Harmer and Lunt filling the vacancies</p>
        <p>A. Shaw proved too good for T. Bridge, and though the game was at times exciting, won by 15-10. That well-known enthusiast J. Willett found a doughty opponent in J. E. Jefferson, who gained a well-deserved success by 15-11.</p>
        <p>Harry Williams triumphed over T. Bolton by 15-10. T. Berry was outplayed by J. Monks who placed a margin of 7to his credit. G. Ball, the veteran trundler, showed that he can still give a good account of himself with the woods, and his opponent, J. Worsley, had no chance.</p>
        <p>T. Rigby experienced a severe drubbing at the hands of J. Horrabin. The scores of 15-4 speak for them- selves. G. Holbrook began well in his game with A. J. Jones, but did not maintain his form and was beaten by 15-7. Herman Williams had an easy task in his trial of skill with T. Brooks who made a very poor show and was beaten by 15-4.</p>
        <p>A word of praise must be accorded to Mr. Parker, the head gardener, who had superintended the laying of the green. The work has been admirably done, and was generally commented upon on Saturday.</p>
        <p>In addition to the .25 offered by Mr. E. England, the Mayoress has given a donation of £5 towards prizes to be competed for by local bowlers.</p>
        <p>The annual bowling main in connection with the Junior Conservative Club for the prizes kindly offered by the President (Mr. Roger C. Parr), took place on Saturday afternoon on the green at Orford-lane. The prize winners were: 1, A. Field • 2, H. Wells ;3, G. Molynenx • 4, A. Perry; 5, H. Taylor: 6, T. Hackett; 7, R. Knight; 8, J. Langley ; 9, R. Sutton; 10, F. Wilkinson ; 11, T. Clayton; 12, R. Sunderland.</p>
        <p>The closing main in connection with the Lovely-lane Bowling Club was held on Saturday last. The members turned up in good numbers, and throughout the after- noon there was some very good bowling. The follow- ing is a list of prize winners :-Ist, T. Litherland ; 2nd, J. %V. Willett; 3rd, J. Wilson; 4th, Harry Cook; sth, J. Millman; 6th, F. Walker; 7th, M. Johnson; Bth, N. Cook, jun. ; 9th, W. Dutton; 10th, J. H. Dutton; 11th, Alf. Dennett ; 12th, William Sanders; 13th, R. W. Cook; 14th, E. Walker. The prizes were kindly pre- sented to the Club by A. H. Crosfield, Esq., Mr. Coun- cillor James Evans,Mr. Nathan Cook, Mr. J. Booth, Mr. James Crozier, and Miss M. Dutton, respectively.</p>
