      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>DEPARTURE IN SHOP-BUILDING. A VISIT OF INSPECTION TO MESSRS. HODGKINSON'S PREMISES.</head>
        <p>A DEPARTURE IN SHOP-BUILDING. A VISIT OF INSPECTION TO MESSRS. HODGKINSON'S PREMISES.</p>
        <p>Bridge-street as our grandfathers knew it is slowly but surely passing from our view. The narrow and winding thoroughfare of centuries ago with its shops in their quaint and homely architecture of stout oak beams bulging walls and numerous gables, topped with fantastic finials is rapidly disappearing and giving place to a wide and busy thoroughfare, bor- dered by palatial business establishments, sumptu- ously furnished and appointed in a manner which our grandfathers could never have dreamt of. This transformation is proceeding apace and ere long we shall have a6O feet thoroughfare from the historic Market Gate to Bridge Foot. Our illustration shows the new premises of Messrs. Hodgkinson Limited,and they are typical of the change that is coming over Bridge street. The business of Messrs. W. Hodgkinson, Limited, has grown even more rapidly than the town itself. By adding furnishing and upholstering to their already extensive drapery and general outfitting business. Messrs. Hodgkinson found it necessary in the past to pcquire additional premises in other parts of Bridge- street. . When the time arrived for the demolition of the shops to allow of the widening of the thorough- fare, Mr. Hodgkinson, fully alive to the necessities of the future, decided that the time had arrived whenl steps should be taken to erect a building where all the business could be carried on under one roof. Being in possession of the land upon which the business premises stood an adjoining plot at the rear was secured, upon which stood the Eagle and Child public- house, stables, &amp;c. Instructions were finally given to the architects to design a building, calculated to meet all requirements. Operations were commenced in the summer of 1903. The old buildings were speedily demolished and' then commenced the difficult task of under-pinning the whole of the distillery buildings on the south side belonging to Messrs. G. and J. Greenall. The work was carried out uncle( circumstances of great difficulty and danger but it was accomplished in a few weeks without accidents of any kind. The erection of the rear portion of the new premises was then proceeded with, but owing to the decision of the Corporation to widen the continuation of Barbauld-street, across Rylands-street, some delay was occasioned and it was found necessary to take down a portion of the premises already erected. -</p>
        <p>The completed portion of the premises represents two-thirds of the entire whole. The business has been transferred here and will be continued uninterrupted during the completion of the building operations.A de- vice has been resorted to whereby the old shop windows can be used for display purposes, pending the erection of the new frontage, and a covered passage provides an entrance from Bridge-street. As will be seen from the illustration, the new buildings will have a hand- some frontage to Bridge-street and a portion of Patten-lane. It is to be built in Free Renaissance style of architecture, and will be faced with Berman- toft's semi-vetrified buff terra-cotta, whilst a portion along Patten-lane will be faced,. with white enamel bricks. An arcade has been provided along Patten- lane which will enable Messrs. Hodgkinsou to enlarge their window display and provide a great attraction for those who enjoy shop-window gazing. A reporter of "The Examiner" was privileged to pay a visit of inspection to the new premises one day, this week. The business was proceeding with- out the least confusion or inconvenience under the superintendence of Mr. Hodgkinson, and the staff, assistants, and workpeople seemed to be already accustomed to their new quarters.</p>
        <p>The building, including the basement, is four storeys high with an attic in addition over a portion of the premises. Accompanied by Mr. H. Hallard, the Clerk of Works for Messrs. Hodgkinson, a round of inspec- tion was made. Commencing in the basement, here were found Mr. Hodgkinson's private office, counting- house, strong room, assistants' dining-room, scullery, cloak-rooms, heating chamber, dynamo-room, and goods store, where goods will be received from the manufacturers and afterwards passed up the lift to the several departments. The ground floor may be said to be the shop proper. The front portion is de- voted to the drapery and outfitting business and the rear to the furnishing department. Ascending a magnificent oak staircase, with two approaches, a balcony is reached from which a good view is ob- tained of the whole of the ground floor. A beautiful glass dome 'throws a flood of light over the building and adds greatly to the beauty of the interior. Pro- ceeding up the staircase, the showrooms are reached. Here are some well-appointed fitting rooms, and when the buildings are completed there will be provided a tea room, writing room and other conveniences for customers. It may also be stated that the front portion of the basement will be formed into a magnificent cafe, of palatial dimensions, with an entrance from Bridge-street, which it is anticipated will be one of the finest of its kind. On the second floor are workrooms fitted and equipped in the most up-to-date manner and divided into their several departments. We are informed that they are second to none of their kind. Here were to be seen a large number of young women and girls with smiling faces busily engaged in dressmak- ing and millinery. Great attention has been paid to the heating, lighting and ventilation of the rooms so as to make the conditions of employment as comfort- able and healthy as possible. An outside iron stair- case provides an additional means of escape in case of fire. The top. floor is devoted to the upholstering department, adjoining which is a dining room for all the work girls. Each sales department is connected with the count- ing-house by the Lamson pneumatic tube system which is the first of its kind to be erected in this part of the country. It rapidly conveys the cash from the counter to the lady in charge of the cash desk in the counting-house. The general arrangement of the rooms is admirably suited to the requirements of the business, and when the buildings are finally com- pleted, Messrs. Hodgkinson will be in possession of a model business establishment. The facilities afforded will no doubt tend to further increase Messrs. Hodgkinson's wide circle of customers. The works are being carried out aceording to the designs and under the supervision of Messrs. J. E. Wright, Garnett and Wright, architects, of Sankey- street. The builder is Mr. C. W. Davenport, of Stockton Heath. Mr. W. Hallard is the clerk of works. It is interesting to note that the shop windows, oak staircase and shop fittings have been executed by Messrs. Hodgkinson's own workmen at their cabinet manufactory in Wharf-street, off Mersey- street. The sub-contractors have been as follows: plastering, Messrs. G. Osborne and Sons; heating and ventilation, Messrs. Saunders and Taylor, Manchester; electric lighting, Messrs Ingrams,Limited; gas heaters and hot water supply,Messrs. Fletcher,Russell and Co.; painting, Messrs. S. and H. Chandley ; fire-prooooors, Messrs. Mather and Platt, Manchestor ; woot-T-llock floors, Mr. Roger Lowe, Bolton: glass, Messrs. Williams and Wilson, Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>TRADES UNION CONGRESS.</head>
        <p>TRADES UNION CONGRESS.</p>
        <p>(Continued from, Page 7.)</p>
        <p>Conscription and the Fiscal Question.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday the Congress considered several ques- tions which have a wider significance than those con- cerned entirely with the organisation of labour, and during these discussions the great meeting was quite at its best. The two principal subjects dealt with were conscription and Free Trade. The recently published Report of the Royal Commission gave Mr. John Ward, of the Navvies' Union, an opportunity to move a resolution which was one not only of protest against conscription in any form, however gentle might be its early application, but also of congratulation on our newer successes on the establishment of arbitration agreements. Mr. Ward's speech was throughout a con- demnation of war itself and of excessive preparations for war. All the burdens of militarism, he pointed out, fell inevitably on the shoulders of the people, and they who bear most gain least—if anything at all is gained—from • the struggle. The most interest- ing thing connected with this excellent speech was to observe the manner in which every point was taken up and accepted with cheers from the great body of the delegates. The hall was full and there were many of the Leeds public both on the platform and in the galleries. Mr. Ward alluded delic- ately to the visits of the King to Continental Powers, and his suggestion that His Majesty is almost the only statesman in the country, at least with any sort of authority, was approved heartily. The sentiment of loyalty was sincere. Against this resolution there was, indeed, not one dissentient voice. Mr. Alderman , Bowerman, of London, and Mr. Fenwick, M.P., were equally clear and emphatic on the .value to working Tina and women of Free Trade. There are few "fiscal reformers" amongst organised labour men, and the opinion of the Congress was so unani- mous that Mr. Fenwick had to ask that time might be allowed in which to make their position perfectly plain. This he did by recapitulating some of the facts which destroy the case of the "reformers," and by pointing out that this latest essay is only one of a series made by Mr. Chamberlain in his various attempts to divert the national mind in such a manner as to secure public ,confidence at thepolls. Besides questions of large public interest, many were I dealt with which are of moment in labour organisation. The abolition of nighf labour in bake-houses, the closer association with trade unionism of the co-operative movement, the alteration of the conditions • of employ- ment for railway clerks on Sundays, Factory Act reform, and the amendment. of the Shop Clubs Act were ' among these. Deputations were received from the HORSEWHIPPING A LADY. American trade unionists, the General Federation of Two well-dressed ladies met outside the Manchester I Trade Unions, and the Labour Representation Committee I Exchange on Wednesday, and after a heated converse- tion one produced a dog whip and severely thrashed This last body has now affiliated to it organisations with, In round figures, a illion members, and Mr. J. ft the other. The smitten lady escaped into a shop and m Macdonald, the secretary of the Committee, said it left by the back door, thus avoiding further chastise- wouldll want from her assailant, who waited some time in look to the Congress for co-operation in the front of theshop. The lidy with the dog whip toldthe important movement which will so affect the future of the labour cause. f police she had cause to be jealous of the rival she had The work of the delegates on Thursday was concerned • b d .puBA e • mainly with trade union questions, and so much.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <p>was got through that a mere summary of it would con- very little meaning. It had to do mainly with the establishment of more complete safety for workpeople during their hours of labour, and with the improvement of the conditions of many departments of labour under the Government. The Miners' Federation, for instance, asked for the prevention of theemployment of unskilled men in mines. This raised, incidentally, theqnestion of the employment there of foreigners. It is, perhaps, as well to make it clear that the objection against them had nothing to do with their nationality, but to the danger to everybody underground when men with- out any knowledge of mining are sent into the pits. Several amendments to the Mines Regulations Acts were asked for, ands closer inspection required of small houses in which home occupations are carried on. Important amendments to the Workmen's Compensation Act were also suggested. There was for some three- quarters of an hour in the morning a pleasant interlude when the proposal to establish a labour newspaper was discussed. A good many editorial opinions were expressed and financial views put forward. It was decided by a small majority to make the attempt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL</p>
        <p>THE CRIdKET CM:B.—The destination of the First Eleven was Hough Green. Of Grappenhall's three defeats, one was suffered at the hands of Saturday's opponents, so something had to be wiped off the slate, and it was done. Watson was in form with the ball, and Hough Green were all dismissed for 26. The visitors' total reached 90. They thus came away easy winners by 64 run.—On Thursday afternoon,in dull and showery weather, a match was played against an eleven from Orford Barracks, in which the military men were beaten.—With to-day's match, the return with Lostock Gralam the list of fixtures is exhausted and another cricket season comes to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>TRE TRAMWAY EXTENTSION. -The work on the Swing 13ridge in connection with the laying of the tram lines is now being proceeded with. The whole of the wood pavement on the bridge is being taken up in arder that a firm foundation for the new lines will be ensured. As the work is very intricate and delicate it is arousing much interest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>Rugby Football.</head>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>(By " Ovalist.' )</p>
        <p>The football season opened with a rare flourish of trumpets on Saturday,and at 3.35 in the afternoon the Northern Union games were in full swing. Good "crates" were recorded everywhere,the genial weather and the fact that before the first match everybody is full of hope and confidence, being responsible for this happy result. Later in the day many teams found their confidence misplaced, and in the senior league alone no less than six were defeated and humbled on their own enclosures: This is an event that is not likely to happen again throughout the League tourna- ment. Warrington justified the high hopes enter- tained of their capacity by winning easily at Leigh and recording the highest score of the day. The town was therefore not one of those centres into which the entry of football brought weeping and wailing and gnashing of teeth.</p>
        <p>Still exultation at the opening success was tinged with some real regrets which took a lot of the cream off the cake. Though the local champions recorded a 16 points to 2 points victory, it was, in a s3nse, dearly won. Elliot Harris, who scored two tries in splendid style,very badly, twisted his knee when completing his second effort and had to be carried from the field. In all probability he will be several weeks before he done the jersey again. It will be well remembered how his enforced absence last season through injury, crippled the attacking powers of the side on many- occasions. The Fates,are certainly unkind to War- rington's right-winger, but he might have avoided the eccident on Saturday if he had put the ball down immediately he crossed the line instead of trying to get round to the goal. Harris always was a " thrutcher". however, and I suppose he will remain one to the end of the chapter. Add to Harris's injury the fact that Preston and Davies were b3th ordered off, and it will be realised that Warrington's first two points were expensive ones.</p>
        <p>There was much in the game to give Warrington supporters cause for rejoicing nevertheless. Leigh had laid themselves out to give Warrington a surprise, and they succeeded but only by the easy way in which they collapsed when pressure was applied to their defence. Warrington have usually been beaten forward at Leigh, but on Saturday the boot was on the other leg. When the visiting forwards particularly desired the ball they got it, and altogether proved themselves a stronger eight than have represented Warrington for some years. With the front rank doing thus well it was not to be wondered at that the backs gave Leigh something to think about. They showed all their old speed and individual cleverness,while the combination was only slightly below par from short practice. The display of the team was exceedingly gratifying from a Warrington point of view.</p>
        <p>There is ' generally a gale of wind cavorting round Leigh, and rude Boreas was strongly in evidence on Saturday, so much so that the side having his assist- ance was sure of keeping the other side at bay. Leigh were lucky enough to win the toss and they set them- selves out with energy to make points while the wind blew. It was soon evident, however, that they had neither skill nor combination enough to beat the War- rington defence and a penalty goal was all that accrued from their efforts, while Harris nearly put them in a miniority with a dashing effort which failed on the line. When half time arrived there were pro- bably few people on the ground who had any great hopes of the home team.</p>
        <p>For ten minutes of the second half they kept War- rington near mid-field, but eventually the ball came smartly across to Harris, who dashed over the line with the first try of tho season. This practically settled Leigh's fate, but when a moment later, War- rington's biggest and newest forward, Preston, was ordered off by the keferee for some infringement in the scrummage, the hopes of the Leigh men rose. All in vAin ! Harris put paid to Preston's account with a second try, and though he had to be carried off Leigh were a lost side, especially when Fish goaled from an awkward angle. Leigh made a dash to the other end, only to see Warrington's gallant thirteen come back ,undaunted to take three more points through the agency of Dickenson. Then the referee interfered once more and Davies (Warrington) and Blackburn (Leigh) were sent to the touch-line to settle their differences. Left with only twelve men Warrington still attacked and to the disgust of the home crowd they easily outclassed the opposition. Fish cleverly robbed Burgess and ran behind the posts, afterwards kicking his second goal, while if Isherwood had not passed badly when he had cleared the way the left-winger would probably have been credited with a further five points. It was a regular Liao-Yang slaughter for Leigh!</p>
        <p>Speaking of individual performances the only man on the Warrington side who did not display good form was Hallam. Perhaps the cares of captaincy weighed heavily on his shoulders, but whatever the cause be showed little of his old reliability. I have no doubt, however, that he will soon come into that form which has won for him the reputation of being one of the steadiest backs in the Northern Union. He has a fine side under him, and with average lack the Warrington team this seasm should prove itself a great one.</p>
        <p>Leigh seem to me to be some distance up Queer-st. They had only one new man on view last Saturday, a three-quarter named Gartland, of Maryport, and he does not seem likely to electrify the Mather-lane enthusiasts. The old hands are not improving., and there will need to be some stirring about if Leigh are not to be struggling once more to avoid being choked by the wooden spoon. Dunbavin gave a smart dis- play and without him the home side must have been simply crushed. He was c9rtainly the best of the fifteen, and did not deserve to be left off," as he is this week. Burgess has met .with a similar fate, and yet he strove hard against better men. However there will have to be some bold experimenting if Leigh are to pick up points.</p>
        <p>• The " A. " team of Leigh succumbed pretty easily at Wilderspool, where the locals were too speedy ana smart or the visitors, even with Brookes and Short absentees. Savage got a couple of fine tries and gives promise of making a dashing wing man. Kenyon has yet to score his first try, though he came near it more than once. Perhaps he is reserving the great effort for to-day.</p>
        <p>The season at Warrington opens in real earnest this afternoon when Warrington and Halifax will renew their acquaintanca, with a fresh memory of that des- parate struggle at the Willows for the Northern Union Cup. Halifax were certainly top dogs that day, but doubt whether they will be suchall-conquering heroes to-day. Warrington have buttressed up the ramparts so to speak,durins the interim,and the Halifax forwards may run up against something hard and hurtful this time. They had a very "soft thing" in the cup final.</p>
        <p>Warrington will nAt be so badly off for the match as many people have feared during the week. Preston and Davies are both eligible and fit; as their cases do not come up till Tuesday. next. It is suite an erroneous idea that they cannot play before the meeting follow- ing their cmflict with the teferee. Harris is the only absentee from the full side, and Kenyon will thus get his opportunity on the right wing.</p>
        <p>Harris is at present an inmate of Mt` Allison's establishment in Manchester. His knee has been badly inflamed but Mr. Allison reports that he is going on satisfactorily. There is reason to hope that the injury may not keep Harris out of the team more than another week or two.</p>
        <p>Warrington's full side for the match with Halifax full back ; Fish, Isherwood, to-day is: Dickenson and Kenyon, threequarters ; Davies and Hockenhall, half-backs; Preston, Boardman, Shugars, Belton, Thomas, Jolley, Naylor and Cooke, forwards.</p>
        <p>Another forward of weight and ability is anxious to leave Wales for the more salubrious air of Warrington but the terms are decidedly " Igh." There may be more news later.</p>
        <p>Business is booming in the cash department of the Football Club. Over 100 members have joined already, and the cry IS" still they come ! "</p>
        <p>By the way Shugars is the heaviest forward in the team and has played seven times for Glamorgan County.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>Water Polo.</head>
        <p>Water Polo.</p>
        <p>Warrington proved conquerors on Thursday evening when St. Helens 2nd opposed them at the Legh-street Baths. The attendance of the public was much smaller than usual, but members of the fair sex assembled in good force. The visitors were completely outplayed, Warrington registering goal after goal in surprisingly quick succession. A. Ashton opened the scoring soon after the start, and before the interval the St. Helens custodian (Parr) was beaten by Boscow, Sutton, and Peers (2). Towards half-time the Saints did their utmost to score but could not pierce the home defence.</p>
        <p>• Warrington defended the deep end in the second-half but maintained the advantage, and Sutton and Peers added goals. The result was: Warrington 7 goals, St. Helens nil. Praise must be given to both goal-keepers (Woods and Parr) for although the St. Helens goal was so hotly assailed, Parr was applauded for a number of fine saves, The good work of Woods is evidenced by the fact that the viSitors did not send in a single successful shot.</p>
        <p>The second team tried conclusions with Chester, but suffered defeat to the extent of two goals to nil. Meredith was the scorer.</p>
        <p>Two members of the Warrington Swimming Club achieved success at the Bootle Swimming Club's gala. K. Allen secured first prize in the open (two lengths) handicap with 12secs. start, and C. Turner (11secs.) was fourth in the same event.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>Cycling.</head>
        <p>Cycling.</p>
        <p>The charming river-side village of Hale was the Warrington Cycling Club's destination on Saturday. There was a high wind, but the weather kept fine, and the roads were in grand condition. As many of the regular offenders are away from home, there was only a moderate turnout. Tea was provided at the Childe of Hale Hotel, and afterwards bowls helped to pleasantly while away the time. The return was accomplished without incident, the outing having been thoroughly enjoyed.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening the run was to Grimsditch Mill and Stretton, and although there were prospects of stormy weather, a dozen members mustered. The days are drawing in so rapidly that it is now impossible to hold the usual evening bowling matches. In the absence of several musical friends the pleasure of an impromptu concert was also denied the wheelers on Tuesday. Des- pite these disadvantages the evening passed enjoyably enough, and Warrington was reached in good time.</p>
        <p>Another week or two and we shall have said" good- bye" to the season of 1904. Only six unfulfilled fixtures remain on the Warrington Club's card, and October Ist will witness the closing run to Knutsford. On the whole the season has been a decided success, and one of which the members will long retain pleasant recollections.</p>
        <p>LIGHTING-UP TIME.</p>
        <p>To-night Sunday • 7.37 Monday ' 7.35 Tuesday 7.33 Wednesday Thursday Friday</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>Angling.</head>
        <p>Angling.</p>
        <p>The first stage of the competition for the Worsley Shield, in connection with the Worsley Fishery Board was decided in two sections on Saturday afternoon last; one at Worsley and the other at Stockton Heath, War- rington. There were eighteen teams entered for tie Worsley section and twenty-four for the Warrington section and the teams registering the heaviest gross weight in each section were allowed the privilege to fish off in the final stage in a neutral water. A spirited contest ensued and at Worsley the Robin Hood Angling Society of Clifton recorded the highestiaggregate weight which was 11b, 2ozs, 9dr5., whilst at Stockton Heath, the Rope and Anchor Angling Society registered the highest total which was 21bs, 15ozs, sdrs. These two teams will consequently fish off for the final tie on Saturday next at Timperley, commencing at 4 o'clock in the afternoon. May the team representing Warring- ton win.</p>
        <p>In connection with the above was also decided the championship contest in connection with the Worsley Canal Fishery, the prizes being given for the heaviest individual weights recorded by the competitors in the whole competition—at Worsley and Stockton Heath taken collectively. Appended is the prize list, which is published subject to a scrutiny. Ist, T. H. Ogden, Salford, 11b. 2ozs. 2dr5.; 2nd, J. Mather, Warrington, 11b. loz. 2drs. ; 3rd,J. Rowell, Warrington,lsozs. 10drs.; 4th, J. Nicholson, Warrington, 15ozs. 4drs. ; sth, J. Chadwick, Warrington, 14ozs. lldrs. 6th,H. Moore,War- rington, 13ozs. sdrs. ; 7th,J. Cooke, Warrington, 10ozs. 4drs. ; Bth, S. Thomas, Warrington, 9oz; lldrs. •' 9th, J. ' Lawton, Warrington, 9ozs. 9drs. • 10th, J. W. Starkey Warrington, 9ozs, sdrs. •, 11th, J. Sanson, War- rington, 9ozs. 2drs. ; 12th, J. Berry, 9oz. ldr. ; 13th, T. Worthington, Warrington, Bozs. 51drs.: 14th, H. Bertram. Pendleton, 9oz. 9drs. 9 • 15th. E. Shoebridge, Warrington, Bozs. [dr. ; 16th, B• Dobson, Warrington, 7ozs. 7drs.; 17th, G. Gilbert, War- rington, 70zs. 4drs. ; 18th, J. Ward, Warrington, 7ozs. 3drs. ; 19th, J. H. Hackney, Warrington, 20th, G. Smith, Warrington, and 21st, S. Head, Walkden, tied 6ozs. 15drs. ,• 22nd, W. Owen, Warrington, 6ozs. 14drs. : 23rd and 24th, F. Shaw and J. Traynor, Warrington, tied Gozs. 4drs. ; 25th, J. Berry, Warrington, 6ozs. ldr.</p>
        <p>The Contest was conducted at Worsley by Mr. G. S. Smeeth, secretary to the Board, and at Stockton Heath by Messrs. P. Knight and Fred J. Emson, assistant secretary to the Board, whilst Mr. A. Lawson Harkness of Manchester, officiated as referee, and the staff of bank rangers and weighers did their duties in a capable manner.</p>
        <p>The Juvenile Branch of the Warrington Anglers' Association fished their annual contest at Grappenhall on Saturday afternoon last, 59 members competing. Messrs. W. A. Proud and S. James, assisted by Mr. E. Porter conducted, and a pleasant contest resulted in Master F. Dutton being declared the winner, he record- ing the weight of 7ozs. 12drs. Only fish of stipulated standard size were eligible for weighing.</p>
        <p>The members of the Rope and Anchor Angling Society journeyed to Broken Cross. Northwich, on the 4th inst. to fish their annual contest, Messrs. Berry, Hackney and Taylor making all arralgements. An enjoyable match ensued and the following were the winners in the order named. Messrs. J. Atherton, lib 130 z., J. Ward, R. Naylor, j„ H. Hackney, J. Rowell, F. Kenny, E. Leslie, and J. Nicholson. The total weight of fish caught was 101bs 4ozs.</p>
        <p>The members of the Black Bear Angling. Society fish a match to-day at Agden Bridge. The Latchford Angling Society have altered their date of match to September 24th. The Shield Competition in connection with the Warrington Anglers' Association will be fished on Satur- day next. Any member can enrol a team and affiliated clubs for travelling facilities, any number, of teams of members who are clear upon the books. Entries should be sent in not later than Monday morning's post,to Mr. Peter Knight, the general secretary. Anglers are reminded of the open contest to be fisbed at Agden Bridge on September 24th, particulars of which have been published and circulated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>Cricket.</head>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>Warrington made a poor wind-up the season on Saturday, Preston having little difficulty in defeating them. The opening football matches robbed cricket of all interest, and there was a miserably small attendance It is not often that stumps are drawn so early as a few minutes past five, but this was the case on Saturday. With the exception of the captain, the Warrington bats- men gave a wretched display, and the last wicket fell with only 41 on the score sheet. The visitors just doubled this total, and were all dismissed for 82. This closed the match, but as a short practice it was decided to give Warrington a second innings. The venture was more successful than the first, and realized 39 for the loss of three wickets.</p>
        <p>There was nothing particularly noteworthy in either side's batting effort. E. A. Connor (15) was the only Warringtonian to attain double figures whilst H. S. Matthews (18) was the top scorer for the "proud town:' Thewlis is to be complimented upon a fine bowling per- formance, his capture being four wickets for eight runs.</p>
        <p>Sankey Wire Mills visited Glazebury and the en- counter resulted in a draw. The visitors made 100 for 5 and declared their innings, and the Glazebnry score had reached 62 for 9 when the time limit was reached. R. Cook bowled very effectively, taking 6 wickets for 24 runs.</p>
        <p>Rylands Bros' staff triumphed over Mr. G. H. W. Steel's side,N. Nield and Saunders knocking up 12 each. Boardman and Nield bowled in good style.</p>
        <p>Now that the season is concluded a complete review of the Warrington club's doings can be given :-22 matches have been played, four lost, and seven drawn. Warrington scored 2,765 runs for the loss of 167 wickets, the average working out to 1656 per wicket, against their opponents' 2,655 runs for 197 wickets-13'48 runs per wicket. The following are the season's averages :</p>
        <p>FIRST ELEVEN. BATTEcG.</p>
        <p>Time</p>
        <p>Total Most in Aver-</p>
        <p>Name. Inns, not out runs. inns, ages. P. B. Hawtin 19 1 .... 434 ... 77 ... 24•11 J. Lynes 2O ... 1 ... 444 ... *69 ... 23•36 E. A. Connor l7 ... 2 ... 210 ... *64 ... 18•00 A. Thewlis 5 ... 2 ... 54 ... 38 ... 18.00 J. W. Bibby J. Callon l7 ... 1 ... 258 ~.. 45 ... 16'12 T. Wright W. Richardson 20 ... 4 176 ... *34 ... WOO G. IL Übsdell 13 ... 2 ... 121 ... *45 ... WOO W. H. Podmore 20 ... 0 ... 197 ... 53 ... 0.85 G. Hampson J. Thompson Lieut. E. Coke 6 ... 1 ... 36 ... 17 ... 7'lo E. E. Woods B. Sikock T. Houghton F. C. Johnson 1 ... 0 ... 25 ... 23</p>
        <p>W. Hind F. Barber 1 ... 0 ... 0 ... 0</p>
        <p>W. M. Silcock 1 ... 0 ... 0 ... 0 A. Duckworth... 1 ... 0 ... *0 ... 0</p>
        <p>*Signifies not out.</p>
        <p>BOWLING.</p>
        <p>Name. Overs. Maidens. Runs. Wkts. Avge. Lynes E. AConnor 181.2 ... 42 ... 451 ... 38 ... 11.89 W H.Podmore 75 ... 9 ... 267 ... 19 ... 13.42 A. Thewlis G. H. Ur bsdell 46 ... 7 ... 150 ... 9 ... 16.16 J. W.Bibby 43 ... 2 168 ... 16 ... 22.80 T. Wright G. Hampson 17 ... 3 ... 57 ... 7 ... 814 B. Silcock E. E. Woods 0 20 ... 0</p>
        <p>H: Callon 3 ... 0 ..; -8 ... 0 S. Denton</p>
        <p>Bowling. Warrington has now followed the lead of many other towns,and next season lovers of the game will have the pleasure of trundling on our first municipal bowling green at Victoria Park. Judging from the persistency with which the Corporation were urged to undertake the experiment, the green will be much appreciated and should be the scene of many an exciting contest. Although the formal opening ceremony took place on Saturday, it was thought advisable to let the green "settle° thoroughly before allowing it to be regularly used. By the commencement of next season the turf should be in grand order, and the green will remain closed until then.</p>
        <p>The attendance at the opening ceremony was small, but this is hardly to be wondered at when there was a sixpenny admission charge. Of course, the committee's object was to prevent overcrowding, but they might well have fixed a more reasonable sum. Only a very small number of the general public put in an appear- ance. and later in the afternoon the charge was dis- pensed with.</p>
        <p>The Mayoress (Mrs. Bolton) opened the green, and amongst others present were the Mayor, Alderman and Mrs. Smethurst, Alderman Evans, and Councillors Elli- son, Ashcroft, A. Peacock, Wilson and Stott. By the way, the event must have afforded considerable gratifi• cation to Councillor Stott, for the Trades and Labour Council, of which he is such an active member, was abut the first organization to agitate for the green. • As vice-chairman of the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee, Alderman Smethurst called upon the Mayoress to open the green. He was sure it would be a source of pleasure to her to be the first Mayoress who had had the opportunity of opening a public green for the benefit of those who were so fond of the game. The Mayoress then bowled the first two woods, and his Worship followed with a third.</p>
        <p>Alderman Evans thought the competition should not be commenced until they had thanked the Mayoress for her services. Ha begged to propose, therefore, that the best thanks to all present be accorded to her. (Hear, hear, and applause.)</p>
        <p>Councillor Ellison, in seconding, said that as a repre- sentative of Latchford Ward he was delighted to welcome the Mayoress. He was pleased that Latchford had been considered the best locality for the green.</p>
        <p>The thotion was heartily agreed to, and in responding the Mayor said that so long as he was Chairman of the Town hail, Parks,and Baths Committee he would do his best to keep the green in order. If the present green was a success he had no doubt they would be able to have a few others. The present green was an experiment. There were so many greens and players in Warrington, that the experience gained in other parts of the borough would be useful here. If the public would only use the green with the same care that they would tre‘t a private green they would have no difficulty in keeping it in good order.</p>
        <p>The match between teams representing the Works Association! and Warrington and District League was then entered upon,land very interestingit proved. The Works men showed the better skill,and ran out winners by 27 points. The following are the detailed scores : LEAGUE. WORKS. J. Weston l5 R. Fairbrother T. Wells J. Wareham 5 J. Harrison C. Roberts H. Daintith l5 T. Ellison 9 J. Bowyer lO A. Gleave l5 G. Higham J. Unsworth A. Shaw J. E. Jefferson T. Bolton lO Harry Williams T. Berry G. Ball, sea. (capt). T. Rigby A. J. Jones (sub-capt.)... 15 G. Holbrook (sub-capt) 7 T. Brooks 4 Herman Williams</p>
        <p>Total 176 Total 203 Majority for works, 27.</p>
        <p>To come to the individual games, J. Weston and R. Fairbrother were the first pair to try conclusions. Weston opened the scoring, but Fairbrother had a good spell of success and eventually led 6-1. Weston played in determined style with the result that the players were level at 8 and 10. After this Weston had all the luck and ran out winner by 15-10.</p>
        <p>I T. Wells and if. Fogg were pretty well matched, but •Fogg held the upper hand throughout. Wells made a creditable effort but had to admit defeat by 15-12.</p>
        <p>; The contest between J. Wareham and J. Harrison was a runaway affair. Wareham never had a look in and so complete was Harrison's victory that he piled on a majority of 10.</p>
        <p>C. Roberts made a plucky attempt against J. W. Worsley, but the last named always kept ahead and triumphed by 15-12. H. Daintith had little difficulty in disposing of T. Ellison, who drew level at 2, but afterwards fell away and was beaten by 15-9.</p>
        <p>There was an exciting tussle betweenJ.Bowyerand A. i Gleave, and the issue was n doubt until the last few ends were decided.</p>
        <p>' Another splendid game was that in which G. Higham figured against J. Holland. At 3,8, and 10 the players were level, but after scoring another single, Higham could not improve his position and was defeated by 15-11.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
