      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</p>
        <p>Gray—Wallington.</p>
        <p>A large congregation assembled in Wycliffe Congrega- tional Church, on Wednesday afternoon to witness the marriage of Miss 'Eva Cheetham Wallington, daughter of Mr. Edward Wallington, Walton Bank,Lower Walton to Mr. Walter Gray, of Edenffeld,near Bury. The bride has been the mistress of Thewlis-street infant school for several years and her amiable disposition has won for her a wide circle of friends. During the arrival of the wedding party, Several appropriate voluntaries were played on the organ by Mr. W. H. Payton, Mug BC Promptly at one o'clock, the bride entered the church,' and proceeded down the central aisle leaning on the arm of her father. She looked handsome in a dress of ivory enaline, trimmed with chiffon and silk lace, and white felt hat. She carried a choice bouquet composed of white roses, lily of the valley and white heather. Two bridesmaids were in attendance, namely, Miss Florence Wallington and Miss lAgnes M. Wallington (sisters of the bride). They wore pretty dresses of pale blue enaline trimmed with crêpe do chine and silk lace, and black silk beaver hats. Each carried a shower bouquet of pink roses and lily of the valley. The Rev. John Yonge, pastor, was the officiat- ing minister. The bride was given away by her father. Mr. Henry Gray, brother of the bridegroom, satisfac- torily discharged the duties of best man. The congregation joined in singing a marriage hymn, and at the conclusion of the ceremony Mr. W. H. Payton,Mus. Bac., played Mendelssohn's well-known "wedding March.' A reception was afterwards held at Walton Bank, the residence of the bride's father. A party of 40 guests sat down to luncheon in a marque erected in a field adjoining the grounds. Mr. John Atkinson was in charge of the catering. The happy couple left for their new home at Haslingden by the 6.15 p.m. train. The bride's travelling dress was a Scotch tweed costume and black velvet hat. A large number of useful and valuable presents were received, including one from the teachers and scholars of Thewlis-street school.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>Ainsworth—Pigot.</head>
        <p>Ainsworth—Pigot.</p>
        <p>The picturesque Lunesdale village of Whittington ou the borders of North Lancashire and Westmoreland was the scene of a pretty wedding the bride being the grand-daughter of the aged rector. the Rev. E. Pigot, M.A.—Miss May Pigot, and the bridegroom, Mr. George Garnett Ainsworth, of Norton, Cheshire, The bride, Miss Gertrude Frances May Pigot ("May') was the daughter of the late Mr. Arthur Pigot, of Grappenhall, and of Mrs. Arthur Pigot, of Norton Cottage, Halton, Cheshire. She entered the church leaning upon the arm of her uncle, the Rev. H. V• Pigot, M.A., vicar of Stockton Heath. She was attended by Masters North and Edward North,as pages, and by four bridesmaids, Miss Marjorie E. Pigot (sister of the bride), Miss Lilian Ainsworth (sister of the bridegroom), and Misses Eleanor and Gertrude Pigot (cousins of the bride). The bride was prettily attired in a gown of ivory white satin draped with old lace belonging to her mother, with panel of pearl embroidery w r ied by Miss North, the bodice being also trimmed ',vith the same embroidery and tucked chiffon. She wore a pearl and emerald pendant, the gift of the bridegroom, and carried a bouquet of white roses, marguerites and lilies. The bridesmaids wore pretty dresses of white mousseline de soie, tucked and gauged, trimmed with valenciennes lace and insertion; they had white felt hats trimmed with velvet and soft satin ribbon • and each wore pearl and emerald pendauts the gifts a the bridegroom. Mr. W. Fletcher, of Grappenhall, was the best man. The service was fully choral. The newly-wedded pair subsequently hit for their honeymoon at Windermere and tne Lakes, the bride's going-away dress being of pale grey voile trimmed with lace insertion. She wore a black picture hat. ManY choice presents were received.</p>
        <p>Miller—Berry. At Hill Cliffe Chapel on Monday the marriage was solemnized of Mr. John Albert Miller, eldest sea of Mr. John Miller, Stockton Heath, and Miss Annie Berry, second daughter of Mr. Berry, of Higher Walton. The parties are well known in the district and a large congregation assembled. The bade who was- becominglY attired in a grey tweed costume,with cream voile blouse and large picture hat, trimmed with tips, carried a bouquet of white sweet peas intermingled with maiden- hair fern and smilax. She was attended by two brides- maids, Miss Berry and Miss Reed, who wore, costumes of blue voile. Mr. Charles Berry acted as best man' The Rev. J. S. Hughes, pastor, officiated. The- hymn "0 love divine and golden "was sung by the congrega- tion, Mrs. Hughes officiating at the organ and plaYilig as the bridal party retired, Mendelssohn's;"Wedding March." A reception afterwards took place in the schoolroom, where luncheon had been provided. The health of the bride and bridegroom was proposed b,Y, the Rev. J. S. Hughes, and addresses, were made by the gentlemen present, the bridegroom responding la suitable terms. There was a large number of presents' The bride and bridegroom departed for Menai Bridga where the honeymoon will be spent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.,</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.,</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith. Matter intended for publication must be written on' one side only of the paper and sent in, if possible, ric' later than Thursday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>Councillor Maginn and the "George "</head>
        <p>Councillor Maginn and the "George "</p>
        <p>Licence.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Councillor Maginn we all know adds at times little spice and humour to the Council and we are a" glad of it *hen it is kept in a proper channel, for the' debates at times are apt to become somewhat dull an dreary. He has,however,in debating the transfer of the "George" licence fallen into a remarkable error and a 5. he has mentioned it on a previous occasion and there- fore is under the impression that he is right, I will take" the trouble to correct him. He is decidedly in error when he states that Messrs Walker offered for the trans- - of the Feathers licence what was considered to bl equal to an offer of £5,000. As was clearly, as 7. unmistakably shown at the time their offer did Ile" amount in value to £l,OOO and was considered by those' best able to judge as a most ridiculous offer, and the wonder was that anyone could be found to support it Beside as a matter of fact the opposition to the trans- ferring of the "Feathers" licence achieved a gre° work on behalf of the town and thetown has benegted ever since in more ways than one. Prior to then trans" fers of licences could be obtained easily and for a as°l very much under theirvalue. Ever since then the Cs!: poration has been much wider awake in these matter' and has not only obtained higher offers for a lice nceryi but offers to surrender other licences have bee made and obtained at the same time.--Yours CANDOUR.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-09-10_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH 05</head>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH 05</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>ENDING SEPTEMBER 3rd, 1904.</p>
        <p>During the week, the following births and deathS' were registered :—Warrington ttegistration District births, 37; deaths, 31. The excess of births over deaths was 6.</p>
        <p>The death-rate from all causes was equal to 23.6 Pe,4 1,000 against 13.3 the average of the previous 4 weeks,,,an' against 19.8 the corresponding period of last year. i•L'er death rate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equa, to 12.1 per 1,000, against 6.2 the average rate of tue previous 4 weeks and against 5.4 the corresponding period of 1903. The ages of mortality were :- 0-1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-65 65—up. TOtai 14 3 1 3 5 5 31 The deaths included-1 from whooping cough, 11 from diarrhcea 2 from phthisis, 2 from cancer, 2 iv'''. bronchitis, 2 from accidents, 7 from all other causes. a The following cases of infectious disease have bee reported during tl.te week :—Scarlet fever, 25 cases' erysipelas, 2 cases, Total number of patients in hospital, week ending September 3rd, 119. 11, METEOROLOGY.—The mean temperature was 59 and therefore 5 deg. higher than the preceding We,ehe The maximum temperature 79 F., was recorded on tL'at, 28th August, and the minimum temperature 42F., on 3rd inst. The rainfall measured '55 inch. The general directi°l3 was S.W.</p>
        <p>J. GUEST GORNALL. Medical Officer of Health.</p>
        <p>Warrington.—Printed and published by Joint &amp; Co., at The Examiner General Printing "°' a ll Bewesy-street, and published by them at the esrci- place,and also at The Examine Offices,Victoria-roael, WIDNES ; 32, George-street, Sr. iiELENs; 3, 313tept. street, EARLESTOWN ; Church-street Iturscorts, -- 10th, 1904.</p>
      </div>
