        <p>WIDNES A RUNCORN BRIDGE BYE-LAWS. OTICE IS HEREBY GIVEN THAT ON WED- NESDAY the 31st day of August 1904, the follow! ng Bye-laws were duly made by the Widnes &amp; Runeortl Bridge Company with respect to the Widnes and Runcorn Bridge of the Company in pursuance of the powers con- ferred upon the Company by Section 54 of the Widnes A Runcorn Bridge Act, 1900, that is to say : BYE-LAWS MADE BY THE WIDNES&amp; RUNCORN BRIDGE COMPANY. IN PURSUANCE Or SECTION 54 OF THE WIDNES A Rum - CORN BRIDGE ACT 1900, FeR REGULATING THE CONVEYANCE or PASSENGERS, VEHICLES. ANIMALS AND GOoDS, ACROSS THE RIVE% IdERSEr AND THE MANCHEsTER SHIP CANAL, BY MRANS OF THE COMPANY'S TRANSPORTER BRIDGE. I.—Any person who offends against any of the following Bye-laws. shall be liable for each offence to a penalty not exceeding FORTY SHILLINGS. 2,--Contractors' tickets are in all cases non-transferable. 3.—A person, not being a Contractor shall not pass to or from a Car otherwise than through the turnstile, or being a Contractor,shall not so pass otherwise than through the Con- tractors' gate, or being in charge of a vehicle or horse. other • wise than through the passage provided for such traffic, and a person shall not jump onto or off a Car while it is in motion. 4.—A1l passengers shall, on termination of each trip, be landed before any other.persons shall be allowed to enter the Car, and a person shall not enter the Car before such land- ing be completed. 5.—A passenger shall not remain on the Car at the end of a trip. S.—When, in the opinion of the person in charge of any Car, the Car has a full load, no person shall enter or remain on the Car when warned by the person in charge not to do so, nor shall any goods or any vehicle be brought onto or allowed te remain on the Car after the person in charge of such goods or vehicle, as the case may be, shall have been drily warned. 7.—lt shall be lawful for any Officer of the Company to direct any person on a Car to occupy any such part thereof as such Officer may think proper, and a person shall not refuse or neglect to comply with such directions. B.—A person shall not obstruct, or loiter upon, or permit any vehicle or animal in his charge to obstruct, the passage through the turnstile or other place leading to os from the landing places or other premises of the Company. 9.—A person shall not obstruct any Officer of the Company in tbe due execution of his duty. 10.—A person shall not interfere with the safe or proper working of a Car, and shall not wilfully damage any of the ' property of the Company. 11.—A person bringing any vehicle, goods, cattle or other matter or thing onto any Car, shall comply with any order of any Officer of the Company with respect to the placing of any such things in such part of the Car as such Officer may direct. 12.—A person shall not allow any goods, cattle, merchan- dise, luggage, matter or thing belonging to him or in his charge, to remain on any part of the Company's premises' longer than is necessary for the making of the journey. 13.—Any 0 Meer of the Company shall have power to re- fuse permission for goods or articles of any description to be taken onto a Car, in any case where such goods or articles are not carried on a wheeled vehicle or other suitable con- veyance. 14.—The maximum load to be carried, without special permission, on any vehicle by any Car, shall be as follows, viz :—Where such lead is carried on two wheels. 8 tons, and where such load is carried on more than two wheels, 13 tons. 15.—A vehicle of any descriptl on weighing with or with- out its load more than 15 tons; shall not be taken onto a Car unless 24 hours previous notice, with a full description of the vehicle and its load, has been given to the Manager or the Company, and his permission in writing obtained, and then only at such time as he shall direct. 16.—A1l drivers of vehicles and carters shall lead their horses onto and off a Car, and shall not proceed with the same beyond a gentle walking pace whilst on the premises of the Company. 17.—Wheeled Vehicles, with or without horses, shall not be left 3n the premises of the Company, without a person in attendance,end such person shall scotch the vehicle of which he is in charge, to the satisfaction of the Officers of the Company, and shall have control of any horse by the bridle, whilst on the premises of the Companv. ' 18.—A pertain shall, if so required by an Officer of the Company, Recipe any dog or other animal In his charge byo a chain, leash or cord. 19.—The Driver of any Stage Coach, Omnibus, Tramear.or other Carriage, shall sec down and take up passengers at such part of the premises of the Company as may be from time to time appointed by an Officer of the Company. 20.—A Stage Coach, Omnibus, Tramcar, or other carriage shall not, without permission of the Company, enter the premises of the Company for the purpose of standing or plying for passengers or for hire, and shall stand at such part thereof as may be from time to time appropriated by the Company for the purpose. 21.-9, person shall not remove, or get over or under, any bar rail or barrier, or force or open any gate fitted or placed on any part of the Company's premises for the regulation of passenger or goods traffic ' 12.—A person shall not 'take or place any loaded firearms , onto or upon any part of the premises of the Company. 23.—A person shall not, without special permission of the Company in writing being first obtained, bring onto or place upon any part of the premises of the Company, any goods deemed by any Officer of the Company to be of a dangerous nature. 24.—A person shall not throw any stone or other missile from or on to any part of the Company's premises. 25.—A person shall not smoke Tobacco on any part of any Car except on such part or parts of the Car as e.e set apart for that purpose. 26.—A person shall not make or record any bet, or play any game of chance on any Car, landing place or otilsor of the premises of the Company. 27.—A person shall not be in a state of intoxication, or be guilty of any riotous, disorderly or lodecent conduct, or spit, or commit any other nuisance, or use any abusive or indecent language, or beg, or play upon any instrument, or sing, dania, Pray, preach or read aloud, or offer for sale any matter or thing whatever, on any part of the premises of the Company. 28.—Throughout these Bye-laws the corpression " The Company means the Widnes &amp; Runccrn Bridge Co., the word " Car" means any Car used for working the said Bridge, the word " Contractor " mcans a person to whom a pass ticket has been granted under Section 51 of the Widnes &amp; Runcorn Bridge Act, 1400, the word " Horse" incindes any other beast of burden or draught, the expression "Officer of the Company " includes the Manager. Secretary, the Collectors, persons in charge of Cars and police officers on duty on the premises of the Company, the singular may be construed as the plural, the plural as the singular, and the masculine as the feminine. And notice is hereby further given that it is the intention of the Company to apply to the Board of Trade on the 11th day of October next for confirmation of the above Bye-laws. Dated this 2nd day of September, 1904. F. H. STABLES, Secretary. Widnes &amp; Runcorn Bridge Company.</p>
