        <p>BILLIARDS. THE STROKES OF THE GAME. 13T Et.X.431 1...E.NT X • [COPYRIGHT.] SlDE.—continued. Running Side and Check Side. To many beginners the terms "running side" and "check side" are confusing, and even many good players, though quite aware which is the correct side to play a stroke with, are sometimes at a loss to define which is ru.nningThside and which is check side in certain strokes: more es- Pecially -,as in certain cases the side im- Parted to the cue ball may be either running side or check side according to the angle at which the cue ball strikes a cushion after contact with an object ball. Also a ball may carry check side when it strikes a first cushion and running side when it strikes a second cushion. Running side, as is well known by the merest tyro at billiards, increases the angle of reflec- tion, and check side diminishes this angle and Often destroys it altogether as shown by A and 13 strokes in Diagram 4 (given last week). DIAGRAM 5.</p>
