        <p>A GOOD FAMILY MEDICINE CHEST, W ITH a prudent use, has saved many a life, and yet we think the idea might improved upon and reduced to a more simple form. Take some Compound such as COCKLE'S ANTIBILIOUS PILLS. and we find that the desired end may be obtained without scales and weights, or little mysterious compartments, and enchanted bottles with crystal stoppers. Others might be used but COCKLE'S PILLS as tested by many thousands of persons, and found to answer their purposes so well, may be set down as the best.—Observer. eoeKLE,s FREE FROM MERCURY. ANTIBILIOUS OF PURE VEGETABLE INGREDIENTS. PILLS. For BILE LIVER ACIDITY, INDIGESTION, &amp;C. - - - THE EARLESTOWN MYSTERY. The resumed inquiry into the death of Annie Elizabeth Plumb, a middle-aged woman, a native of Staffordshire, who on Sunday week was found dead on Newton Common, took place at Earlestown Town Hall on Tuesday. Since the adjournment, the police had dis- covered deceased's brother, William Lawton, carriage cleaner, of Edleston-street, Crewe; also her daughter, Annie Elizabeth Plumb, whose evidence was to the effect that deceased had ibeen parted from her husband four years owing to intemperate habits, and had earned her livelihood by hawking. Deceased's husband resided at Edleston-street, Crewe. Drs. Patterson and Latham gave evidence to the effect that death was due to ex- posure and pneumonia, and that the slight scratch on the nose had nothing to do with death.—The jury eturned a verdict in accordance with the medical vidence.</p>
